      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The King rrnd Queen at Ilatfield.—Hatfield Hnuse, where Lord and Lady Salisbury entertained the King and Queen over the week-end, previous to their Majesties departure for. Windsor, for Ascot race-week is one of the most historically interesting of the great houses of England. Edward VI. end Princess Elizabeth were both residing there when called to the throne, and the house contains many unique relics of Tudor limes, such as the saddle used by Qusen Elizabeth, the Casket Letters of Mary Queen of ..‘:,cots, lied in the banqueting hall there is the famous portrait of Queen Elizabeth inset in the panelled walls. Here too, are the banners taken from the French by Wellington, and the armoury contains) one of the finest private collections in the country. What remains of the old royal palace is now used as stables, but the house is regurcled as one of the best specimens of Jacobean architecture. and the old quadrangle is beloved of artists. Many royalties have visited Hatfield, including, in modern times Queen Victoria, the German Emperor. the Empresa Marie of Russia, rand King Edward and the Queen have both paid previous visits there. The Ascot of Oldee Times.—Ascot Races %here the social aide of London life has beet, centred this week. are said by Turf historians to have been founded by Queen Anne nearly two hundred ye us ago. The Ascot of to-day may not be, quite so select as it is outwardly fashionable, but it compares favourably with the Ascot of a century or so ago; In tbe clsys of Geotge 111, the racing was a minor attraction to the prize-fights, wrestling-matches, and cock-fights, which took place daily on the Common. Nor was it at a French race- course, a year or two ago, that the first sensational " Directoire " costume matte its nppe +ranee, for we ,read when Queen Anne inaugurated t'-e first meeting in 1711, Miss Eorester, one of the Maids of Honour, came there on her palfrey" dressed like a man, wearing a long white riding coat, a full-flapped waistcoat, cocked hat and powdered periwig. The France-British Fete.—lf Britain and France should again be opposed in arms, there will he tens of thousands ef people in both countries who will have the consolation of knowing that they have done their best to avert such a calamity. There have been many enter- prises which should tend to promote n kindly feeling lAtween the two great peoples, and among them mist be counted the France-British charity fete at the White City, which opened on Saturday. The intention was that both French and British charities should benefit by the fete and bazaar, and many alistinguished ladies of both _nationalities lent their active co-operation. The fete was opened by H.R.H. the Duchess of Connaught, and as Sir B. Redwood remarked, a tong list of patrons and patronesses of both nations bore elociuent testimonyto the interest which had been taken in the project on both sides of the channell. In such circumstances, the fete served two excellent and beneficial objects, it pro- duced a goodly sum for the charities, and it strengthened the evidence that whatever may happen, the peoples of the two great countries desire to be friends. A Missionary Exhibition.—Church folk have not only been interested in the great ecclesiastical Pageant at Fulham Palace, but al-o in the missionary exhibition "Africa and the East," at the- Agricultural Hall in London, organised Ly the Church Nlissionary _Society. It is intended to illustrate the value and importance of Church work beyond the seas, and it is apart from its primary object, of interest to one and all, and as a mere show. In nddition to curios and trophies from 551 mission star iona, there are typical reproductions of nature life in distant lands. Very interesting is the African village, showing a typical \Veit Coast settlement and the rites, customs, and occupations of the natives Scattered about amidst tropical surroundings are the mission churches and schools, and the rude native build- ings, and a similar display of a realistic kind is giver, by the Red Indians, Persians, Japanese, and other races. The exhibition, which is the outcome of of a suggestion made at the Pan-Anglican Conference of last year, remains open daily from noon until July 3. Comnlimentaro Christian Names.—Sometimes persons and places owe their names to accidental circumstances. There is a story of.a colonial Governor- who, on being asked to name certain new townships, bestowed on them the names of his wife's pet dogs, which they enjoy to this day. Earl Carrington's danghter, who was married last week to Visconnt Bury—pronounced "Bewry "—mere: her Christian name largely to having been born at Government. House, Sydney, while her father was Governer of New South Wales. Her full name was Judith Sydney Mijee Carrington—"Mijeee being a term of endearment in the language of the Australian blacks. Lord Onslow paid a similar com- pliment to New Zealand during his term of office as Governor, and gave his son the Maori name of Hubs. Fashions and Colours at Ascot.—Fonlardhas "caught on" tremendously, and has been greatly to the fora at Ascot, the leading co'our combinations being ivory spotted with the faintest shell pink, or silver grey, and pale green spotted with white. For the very elaborate race frocks, double chiffon has been used, in tones of rose pink and pale green, while for comparatively simple creations batiste has been employed, much inlet with lace and embroidery.. With all pale hued gowns, black hats ttimmed with leaves of foliage—the " young- green " shades being those most sought after—have been worn this week at Ascot. Weird Skirts.—Curions deuhle-ehecker skirts are much in evidence, the tipper portion being gFner•illy like the bodice. and reaching, sheath-like, well below the knees, while the 'under part is of an entirely different fabric and colour and pleated or g,atheseri very fully indeed. To tho exceptionally tall and elegant figure this notion is just :possible. but to %some!) or raetely the average height, or below it, it is positively cruel. Never perhaps has there been a time when it was more desirable to carefully study our good slid had points and then follow, or defy, Dame Fashion our common sense and goo.i taste dictates. Ornarnen'al Pc,:•:kets.—T»ilors tur:u building immense eet .eciliets on eur coat-shirts, but these are care- laity and securely stitched down. and no little loop- hole permits of even a railway ticket being thrust inside. This state of affairs is scarcely to be wondered at, considering we inust, if we desire to be modish, look as though we had been pulled through a gas pipe. If- we were permitted real pockets," we might be tempted to sttaff things into them, and then of course, our figure, and the hang of the coat would be hopelessly ruined. Motor Coats and Bonnets.—ln the matter of motor coats, the race for favour between alpaca and Shantung has resulted in an easy win for the latter. As regards shape the most suitable follows the lines of the ulster, with closely fitting sleeves, and a collar that turns up tightly round the neck. ' The •favourite headgear for " serious " motoring, appears to be the 1830 bonnett, in fine straw, trimmed with a becoming little ruche of ribbon, with rosettes over each ear, and strings that tie under the chin. A long flowing veil falls at the hack, and this of course, can be drawn forward, and made use of when desired. The Motor Veil.—We are shewing a greater affection than ever for the motor veil this summer. It accom- panies us on our walks, and when travelling by train or the humble omnibus, and is even appearing upon the river. Now, it may be forgiven in the train or the humble omnibus, or even when trudging along country roads, but not upon the river. A veil under these circumstances is as much out of place as a ball gown would be at at the breakfast table. No veil, no make-up," should be the rule of the river girl or woman, the latter invariably looking its very worst awder "the fresh gladness of Heaven."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>BLIND MAN FINED FOR HIS GUIDE</head>
        <p>BLIND MAN FINED FOR HIS GUIDE</p>
        <p>At liunc—oin, on Monday, Joslma Eden, a blind hawker, was summoned for keeping a dog without a licence. The defendant contended that he was exempt. He did not need the dog in the town, but he used it expressly to guide him in the country.—The Chairman (Mr. Littler) said he had often seen defendsnt, but never saw him with a dog. There were, however, mitigating circumstances, and be would be fined only 2s. Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>MAN C ri ESTE R</head>
