        <p>for the "goose to lay the golden eggs " you can expect a golden return from one of the most profitable adjuncts of poultry farming. Next week, my subject will be „" Turkeys."</p>
        <p>. . grsic•—i AGRICULTURAL EDUCATION IN CHESIi</p>
        <p>New Departure in Training of Teachers'</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Cheshire Education C 055 at Crewe on Monday, Dr. liodeson, the chairraso, jielf the Higher Education Committee proposed Ph; departure with regard to the training of teac'' work in rural areas, especially in evening classes. obe., appointment of an external agricultural science te,l Owe on the Agricultural College soil at Holmes Che'dello suggested, whose duties should be the superintevois of about thirty rural technical evening sc"pef,rl the county, tbe subjects Leine eeuaral iorLi I,:ental science, agricultural science, an" 011'1 economy and gardening. A summer course otie'll enable the student, teachers to rake part in Pg,,, 013`, work such as nrill..ng -and butter making, f. general management oi poultry and cattle, ha"ciealc'h-, the corn crops, summer pruning, and other gil,nr grits, operations, land surveying and levelling, fgrTcht metic, bookkeeping &amp;c. Women teachers mirchara admitted to the Agricultural College at Holmes 0 seo r but for the Summer course it would be desirah.le them to the County Dairy Institute at \Voiles.' instructiop in dairying, poultry keeping, coelc,glAe, housewifery, making hygiene the underlyiug. selfno. r, which the practical work would be the appirceAti‘A was estimated the cost would be al.out 1:75."1,,cav The matter was referred to the Agricultural IV."' (I te • Committee. „noir3-10 Mr. R. G. James, of Birkenhead, was alliiref assistant instructor in horticulture for Ores or give instruction in gardening to boys in eleil'e schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>MILITARY TRAINING ON SUNDAY.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRAINING ON SUNDAY.</p>
        <p>• The follovving letter has br en forwarded to the Prime Minister by the Committee of the Lord's Day Observance Society : The Lord's Day Observance Society, 18 Buckingham Street, Strand, W.C. To the Right 'Honourable H. H. Asquith, K.C., First Lord of the Treasury. Sir,—l am instructed by my Committer-, ti col; your attention to the persistent cr!,r)t .lirg made to utilize Sunoay as a t:,.•y ior military exercises at present for the training of the Territorial Army; which, if permitted, will soon give rise to demands for the co-operation of the Regular forces. I am to urge that the Ministry of which you are the head should use its authority to discourage so mischievous a movement, end should recocnise that the day is already earmarked by its Sinker for higher and better purposes. It is not the first time in English history that similar attempts have been made. Indeed, the fact that Royal and Ecclesiastical authorities favoured such misuse of the day three centuries ago is put forward as a sufficient justification for cuoueril pro- posals of the kind. But this shallow plea wilfully ignores the deter- mined opposition which was aroused throughout the country, and the disastrous results to Church and State ot disregard of such remonstrances. The responsible leaders in both departments of the National life were swept away by the f•torrn which they had wantonly provoked, trod their Sunday. trained warriors could not save them. The Puritan spirit which recoiled from Sunday drill, and favoured worthier methods of employing the Lord's Day, secured better discipline, and a more effective discharge of military duty. than the irreligious and self-indulgent spirit which scoffed at such scruples. History does not necessarily repeat itself on precisely the same lines. Foreign invasion may conceively replace civil war as a test .of national character and military preparedness. But,assuredly, the experience of • three hundred years since does not favour a return to methods which then failed so conspicuously. My Committee trust, therefore, that such return will be authoritatively checked. I am, Sir, Your obedient servant, FREDERIC PEAKE, ILA., LLD., Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
