      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>F</p>
        <p>THE ENGLISH BOGEY !N GERMANY.</p>
        <p>HOW THE TEUTON SEES HIMSELF</p>
        <p>[By C.E.P.]</p>
        <p>The Berlin correspondent of the "Liverpool Daily Post" sends an interesting article on the above subject. He tells us that the German, if somewhat flattered, is always puzzled by the tribute paid to his country by the leaders of the Dreadnought, and other alarmist agitations in England. He sees in the English Press hints at a supernatural efficiency, resolution and patriotism, which he never knew he possessed, antis inclined perhaps to wish that he were only half as terrible as he is painted. He points out that in Germany England is being held up as the great model, and that she is borrowing English ideas in every sphere of life. The idea is almost being taken for granted in this country that Germany is marching to com- mercial and industrial success at our expense and for this idea the English Press is largely responsible. On the other hand the German Press is filled with the gloomiest reports from all branches of industry. The industrial crisis which began in 1907 appears to have been more severe than that experienced in this country. The textile industry is one in which Germany is usually painted as driving Great Britain from the world's markets. A report was published a few days ago in the Berlin " Kaufmannschaft" which affirms that the export trade in textiles is in a hopeless condition, that, despite , Germany's retaliative measures, foreign tariffs are shutting out a once flourish- ing German trade, and that it is England which is the successful rival in many domains. "English competitors are driving us from the linen markets." A dozen of the chief texile export trades are men- tioned; and it is declared in every case that Germany's share is falling off relatively. Turning to naval affairs there is constant criticism of the Marine Department, and con- trasts unfavourably to Germany are daily being made. The "Berliner Tageblatt " lately pointed out that in German yards there was an official for every seven men who worked, whereas in happy, unbureau- cratic England the proportion is one to thirty. A further point of criticism and comparison is directed towards the different systems of Government in the two countries and the almost impossible task which Prince Billow has to face to raise the £25,000,000 necessary to meet current expenses. Even in Education a point in which it is here claimed, and with a good deal of justification, that Germany is ahead, education is said to be ruined by clericalism, red-tape officialism, and the re-actionary scientific spirit. The corres- pondent finishes his article by saying "Everywhere are to be found Germans ready to affirm that in each department of national life their country is being beaten from the field by better-equipped nations, in which Great Britain is often put first." There is no doubt that in all this there are elements of exaggeration as well as of truth. Germany is on the whole a progressive nation, and in some departments she has given us the lead, but in others, on the authority of many thoughtful Germans, this country is far ahead. Each country has a good deal to learn from the other. Many Germans are begin- ning to doubt the wisdom of their Protective system. Considering their system of pre- , ferqptial railkvay rates, bounties, selling under cost to outsiders to maintain home ! prices, &amp;c., &amp;c., they do not see why their , export trade should have such a serious set , back since 1907, and they are asking the ! question does it pay the country as a whole : to give so much advantage to foreign nations , for the sake of maintaining such a high level • of prices at home?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>WHAT 1 BLESSING.</head>
        <p>WHAT 1 BLESSING.</p>
        <p>What a blessing it is. Sought after by thousands. Many a miserauleernan and woman is happy now. Nights of unrest, days of trouble; Any irritating skin disease means this. Eczema means it. Piles, too. Just as had and just as hard to cure. But Doan's ointment does it. Relieves at once and cures quickly. A !pacific for irritation of the skin. A blessing to the suffering public. Here is a Wigan proof to back it : Seven years after his cure by Donns' Ointment, Mr, W. Robinson, 47, Ince Green-lane, Wigan, said :—"With the exception of one out,break six months after giving my testimony (when I again used Doan's Ointment with equal success), I have remained free from the piles ever since a matter of seven years.l always recommend Doan's Ointment." -</p>
        <p>Mr. Robinson's original statement is as follows : "For two years I suffered very badly with piles ' • the itching and irritation were dreadful, and at times almost unbearable. "Reading that Doan's Ointment was a reliable remedy for such trouble as mine, I sent for some. I was glad to find that a few applications gave me groat relief, and was encouraged to continue the treatment. From that time I made good headway, and was soon quite cured. "I shall take pleasure in recommending Doan's Oint- ment to all I know to be suffering as I did. (Signed) W. Robinson." - - - - - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>Piles. eczema, and other skin complaints, are torment- ing and distressing ; they spoil the sleep, make you nervous and irritable. Doan's Ointment allays, the irritation and inflammation of itching skin troubles, eczema, piles, shins, pimples, chilblains, etc. The first application gives relief, and a lasting cure follows. Doan's Ointment is two shillings and ninepence a pot (six pots thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, on receipt of price, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., S. Wells-street, Orford-street, London, W. Be sure you get exactly the same kind of ointment that Mr. Robinson I ad.</p>
        <p>1. ! Supposing it to be true that British firms suffer more than foreigners from the causes thus stated, still tartff taxation is no remedy. These complaints may be summed up in the statement that British firms are over-taxed by their local governors, by their railways and by Shipping, and by their workmen's trade unions. If the British firms are unfairly over-taxed already, it will merely aggravate their condition if Parliament sets about imposing still further taxation all round— which is the true meaning of Tariff Reform. 2. . But, in fact, the Birmingham Tariff Commission had decisive evidence from many engineering employers that the output of the average British worker exceeds that of his Continental competitor by 20 to 50 per cent.—and more in some instances. His higher pay and shorter hours are, as a 'lite, the natural result of his higher efficiency, and also of the better economic conditions of the country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>ENGINEERING INDUSTRIES AND FREE TRADE. NOTES ON THE REPORT OF THE BIRMINGHAM TARIFF COMMISSION.</head>
        <p>THE ENGINEERING INDUS- TRIES AND FREE TRADE. NOTES ON THE REPORT OF THE BIRMINGHAM TARIFF COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>Amongst the sAveral "reports" issued by the Birmingham Tariff Commission, the most highly Protectionist of all is that upon. the British engineer- ing industries. The majority of the witnesses who gave evidence before the Commission demanded Pro- tection up to 20, 30, 40, and even 50 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>RAMPANT PROTECTIONISM</head>
        <p>RAMPANT PROTECTIONISM</p>
        <p>No such rampant Protectionism had been eyelid suggested in this country until the issue of the Com- mission's Engineering Report. Contemptuously. and without mention, the bulk of the engineering firms threw Overboard the original. 10 to 15 per cent. maximum tariff proposals of Mr. Joseph Chamberlain and of the Tariff Reform League. They want • 10 to 15 per cent, upon semi-raw material ;'and higher rates proportionately upon all fully-manufactured (roods. Thus Steel plates, ingots, castings, forgings, angles, bars, &amp;c., 5 to 15 per cent. ; and in one case 40 per cent. Agriculturalmachinery 10 to 30 per cent. ; with, in some cases, to £2 extra upon each machine. • Motor cars, cycles, and parts, 25. to 50 per cent. • These are mere samples. The long Tariff lists, however, include every imported commodity in the nature of semi-raw material, or machine, or implement, or. tool, from a motor-car down to a cast-iron pipe or an American centre-bit or bradawl. The proposed duties may be roughly averaged at 20 per cant. all round. .</p>
        <p>As regards the burdens thus to be imposed on the home consumers of these immensely varied products, the Birmingham Commission asked no questions, and the Tariff Reform engineers gave no replies. WHAT iS THEPRETEXT ?</p>
        <p>What is the justification or excuse for there revolu- tionary proposals, in a country which has been the inventive birthplace of most of the great machines of civilisation, and which has hitherto well held its own, free and unprotected, in the face of the world engineering competition? . The engineering Tariff Reformers admit that British engineers do not do very badly. Thus exports of machinery from the United Kingdom grew almost continuously from £8,000,000 in the late eighties to nearly £32,000,000 in 1907. In the last-named year our imports of foreign machinery (including all the special inventions of all civilised countries) were little over £5,000,000; being only one-sixth part of our exports. Therefore our surplus of engineering exports has grown far taster than our population. Also, our shipbuilding increased 50 per cent in the "last ten years. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Tariff Reformers, per contra, point out that though our engineering output has grown faster than our population, it has not grown as fast (proportionately) as the engAneering output of the six principal ciyilised countries. Fifteen years ago we exported £5.000,000' worth of engineering work More than did those six countries put together. Now we export £17,000,000 less than they. Also, to India and the British Colonies we formerly sent' ten times as much as all other. countries put together. Now we send to India and the Colonies £7,500,000 worth per annum, against, Y-6,000,000 sent by all other countries. Our exPi!nffs to India and the Colonies have increased, but the exports of the competing foreigners collectively have increased faster. • THE FREE TRADE RETORT. The answer is in three short points :</p>
        <p>I. We British 'people are 4.4,000,000 persons on 120,000 square ,miles of land. Our civilised rivals are 200,000,000 of population 'on many million square miles. It is not sane and sober patriotism, still less is it sane and sober business, to assert that we ought to be afraid or ashamed of ourselves because we do not continue always to surpass the whole civilised world's total in every kind of manufactured. product. We were the great manufacturing pioneers ; we ,now keep very well ahead in a fair share of the world's industries. These two facts are reasonably sufficient for reasonable national ambition and dignity. Politicians and traders who Set up on our behalf the astonishing claim to entire world dominance must not be surprised if they stand suspect of seeking their own sectional an personal aggrandisement rather than that of the nation. _</p>
        <p>_ 2. Even if we should seek to dominate, all the world's chief industries, Tariff, Reform is the diametrically wrong direction in which to make the attempt. The fundamental principle of Tariff Reform is that whenever foreigners, by natural advantages, can sell to us a product more cheaply than we.our- selves can make it, we should exclude that proiuct, entirely or partially. This, of course, would make our own productions more costly, and would pro- portionately injure us in the international competition. Tariff Reform in the engineering industries, taken alone, means that out of some 400 sections of manu- facturing industry each one is to be protected by tariffs, which, in every case, amount to taxing the other 399 sections for the benefit of that one. The experiment has been tried in every. Protectionist foreign country; and the universal complaint of theSe foreign producers is that they cannot conmiete against British cheapnesS because (amongst other reasons) they have .to pay 25 to 40 per cent. more for their taxed factory and workshop equipments. 3. Where the foreign competing industries have</p>
        <p>grown faster than our proportionately, in recent years, the- obviouS explanation, in most cases, is that these foreign industries are younger than ours, whilst in the cases of some new trades, such as the motor-car and electrical machinery trades, for reasons which are well known, several foreign countries got A good start of us. -An infant may grow three times as fast as a youth; but no sane and sober expert could, therefore, prophesy that the child would always continue to gym at that same rate—or until he was eighteen feet high.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>" COMPENSATORY 7 TARIFFS.</head>
        <p>" COMPENSATORY 7 TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>Many firms in the engineering industry, however, repudiate Protectionist ideas. But, on the other hand, they affirm that British engineering firms suffer dis- advantages from which engineers in foreign countries are free; and they advocate " moderate ' tariff duties as "compensation " for these disadvantages. These alleged British disadvantages, as compared with foreigners, are chiefly : 1. Heavier British taxation, local and municipal.</p>
        <p>2. Bigher British charges for railway carriage and shipping frieghts. 3. Stronger British trade unions, -which enforce higher wages, . shorter hours,and other corditions of working, ramie costly than in foreign countries. 4. British municipalities also enforce upon British firms trade union conditions, which foreign firms, it is said, can easily evade.</p>
        <p>THE FREE TRADE RESPONSE</p>
        <p>To these demands for " compensatory " tariff duties Free Traders answer:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARIAING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARIAING.</p>
        <p>Lty redcap.]</p>
        <p>THE GOOK</p>
        <p>There are two classes of people whom I Write this article for---the farmer and the cott9ger. I am quite confident that if carried out on the right lines the rearing and feeding of geese forlhe market pays well. lam quite sure also- that none of my readers will expect me to recommend the breeding of these birds in back-yard and suburban runs. Ido not say that you need not feed one or two for Christmas, because this would be a very simple matter, as much extra room would not be required to fatten a couple of raw goslings for one's own private use. Apart from the actual btisiness side of rearing geese for the market the goose is of great industrial value to the FARMER.</p>
        <p>especially. Close observation an 4 study has proved that a flock of geese on old grass land have in a short time vastly improved its value as a grazing crop. Let me quote you from the Journal of Hof ticulture, 1862, page 227. Mr. J. R. Pearson, Chilwell, Notts. writes : " Some years since a home field of rich</p>
        <p>pasture had a flock T-of geese turned into it for the first time. I soon afterwards remarked that a space increasing in size around the stable-yard was divested of buttercups ; and from its rich appearance pre- sented. a striking contrast to the rest of the field ; it struck vie. immediately that a goose common was always green. Watching the *geese, I saw them pull up the buttercups and eat the roots, leaving the stalks on the grass.. Having mentioned this to. many, I have never met with one who knew_ it before, though gome have said they. always thought geese healthy things among stock. The destruction of so acrid a plant as the ranunculus acris must be desir- able for a cow pasture, as it takes the room of good grasses, in addition to being itself injurious." Mr. Pearson is not alone in having noticed the improved value of pasture land as a consequence of geese having run on it. Others have testified in a similar way and surely this must appeal to the farmer, because a goose can do what it is practically impos- sible to do by hand labour in the direction, of ridding the pasture land of undesirable growths. I have myself noticed many times how green the grass grows round a pond constantly inhibited by geeee. Whilst I would recommend that flocks of geese should be so run on old pasture land. .1. am at the same time mindful of the difficulty (especially with the Cheshire _ _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>farmers and also in the- Midlands) of keeping the stock guarded against foxes. However this difficulty could be got over at a very small cost and a little trouble. I would advise you to get some wire netting, say about four or five feet high and nail it to sharpened stakes. These could be readily removed from place to place. And I am sure that it would repay you 'many times over, not only in the return from your stock of geese, but in the improved condition of your land, which would in consequence be ridded of undesirable herbage and be much benefited from a course of natural</p>
        <p>very</p>
        <p>fertilisation. It is not necessary to go to much expense in supplying your stock with housing accom- modation. In some parts of the country the geese are allowed to just sleep out in the open air and are quite content. Of course it is natural. But in face of the difficulties already mentioned I should recom- mend you to erect a cheap house about 4 feet high, otlier_ dimensions according to the number</p>
        <p>kept In one flock; the house need have no bottom in it, as the constant moving about from one place to another will suffice to keep the birds clean. If the English farmer only knew of the real value of the goose I am sure more would be bred, and knowing as I do the accommodation as his disposal I would urgently recommend a more extensive raising of geese for domestic use. The</p>
        <p>COTTAGER</p>
        <p>in country places has also as a rule many facilities for rearing geese, especially those who live in the bye- ways wnere a pond is handy. It is often the case that a wide stretch of grass grows profusely on the road side of many of our country lanes and on this goslings would feed and grow in abundance. I feel sure that it is generally a case of ignorance and I say this respectfully) in the details a management that keeps many of our rural dwellers from adding to their income by rearing and feeding geese for the market. I think it must be the manner in which they " gobble " up their food that gives a wrong impression. It is not an uncommon saying among the Cheshire people that " it takes as much to feed a goose as would feed a pig." Certainly‘if you gave to geese all that they would eat in the shape of good food it would be found expensive ; but this is unnecessary and I will give you a cheap method of</p>
        <p>FEEDING,</p>
        <p>In a few week's time there will be a glut of small potatoes,•and while these are not the most satisfying of foods, if mixed with a few handsful of meal will be. found sufficient`to keep the goslings growing. Having shown you the necessity for green food in abundance, you will see that together with such food as•meal and potatoes, it must cost very little per head to keep them on the move. It is, however, essential that the goslings should have a special diet for the first week or two after hatching out, and the following can be recommended : Oatmeal and Middlings mixed with skim milk, or a good brand of Biscuit Meal will be found a very convenient food. To this could be added a quantity of pin-headed oatmeal with profit. After having given the goslings a good start by this method of feeding, you will experience very little difficulty afterwards. In the autumn the farmer has special advantages in running a flock of geese on the stubble fields after gathering the corn, and it is surprising how quickly geese are fed up in this manner. After such a method of feeding very little is required to finish them off for market; which should be done by feeding on ground oats, Barley-meal and Thirds mixed with meal. There are numerous</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>VARIETIES</head>
        <p>VARIETIES</p>
        <p>of geese, but I should only recommend two, namely. Toulouse Geese and White Embden as the best for domestic use. The Toulouse, as the name implies, is of French' origin, but by some it is contended that the Toulouse originated in Spain. Whatever its origin may be it is sufficient for us to know that it is a grand utility variety and wherever it may have originated from it has, like the other things, imported into England been improved. In colour it is white and grey. with black markings, and a good strain of •Toulouse make an admirable addition to the stock of the farm-yard. It is difficult to decide as to the superior merits between this variety and the White Embden. There is so little difference that you con scarcely go wrong whichever your choice may be. Both the Toulouse and the Embden are quick growers good layers and carry an abundance of flesh of a delicate flavourd would not recommend crossing but rather keep them pure and vigorous by the introduction new blood. It is not expected that one could say all in con- nection with the goose in a column, but sufficient has been said to show that this much neglected branch of farming in general can .be made to pay. The question of dressing for market would at least take half a column to go into details but I am- so struck with the splendid way in which geese are dressed and presented to customers in our local markets that J feel sure to anyone who is yet a novice a few hints would be best derived by a visit to an experienced hand. lam delighted to be informed that my article on ducks a few weeks backs has sufficiently encouraged one of my readers that he has make a start. And so I would urge ` all who have the accommodation at their • disposal to give a place to the goose, and if bred on the right lines. lam confident that your interest and knowledge and profit will be considerably inereased. Especially would I appeal to the farmer,who has such grand opportunities. And while you can never hope</p>
      </div>
