      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL NELSON LODGE OF ODDFELT.OWS V. PARISH CHURCH YOUNG MEN'S CLUB.—The Admiral Nelson Lodge of Oddrellows were entertained by the Parish Church Young Men's Club to a gvr.e of bowls on the green at the Rectory on Saturday last, with the following lesults</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>ADMIRAL NELSON LOD GE. YOUNG MEN'S C L ÜB.</head>
        <p>ADMIRAL NELSON LOD GE. YOUNG MEN'S C L ÜB.</p>
        <p>' S. D. S. D. W. Bibby ii 115 C. G3rner l3l W. Holhrook A. Perry - H. James J. Perry E. A. tin.nders - R. Antwis l3l , D. Percival _ _S_ _ ts ) '</p>
        <p>, A. lAwton l3 tn. J. Hill - J. Hankinson ..... .... 61 J. Yates Walter Dennett J. Burrows</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>116 71 62 .16 NOTES ON GAMES, ETC.</head>
        <p>116 71 62 .16 NOTES ON GAMES, ETC.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington were in grand • form on Saturday and placed a brilliant victory to their credit. The engage- ment was a " friendly " with Werneth, and the attendance on the Arpley enclosure was about the largest seen this season. Werneth.had first turn at the wicket and were all tumbled out for 51. The batsmen had the utmost difficulty in dealing with the deliveries 'of the Warrington bowlers, G. M. Swailes, who scored 14, being the only one to reach double figures. The Rev. Horace Gray, who made a welcome re-appearance in the local eleven, bowled with deadly effect, capturing seven wickets for 13 runs. • .</p>
        <p>Warrington had an easy task before them but they left nothing to chance and their innings had realized 198 for seven wickets when the match was brought to a close. The feature of the afternoon was the dashing display of T.Wright who make 69 in faultless style. He scored all round the wicket and his driving was marked with splendid execution and power. He is to be congratulated upon a really notable perfotm- ance. J. Callon also did yeoman service, placing 31 to his credit, and hitting five 4's. JPW. Bainbridge carried out his bat after making 23.</p>
        <p>Warrington second also gave a good 'account of themselves, defeating Werneth on their own ground in decisive fashion. Wetneth were dismissed for 98, the bowling being shared by Waywell who took three wickets for 49 runs, D. Plinston five for 39, and J. Thompson, one for one. Warrington made no mistake about their task, and the issue of the game was soon beyond doubt. D. Plinston (35 not out) was the most successful batsman, and other leading contributors to the total of 178 were J. Waywell.(2B), R H.Lowe (26) and T. Houghton (24). J. Dunkerley bowled ivell for the home side, accounting for eight wickets.</p>
        <p>Warrington occupy the 'second position in the Test Lancashire League table, Prescot holding the lead. There were two" matches in each Division on Saturday. Prescot defeated Earlestown to the tune of 164 to 121, and St. Helens accounted for St. Helens Rees in ridiculously easy fashion, the rezpec- tive totals being 205 for 7 and 62. In the second division, Prescot triumphed over Earlestown by 267 to 85, and St. Helens Rees administered defeat to St. Helens to the extent of 37.</p>
        <p>The following are the League tables up-to-date : FlitST DI VISION. Club. P. W. L. D. Pts. Prescot Warrington St. Helens Recs. E:trlestown St. Helens 4 1 ... 2 ... 1 ... 3 *Newton Two points deducted for playing ineligible player.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>SECOND DIVISION.</head>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Rees --- 1 0 Preseot Warrington Earlestovrn _ 4 ... 2 ... 2 ... 0 ... 4 St. Helens Newton 4 ... 0 ... 3 ... 1 ... 1</p>
        <p>St. James's are doing their utmost. to retain the championship of the Warrington and District League, and still bead the table, with Sankey Wire Mills 2nd one point behind. There was a battle royal between these elevens on Saturday and a rare sport- ing finish which delighted the spectators. St. James's ran out winners by 3 runs, their top scorer being J. Constable who knocked up 34 in grand style. C. Holt was also in his best form and compiled 32. The feature of the Wire Mills innings .was a splendid dis- play by H. Hawthorn which realized 55.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery and Ryhinds Bros. tried con- clusions, the wiremen running out winners by 14 runs. Newton batted well for his 21. Carter took six wickets for six rims, Jewkes four for 14, and McGowan six for IS.</p>
        <p>There was a keen finish Of the match between Walker's Brewery and Latchford Baptists. Having dismissed their opponents for 24, the Brewery eleven Were just able to pass that total before time arrived, with 6 wickets in hand. Rylance took, eight wickets for 10 runs, and E. Allen two for 14. For the Baptists Conway captured three for eight, and Fletcher one for three. -</p>
        <p>Che.bire Lines inflicted a severe trouncing upon the " Guardian " Office, the majority being 77. Duckworth did splendid service with the bat, and Made the fine score of 42.</p>
        <p>The Junior League match between Orford and All Saints' was notable fir low scoring, not one player reaching double figures. Orford gained the N ietory by 4 runs. Jolley took six wickets for nine runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington Municipal Officers' Guild continue their victorious career. On Saturday they entertained Marbury and defeated them in no half-hearted way, Batting first, the home team declared their innings after compiling 04 for 9 wickets and then dismissed .the Marbury men for 18. For the. victors, B. Price 123), M. Nicholl.s, chief constable (21), and A. E. Hands (19) batted well for their respective scores, while B. Green captured six wickets for seven runs and S. Price four for 10.</p>
        <p>Sankey had not much difficulty in disposing of Howley, the top scorer being S. Bingham with 17.</p>
        <p>'glum met their local rivals, Oughtrington Park, and gained the victory by the narrow margin of four. W. H. Podmore did good service for the winners, his total belng 33, while N. Wood knocked up 21 for the Park eleven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>. . 'Warrington Cemetery, last year's runners-up in fhe Lancashire and Cheshire Club championship, are going strong to keep the Crosfield Cup in the Warring- ton district for another year, and in their match against Eccles-, at Wigan, they scored the maximum points-25:.? against 130. For Warrington flip best wins were those of G. Ball, sen., and G: Ball, jun. (father and son), each winning his game 21-6. The losers made n very poor show indeed, which was rather unexpected after their previous displays. The eight teams left in the competition are Leigh 2nd, Kersal, Warrington Ceinetery, Chorlton 8.G.. Leigh Railway, Bowdon, Roebuck. and Newton-le-Willows. Whichever are drawn together an interesting game should be seen.</p>
        <p>In the second team competition, there was an exceptionally keen struggle between Warrington Cemetery and Wigan, resulting in a victory for the Warringtonians by two points.</p>
        <p>The team to represent Lancashire and Cheshire aglinst Yorkshire at Halifax on Jane :26th, is undoubtedly a very strong one and includes a number of local names. There is one player at least Making his debut for the county, viz., John Brooks, of Cadis- head, and he has fully earned the distinction of figuring in this match by his consistent play.</p>
        <p>Of the four matches pltyed on Saturday in connec- tion with the Works Bowling Competition, that between Cheshire Lines (5) and Crosfield's No. ,2 (15) was the most interesting, the former only winning by 6 points.</p>
        <p>Both captains had to yield to their opponents. In the first place the Cheshire Lines captain lost by 2 points to W; Lawt-m, sen. The play at the commence- ment was a little tedious, for Lawton had. giined 10 to his contestant's modest 3. However, enthusiasm was roused when Higham increased his score to 9, whilst his opponent remained at 10. The game finished very keenly, Lawton carrying off the honours.</p>
        <p>From the game between Willcock (captain of the losing team) and Timperley much pleasure was derived. Timperly, had to his credit 8., whilst his contestant obtained only 4. Timperley kept in form and led all through the game, but his opponent made a plucky effort, in the end only losing by 4.</p>
        <p>Ryland's Square lfil/ (25), and Corporation (5) were opposed; the latter,thanks to the remarkably good form of their players, winning by 22 points.</p>
        <p>Some good games were witnessed, ' that between Randles (Corporation), and Dutton (Ryland's) being of the keenest description. Randles with a total of 8, had an advantage of 3 over his opponent, and from that stage the figures went steadily up, becoming more equal every wood or so, and peeling at 12. The match became very exciting for the players were on level terms at 13 and 14, but the victory was claimed by the Ryland's representative. Another game well worth watching was played by T. Broadhurst (Rylands') and W.Robinson, the former winning by 1.</p>
        <p>Armitage and Rigby and Rylands' Engineer -pro- vided a fairly interesting match. Armitage and Rigby started with a handicap of 10 to their contestants 15, and eventually won by 27 points—a creditable achievement. Rosterne for Armitage and Rigby, met T. Smith and gained 5 before his Opponent obtained a single. The figures steadily increased and they peeled at 7 and. Rosterne again got a lead of 3, when-his contestant was 10. At 14 they were equal, but Smith won the day.</p>
        <p>That the match between Fletcher's No. 3 (30) and Monks, Hall No. 1 (5) was a one-sided affair goes without .saying, when it is seen that with such an advautage as 25 points Fletchers' lost by 50.</p>
        <p>The representatives of Monks Hall (lid exceedingly well, all except two paining the required number. The best game was between W. Thomason (Monks') and J. Massey. Five were to Thomason's credit, whilst Massey only registered one, but Massey shortly peeled with him at live. Massey reached 7 before Thomason could add another one, and then Massey increased his score to 9, without his opponent having added another. There was somewhat of a surprise when they were equal at 9. They again peeled at 12 and Massey added another two, but his opponent claimed the victory by one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>IN NEW ZEALAND.</head>
        <p>IN NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>Another Letter from Mr. F. Stott.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the election of Councillors and Mayor took place in Wellington and other cities in New Zealand. The election of Coun- cillors takes place every two years all the members retiring together. The Wellington Council is composed of fifteen members, in addition to the Mayor. There were thirty- three candidates for the fifteen vacancies. The election of Mayor takes place annually, and instead of being elected from, or by the Council, he is elected by the direct vote of the citizens. Every alternate yeaithe election of Mayor takes place contemporaneously with the election of Councillors. The interest in the Mayoral election was much greater than in the election of the Council, and I am informed that this was no new feature. The Wellington Labour Representative Com- mittee put forward eight candidates for the Council election and one for Mayor. Two of the candidates, who were members of the old Council, were re.-elected, the six others being near the bottom of the list. The candidate for the Mayoralty received scant support from the electors, polling less than 1,000 votes. Party politics are, apart from the Labour• candidatures, almost entirely absent in Municipal elections here. One regrettable feature, common in many English , boroughs, exists here. It is the fact that a large proportion of the electorate abstained from voting. There are 29,000 electors on the roll, but only some 13,000 registered their votes.</p>
        <p>PREFERENCE FOR TRADE UNIONISTS. Just now the employers of New Zealand are "up in arms" against the preference clauses which make it illegal for any employer to engage a non-unionist whilst there are unionists signing the unemployed registers of their union. " Preference " is not statutory, but appjies only to certain trades who have been fortunate in securing a preference clause in an a*ard under the Arbitration Acts. A number of employers have, quite recently, been heavily fined for breaches of the awards. As a result of these fines, the various employers' associations in the Dominion are agitating, both by resolu- tion and petition, for the preference clauses to be expunged from the awards. A non- unionist has no locus standi under the Con- ciliation and Arbitration Acts. HOW SOCIALISM COMES.</p>
        <p>How often do we hear the question "How will you begin Socialism ?." put to Socialist ad.vocates, An incident has recently taken place in New Zealand which is a typical example of how Socialism: comes: In order to complete the Main Trunk Railway between Wellington and Auckla-nd, it was important that a length of railway, some 80 Mlles in extent and belonging to a private coiupa;ny, should be purchased by the Government. Several loint meetings were held between the directors of the Company and repre- sentatives of the Government. The terifis of purchase were provisionally agreed upon and subsequently ratified by the shareholders and by Parliament. On a certain date the line became National property-. Persons leaving their business in the evening travelled home by the Company's line, and returned to the city the following morning In; their ," own line" and in their "own train." The change was made so quietly that pafsengers .would not have known any change had taken place, had they not have been informed by the Press. I have not yet heard that any homes have been destroyed or that Christianity has suffered as a result of the change.</p>
        <p>Practicalry the whole of the railway service in New Zealand is now National property. It may be interesting to know that workers can travel to and from their work, a distance of WI miles each way, for the sum of two shillings per week, and that children who have to travel to the nearest school by train are conveyed free of charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WAR SCARE.</head>
        <p>THE WAR SCARE.</p>
        <p>The '• War Scare" has been felt on this side of the globe as well as at home, and as you are aware, the New Zealand Premier made an offer to the Home Government of a Dreadnought, and if necessary another Would be given. This offer appears to have been made hastily, without the consideration</p>
        <p>and consent of the New Zealand Parliament, and even without the Cabinet having been called together to consider the question. Sir Joseph Ward's action has been strongly criticised here, especially by the Labour and Socialist organisations, and it is expected that when Parliament assembles in June next, some of. the members will raise a pro- test against it. One cannot be enamoured of a professedly democratic Government which permits of one man placing such a heavy tax upon the people as the offer of Sir Joseph Ward will result in. PNEUMOCONIOSIS.</p>
        <p>The passing of the Compensation (Amend- ment) Act 1908 has been the cause of friction between the miners and their employers. The ,Act of last year included a number of diseases for which compensation may be claimed and pneumoconiosis (or miner's disease) was one of them. The Insurance Companies increased their premiums on the plea of greater liability, and the employers declined to bear the extra cost. The Employers Federation therefore gave notice that all men before being allowed to com- mence work, after the date when the Act became operative, should undergo a medical examination. The object of the employers was to refuse employment to any of the men found to be suffering from the disease. To this the miners objected and the result was that the men were practically " locked out." The two main objections to the examina- tion were (Ist) that the men found to be suffering from the disease would be deprived of their means of livelihood, even though they had contracted the disease in the mines, and (2nd), they were of the opinion that the demand for mPdical examination was to not only find who were suffering from pneumoconiosis,but men sufferingfrom weak- ness of any kind would be refused employ- ment. The lockout lasted for some weeks and has been temporarily removed by the Government deciding to guarantee the State Insurance Accident Department against loss if they would insure at the former rates for all other diseases. The employers, although dissatisfied at the turn of events, decided to waive their demand for medical inspection, and the men returned to work. Strong pro- tests were raised against the action of the Government, especially by the Insurance Companies, who object to the State Insurance Department being subsided out of National funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EMIGRATION.</head>
        <p>EMIGRATION.</p>
        <p>The various Trades Councils and Labour organisations are urging the Government to cancel their policy of Assisted Emigration as being against the interests of the workers of the Dominion. Not only are they asking for assisted emigration to be stopped, but are asking the Government to make known as widely as possible, through their representa- tives in London, what the real conditions are here concerning unemployment. There is a large army of unemployed• here, and the winter season having just commenced, it is expected that during the next three or four months, the number of unemployed will be largely increased. The Government have given instructions to the High Commission to have a closer inquiry made into the quali- fications of all candidates for assisted emigration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ON YOUR SIDE.</head>
        <p>ON YOUR SIDE.</p>
        <p>I have been keenly interested in the excellent reports of the Election Petition which appeared in The Examiner." In all probability the fact that the respondent was my successor in the Council made the report more interesting to me. I have to-day learnt the result of the bye-election and regret that my friend and comrade James Hampson was not placed at the head of the poll. I was surprised and disappointed to find that Warrington had been defeated in the first round of the N.U. Cup Competition. Football is just getting into swing here. I was hoping that we should see a N.U. team in Wellington this year from the old country. However, the Northern Union is in its infancy here, and it is questionable whether it will become successful owing to lack of spectators. When we consider that the population of Wellington is about the same as Warrington it would be difficult to obtain support for a numbet of first-class professional teams. I take this opportunity of informing our Warrington friends that Mrs. Stott and the children arrived safely by the s.s. " Corinthic " on March 21st and that we are all in excellent health. No doubt the four months' work in the open air near the open sea is responsible for my health being much better than for many years past.—Yours, &amp;c., FRED STOTT. Weka Bay, Roseneath, Wellington, New Zealand.</p>
        <p>April 29th, 1909. P.S.—I regret to find from the " Exarhiner " received to-day a report of the death of Mr. J. Podmore. I have not yet forgotten the " fatherly " grip he gave my hand when I bade him " Good-bye " in August last year. ' F. S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY. ---- Meeting of the Libaral Ward Committee.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD VACANCY. ---- Meeting of the Libaral Ward Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for White- cross Ward was held in the Thewlis-street schools on Wednesday evening. Alderman James Evans pre- sided over alarge attendance which included Coun- cillors Barton and Hawthorn, Messrs. R. W. Cook and W. Unsworth (secs). and J. Howes, jun. (agent). The question of the vacancy created by the election of Councillor Evans to an aldermanic seat was con- sidered, and several names were suggested, with a view to selecting a candidate. A deputation was appointed to wait upon these gentlemen. A vote of congratu'ation to Alderman Evans ,on the, honour conferred upon him and upon the ward by the Town Council was heartily accorded on the proposition of Councillor Hawthorn seconded by Councillor Barton. Alderman Evans suitably replied and said he regarded his election to an aldermatnc. .seat as a great honour not only to hfinself but to the Whitecross Ward.' He should contfnue. to do his his utmost on behalf of his constituents and for the town! generally. We understand that the four gentlemen whom it was decided to approach are : Mr. H. Roberts, Captain' G. IL Crosiield, Mr. L. Greenink and Dr. Markel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ROSE TREES IN A TOWN GARDEN.</head>
        <p>ROSE TREES IN A TOWN GARDEN.</p>
        <p>The trees must be welt watered and fed if the best results are to be obtained. I had one small specimen of that grand variety Mme. Abel Chateney, which was last year soMPIN hat neglected on account of its apparent uselessness. Then 1 commenced to regularly water end feed it with remarkably good results ; young shoots began to grow, and in a very short time more than a dozen buds formed, and ell opened beautifully. This is a grand variety for growing in a town garden and for filling vases. 'I do not know of any other sort that opens as freely in water when placed there in the bud stage.—Avon, in "'rho Garden."</p>
        <p>COM M ERC lAL FAlLllßES.—According to Tamps Mercantile Gazette, the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and \Vales during the week ending Friday, June 1104 was 144, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 90; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 54. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: Bankruptcies, 61; Deeds of Arrange- ment, 59—Total ]2O; heing an increase of 24. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 24 weeks of the present year is 3,729; the total number recorded in the corresponding 24 -weeks of last year was 3,959, showing a decrease of 230.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>ECZEMA MADE SKIN PEEL Off</head>
