      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JUNE 19TH, 1909.</p>
        <p>We trust that the</p>
        <p>THE WAIL OF country will give as THE LANDLORD. much attention as it</p>
        <p>can spare from other matters to consideration of the hard case of the EARL OF ONSLOW. This nobleman is the Chairman of Committees in the House of Lords. He is a Conservative. He has, in fact, held salaried positions in Conservative administrations, and Colonial positions, likewise salaried, to which he was appoinied by Conservative Governments. When the Liberal party came into power it 'might have appointed one of its own supporters to the post of Chairman of Committees in the House of Lords; but it did not do so. It left tile Earl of Onslow in comfortable possession of a salary of between two and three thousand pounds a year. That was not ungenerous. It was even noble. The post of Chairman of Committees in the House of Lords is not absolutely a sinecure, but it is not onerous. The Chairman of Committees in the House of Commons is a frightfully overworked man in comparison with the Earl of Onslow; but it is the Earl of Onslow, who receiving a quite comfortable and even gen- H)us recompense for sitting, on occasion, in the same seat in the House of Lords, and doing nothing in particular, who announces that he is being driven to sell part of his estates and dismiss many of his labourers because of the manner in which he is being oppressed by the Budget. It seems that the Budget is oppressing 'every- body. Lord ONSLOW is selling his land because he cannot afford to keep it; but, of course, he could not sell his land if there were not ready purchasers, who understand quite well that the Budget excepts agricul- tural land from its operations. There are men in the City of London who declare that the Budget will be ruinous to the commerce of the country, but they find the price of securities rising day by day, and they are speculating in the biggest boom that has been known for something like a dozen tears. The Budget is to ruin the public- house interest; but two days since tied- houses were selling in London far in advance of the prices that were anticipated by their owners. The Budget that was to injure every interest and harass every owner of every sort of property whatever, has, in fact, up to this time brought nothing but pros- perity and profit to all the interests which it is supposed to be rolling in the dust. Lord 0/4sLow's point of view is that of the other landed-proprietor who announced the other day that in consequence of the Budget he might find it necessary to cease to give tips to his railway porters. We are afraid that we must go to the length of saying that Lord ONSLOW'S statements about the effects of the Budget are not only without substance behind them, but that they are untrue. Therq is nothing in the Budget which should mak° him desirous of selling his land or of dis- missing his labourers. But in any case he is mistaken in supposing that this ill-considered action of his will injure anybody. The work that has been done by his own servants will, he says, be done by contractors. The work will have to be done—that is to say, it will employ the same number of men—in all prob- ability it will pay the same amount' in wages. Lord ONsLow's iidea, seems to be that some- thing must be crushed .because quite mis- takenly, as it appears, he supposed himself to be unduly persecuted by the Budget. But if there is any injury done it must be to the men who have devoted the best part of their lives to Lord ONsow's service, men who may not be taken on by the contractors because they have become old or decripid whilst toil- ing for his lordship. They will be 'thrown on the parish probably; but there will be work for others in their place. As for the sale of his lordship's land, why that will, according to his own admission, be a great public advantage. There are a great many people in Surrey, he sa!ys, who want land fox resi- dential purposes. Just so; and when the land is thrown open to them they will employ a much greater number of workmen than ever Lord Onslow did. We will take the liberty of suggesting to his lordship that if he looks into his own mind with a sincere scrutiny he will find that heis proposing to sell a portion of his estate because he finds it to be to his advantage to do so, and not at all because the Budget has made him feel like a toad under the harrow.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Pro- OPEN-AIR POLITICS. gressive Labour League is doing an excellent public service in exposing tariff reform fallacies and upholding the great cause of Free Trade and progress. The splendid suc- cess which has so far attended the open-air campaign shows that the people of Warring- ton are not being carried away by the specious promises of the Tariff Reform League, and the enthusiasm which the men- tion of Mr. Lloyd George's heroic Budget has invariably called forth is an evidence' that the workers have firm faith in. Free Trade finance as a basis of social reform. Many aspects of the question have been dealt with by the able speakers which the League has been fortunate enough to) secure, and the addresses have been marked by lucidity and directness, coupled with a wealth of homely illustration. Large audiences have listened' with deep ,attention and have not been slow to appre- ciate the chief points emphasized and</p>
        <p>elaborated—that tariff reform, far from meaning more work and better wages, would hamper industry, raise the price of the necessities of life, and tend to a reduction of wages. The meetings have been characterised by remarkable unanimity, - and although questions are always invited few have been forthcoming. The members of this active League intend to persevere with their campaign which, we believe, is destined to have most valuable results in moulding and consolidating progressive opinion in War- rington.</p>
        <p>The question raised by A CORONER FOR Dr. Joseph at the last WARRINGTON. meeting of the Town Council about the need for a town's coroner in order to lessen the percentage (6'3 per • cent.) of uncertified deaths has progressed a step further. At the conclusion of an inquest at Warrington on Wednesday, Coroner Brighouse said "he wished to mention a matter which was of moment to himself and to the borough of War- rington." He then read correspondence which had passed between himself and the Medical Officer of Health, Dr. J. Coote Hibbert, on the high percentage of uncertified deaths in Warrington. A copy of the correspondence is printed elsewhere in this issue. From it our readers will not be able to conclude much more than that each writer has considerable justification for his conten- tions. Mr. Brighouse has both the law and propriety on his side in. avoiding any unnecessary intrusion on the privacy of a family in distress, but it seems to us that he rather misses the point of the medical officer's argument. Dr. Coote Hibbert refers specific- ally to the number of deaths among infants which are uncertified, while the Coroner generalises upon presumptive cases where people of all ages many die from causes which cannot be considered suspic- ous and therefore not worthy of inquest. The important point is that there is a carelessness about child-life among the poorer classes, particularly •in regard to feeding, which leads to the conclusion that uncertified deaths in this class might with advantage be inquired into. Herein lies the reason for Dr. Joseph's suggestion that a borough coroner would be a useful official because he would instil a fear of inquiry into the minds of neglectful and careless parents. Coroner Brighouse does not allude -to this aspect of the question and we• should not be surprised to learn that he deems any division of inquest duties unnecessary.</p>
        <p>In the current number TARIFF REFORM of "The Shoe and AND THE Leather Record"appears LEATHER TRADE. a long letter from Mr.</p>
        <p>C. E. Parker criticising the conclusions based by the "Daily Express" on a suggested ten per cent. duty on imported leather and leather goods. The "Daily Express," which only received 74 replies favourable to the duty from 328 firms in the trade which were circularised, proceeded to make calculations to suit its own policy. In return for the duty, the" Express" promises employment for 28,875 hands and £37,375 extra weekly wages. Mr. Parker shows by sound argument that such a duty would not benefit the leather trade, but would force up the cost to the consumer by from three to four million pounds. The increased cost would result in less spending power, less demand for the manufactured article, and a subsequent re-action upon both export and import trade., "The Shoe and Leather Record" agrees editorially with Mr. Parker's reasoning, and adds its conviction .that "both the shoe and the leather trades are better off under Free Trade than they would be under a protective, system. Moreover, as Mr. Parker points out, there are large numbers of people whose wages or profits are not affected by foreign competition in any way whatever. If it were possible to separate our population into two groups, one group who were in competition with the foreigner, and the other who were not so affected, the latter would be found by far the more important. On broad national grounds the plea for protection is not yet made out; and from the point of view of the industries with which we are specially concerned, protection would, in our opinion, do much more harm than good."</p>
        <p>Those persons who MR, BALFOUR AND were koping to dis- THE FINANCE BILL cover in Mr. Balfour's speech upon the second reading of the Finance Bill one of those " signals " by which he is accustomed to intimate to the House of Lords that the 11 Opposition looks to them to" do their duty" must have been extr,emely disappointed. The general tenour of his criticisms was such as to suggest rather that he was making a perfunctory attack, because it is the business of the Opposition to oppose, upon a measure with which he was largely in agreement, than the determined hostility which would adopt almost any means to destroy the Liberal Budget. If the House of Lords were to reject the Finance Bill they would create a crisis which would go deeper down into the founda- tions of political life than the dislocation of national affairs—serious as that would be— and the question whether, in the legal and historical sense, such action would be positively unconstitutional. It is something more 'even than a constitutional principle that the House of Commons should be, as Mr. Balfour has said, " uncontrolled " in its dealing with national finance. It is the essential condition, the indispensable element in an atmosphere of democratic and repre- sentative Government. The Upper House, since it i&amp; not a representative House, could not put forward the claim to decide that this or that form of taxation is objectionable and must not be employed—and the corollary of such a decision, it must be remembered, is the decision that an alternative form must be adopted—without changing the whole face of 'political life, and bringing to the front a totally different set of problems and issues.</p>
        <p>In spite of the rigidity SHOPS FOR BRAIN of the party' _syStem, _ _</p>
        <p>AND MUSCLE. there are tili some.</p>
        <p>things upoft which both sides in the House of of Commons can agree. Mr: Churchill's Labour Exchanges Bill passed its second reading. without a division. That legislative megatherium, Sir Frederick Banbury—who admitted that he would have 'killed his own colleagues' Unemployed Act if he had had a chance—was the only person to offer it any serious opposition. Frankly speaking, it is extraordinary that we have not,in this respect imitated the Germans before. One would have thought that to any one who had studied, as several generations of 'politicians have studied, the works of the 'orthodox political economists, Labour 'Exchanges would have been recognised as the necessary corollary, of their doctrines. 'Complete fluidity of capital and labour were needed to secure, we were told, the efficient working of the industrial system. Yet !right up to the present we have !conducted the flow of labour along diffi- cult and roughly-hewn channels. "Labour," as Mr. Money puts it. " has had no shop." Whilst every other commodity has had a- world market and an amazingly ,sensitive distributive machine, labour .has been hawked and peddled in the old .Plantagenet• Way just as if trade were still a</p>
        <p>purely local affair. If there is work going men hear of it late and rush to it in excessive numbers. Hence waste. If they hear of none they tramp the streets looking for it until soul and body are sick, and a • man of spirit is hard put to it not to" hit out" at a society which seemingly turns him n the Cold shoulder. Hence more 'waste of every description. This at least will be saved by the new bureaux, and this alone makes them worth having.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ND' AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>ND' AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Visitors to Queen's Gardens this week have noticed a handsome addition to the embellishments of the grounds in the shape of a massive vase which has been placed in a prominent position. The vase is one of two which were presented to the Corporation by the late Mr. Joseph Davies, of the Old Warps, Latchford. The other stands in the Centre of the lawn at the back of the Town Hall. * * *</p>
        <p>A very good specimen of the English "bull" was presented (unconsciously) by the Rev. Thomas Gladding to the congregation of Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday Morning. While narrating a story for the edification of the children he said "there in the empty box lay a nest and five eggs." Even the grown-up children missed the joke at first but " bulls " in church are not stch familiar things that they can be recognised at a glance. * * * _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Gleave,of the Free Trade Union, whose address on the fiscal question at War- rington a few weeks ago created such a great impression, will speak at the Progressive Labour League's open-air meeting next Monday evening. During the past week Mr. Gleave, who is an exceptionally able defender of Free Trade, has been addressing a series of meetings at Birkenhead and he is doing much useful work in combating the fiscal heresiesibf the Tariff Reform League in the north of England. ** * _ _</p>
        <p>Dear fuel is not altogether an evil. It checks waste.—Lord Airedale. It does not matter whom I tax; he will protest.—The Chancellor of the Exchequer. Thanks to the Women Suffrage movement young girls are now attempting to solve national problems that are unknown to boys of the same age.—Miss Gertrude Kingston. There is a person we do sot want in our schools—a teacher who has no belief at all, who will willinglST teach anything, who sub- mits to confirmation to get into a Church Training College, or enters a Roman Catholic Training College with indifference.—The Rev. Prebendary Bernard Reynolds. . * * *</p>
        <p>"How to be happy though married" is a problem which is solved with much more facility in America than in this country. For instance, Los Angeles County has set up a new record since January by granting divorces in the ratio of one to every, four marriages. A year ago the ratio was Ito 6. In San Francisco it was 1 to 7, and in the United States Ito 12. You need not con- skier the marriage tie anything of the kind in such places as Los Angeles. It is more like a pretty silken bow for temporary wear. * * *</p>
        <p>A curious revelation of the "ways and means" problem as it affects the poorer classes was made in the Police Court on Monday, when a pawnbroker stated that over 700 people entered his shop ,on the Tuesday following Whit-Monday, and 100 of -them before 7 a.m., all with articles on which to "raise the wind." No doubt these poor folks enjoyed themselves on the Bank Holiday and came home with empty purses. We wonder what proportion of their scanty coin found its way into the publican's till? As a rule the connection between the pub and the pawnshop is pretty close. * *</p>
        <p>Is the Isle of Man to become a haven of refuge for overtaxed millionaires? In the House of Commons on Wednesday, Mr. A. J. King asked the Chancellor of the Exchequer whether, under the finance Bill, Clause 50, residents in the Isle of Man will be able to claim exemption, abatement, or relief under the Income Tax Acts, or ,whether they will, for the purposes of these Acts, be regarded as residing outside the United Kingdom. Mr. Lloyd George's reply was: The Isle of Man is not included in the United Kingdom for purposes of taxation, and persons resident in the island are not resident in the United Kingdom for the purposes of Clause 50 of the rinance Bill.</p>
        <p>* * * 'The term "Cave," which is being applied in these days to the small group of Liberal members who are opposed to the land taxa- tion proposals in the Budget, was first applied .by John Bright in the House of Commons on March 13th, 1866, to the small band of Liberal dissentients Who were opposing the Franchise Bill of which Mr. Gladstone had charge in Earl Russell's Government. Addressing Mr. Horsman, who' had been Chief Secretary for Ireland, Mr.. Bright said "he retired into what might be called his political Cave of Adullam.'' to which he invited everyone who was in distress and everyone who was discontented."</p>
        <p>* * * The tariff reformers are greatly perturbed because Mr. Joseph Howes persists in directing public• attention to the way in. which the landowners handicap our indus- tries by exacting heavy mining royalties. That he has succeeded in "touching the spot" is evident from the strennons efforts of his opponents to under-rate the significance of the impost which hampers our manufacturers in the com- mercial struggle. Mr. Howes, however, is not to be deterred from his pprpose and on Monday evening he returned to the attack in a speech of considerable power, which greatly appealed to his audience. Councillor liroadhurst has described the royhrties :question as "a red herring,". but Mr. Howes very. aptly replies that it is a strong red herring and it is time they smelled it _ ***</p>
        <p>' We have no doubt that our readers will peruse with interest the letter from Mr. F. Stott which appears on page. Mr. Stott has soon got keenly interested in national politics and labour conditions out in New Zealand, and his comparison between the conditions obtaining in the new Country and in the old are marked by characteristic keenness of perception and breadth of, view. His refer- erices to the gradual nationalisation of 'almost the whole of the railways in New Zealand should induce serious thought here, where huge Companies adopt a cut-throat policy to •earn dividends and the requirements of the traveller are a secondary consideration. We should feel convinced that we had gained something tangible and precious if we could travel 10 miles to and from business on our omen rail- way at a cost of about 2s. per week, and:.at- the same time our children could travel free to school. Government "of the people, for the people, and by the people" is something like a reality in New Zealand, and the colonists can still afford to give us a Dread- - naught to stiffen our trembling knees in the face of the enemy.</p>
        <p>Some letters written by Henry Ward Beecher to Major Pond are being published in the New York "Outlook." One of them shows that Beecher regarded the high tariff wall built round American industry as a huge blunder. He says: " It is an insult to American enterprise to assume that it , needs Protection. American industry is no decrepit thing, needing crutches and nurses. It puts the Government into the ridiculous attitude of undertaking to supervise all the various and intricate affairs of manufacture and commerce. It is the last vestige of that old system of paternal governments to arrange men's religious beliefs, to determine their social relations, to prescribe their meat, drink and apparel ; to do for them what a free people are a thousand 'times better able to do for themselves. The aim and drift of Protection is foolish and impertinent, but the machinery by which it seeks to secure this bad end is even worse than the cause which it serves and corrupts. Custom houses in their nature are academies of injustice and dishonesty abhorred of God as they ought to be of men. * * Sir William P. Hartley, who has just been elected President of the Primitive Methodist Conference, has been a princely supporter not only of his own church but of numerous other Christian charities and churches. Again and again he, has given large sums to the Primitive Methodist Church, including .E 12,000 to the enlargement eleven years ago of Manchester Connexional College; in 1907 .E 25.000 to add a study block and chapel to the same institution, £31,000 to purchase Holborn Town Hall for use as a Primitive Methodist Church House, 5,000 guineas to the Wesleyan Million Pound Fund; 1902, £7,500 towards the building of the Liverpool Consumption Sanatorium at Delamere, .E 13,500 to Liverpool University for the erection of a botany laboratory. To his native town of Colne he has presented a Queen Victoria Memorial cottage hospital, while just recently Sir William has made several most welcome monetary gifts to for- ward the building of the new parish church of Emmanuel, Fazakerley, and the new mission Church of St. Nathanaers, Walton, while his gifts to temperance, &amp;c., have also been most extensive. * *</p>
        <p>In the village of Dunham Massey, in a cottage garden not far from the stately home of the Earl of Stamford, are two gravestones which evidently indicate the site of a burial ground of more than two hundred years old. i'Apparently few people have either seen or heard of them (says the "Manchester Courier ") and the records of the village contain no record of the people buried there. On one stone is the following inscription</p>
        <p>—We shall all rise again—. Elizabeth Nield Byried Detember 5, 1670. Bvried heere since, Two dayhters. All Died in Peace. On the other the inscription reads: Here Lyeth the Body of John Neild Bachelor, who departed this Life the second day of December, 1702, in the Seaventy-first year of his Age Leaving the interest of fiftie pound to the highways of Dunham Massey For ever. Many years ago the heavy flat stones were raised and underneath them were found two vaults and several coffins. One of the coffins was opened, and inside it were seen the remains of one of the women, which crumbled in dust as they were exposed to the air. In an adjoining garden are two or three yews which offer a silent testimony in support of the assumption that the ground hereabouts was once used as a place of burial. Its origin is somewhat obscure, but tradition asserts that in the seventeenth century there were several families in Bowdon and the neighbourhood belonging to the Society of Friends, and it is quite possible that they may have had a private burial ground near to their meeting house in Dunham Massey. That the money bequeathed by John Neild was used for the repair of the highways is denoted by a deed, dated 31st of August, 1714, which was recently discovered among some old town- ship documents by the Earl of Stamford, at Dunham Hall.</p>
        <p>Personal. Dr. Collins, has been very seriously ill at his home on Wilderspool Causeway during, the week. Last night his condition was unchanged. Aldezman Pierpoint and Alderman John Evans, who were both seized with sudden illness on the same day, are now convalescent and have been out of doors in the prevailing sunny weather. Captain G. R. Crosfield, who has been on a vi,it to America, returned home on the Cunard Liner garonia which arrived at Liverpool on Sunday morning.</p>
        <p>Hill CHM Baptist Church. Anniversary services will be held at this Church to-morrow (Sunday), when the Rev. J. B. Turner, of Clough Fold, will preach.</p>
        <p>TENNIS, CROQUET, AND GARDEN FURNITURE.— J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Co-operative Employes' Picnic. The employes. of the Warrington Co-operative Society held their annual picnic on Thursday, when Windermere was visited. The weather was beauti- fully fine and the outing proved one of the most successful on record. The party, numbering about 150, -left Warrington in specially reserved carriages at 8.30 a.m. There was no pre-arranged programme, the trippers spending the day as inclination dictated. Many of the beauty spots of the district were visited and sails on the Lake were greatly enjoyed. War- rington was reached about 11 o'clock, the outing having passed off without the slightest hitch.</p>
        <p>The Territorials. ' In connection with the 4th V.B. South Lancashire Regiment (Warrington Territorials) it is announced that a Church parade will be held on July 4th, when the presentation of long service and efficiency medals will take place. We understand it is proposed to hold the service on the Recreation Ground in the Park. Warrington Wire-Workers in Trouble. At the Shropshire Assizes, held at Shrewsbury, on Monday, before Mr. Justice Ridley, William Smith (34) and Frederick Page (36), wire-workers from Warring- ton, pleaded guilty to house-breaking at Warrington, and were each sentenced to six months' with hard labour. Street Collections.</p>
        <p>Warrington people had an opportunity on Saturday of subscribing to two worthy institutions, by means of collections which were made in the streets. Th, effort on behalf of the National Children's Home and Orphanage, whose work was described in our columns last week, realized £lB. The annual collection in aid of the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission also took place the sum of £l6 being collected. TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, Sun' CASES, etc. Special lines at 17s. 205., 225.. and 265. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. School Children and Harvest Work. •</p>
        <p>- - A meeting on Monday at Crewe of the Cheshire County Education Committee discussed suggested by-laws for Cheshire schools with regard to the Employment of Children' Act, and the question of employing children in the harvest field was raised, Upon a motion by Judge Bradbury an alteration of the by-law was agreed to under which on school days children under 14 years of age should not be allowed to work more than 31 hours a day. • GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, £5 55., .£8 135., and £l2 12s. Others at higher prices. Fully guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. It. WooD, 47. London rd., Stockton Heath. Advt. St. Paul's Women's Bible Class. On Monday 9ne hundred and twenty-eight members of the St. Paul's Women's Bible Class spent a very pleasant afternoon on Frodsham Hills, which were reached at four o'clock, the party travelling by omni- buses. They were accompanied by the vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp) and Mrs. Bracecamp and other members of the congregation. Arrived 'at Frcidsham, they had tea at Mersey View and afterwards indulged in various. games. Warrington was reached at 9.30, each and all congratulating themselves upon the beautiful day which had favoured the picnic. LAWN MOWERS.—Several good machines on sale in good condition at prices from 12s. 6d.-51, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Music in the Park.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield's band will play the following programme of music in the Park on Sunday afternoon at 3.15 p,m. :—Hymm, selected; contest march, "The President," Hume, Ist prize, Darwen, Green- field, Mossley, Shrewsbury; overture, " Tancredi," Rossini; Arombone solo and chorus', "Comfort ye" and" The Glory "Handel (soloist, Mr. J. E.Hacking); contest selection, " Verdi's Works," arr Round, Ist prize, Abargavenny, Darwen, Shrewsbury; selection, "Rienzi," Wagner, test piece Crystal Palace 1908; "Clod save the King," Conductor,'Mr. G.F. Bedford. The Borough Brass Band will play the following programme in the Park on Thursday next. Con- ductor, Mr. T. Griffin :—March," Victor," T.,Bates ; fantasia, " Old Favourites," W. Rimmer ; waltz, " Willowdene," W. Timmer; fantasia," Continental Tour," H. Round; seltction, " Memories of Old Ireland," J. Ord Hume ; T` God save the King." Warrington Cycling Club. The run to Chelford on Saturday last was fairly well.attended. The roads were in splendid condition after the previous day's rain, and the weather favour- able to cycling. A pleasant run out was made with a brief stop at Knutsford. The destination was reached in good time where members turned their attention to bowls. An enjoyable afternoon on the green was followed by an excellent tea provided at the Dixon's Arms Hotel. The return run proved as enjoyable as the outward passage and home was reached after a successful afternoon without any mishap. The bowling competitions at Sankey are going merrily on. There was a large attendance on Wednesday evening and the second round of the Cup -competition and two rounds of the consolation handi- cap were got through. The run to Warburton was favoured by fine weather and was vell attended. During their short stay members had an enjoyable game of bowls. The club's arrangements for the coming week are to-day's run to Delamere ; bowling on Wednesday at Sankey, and the run to Glazebury on Thursday.</p>
        <p>THE KING IS COMING. But the King of Tonics is here already. H. M. Ashton's Life Tonic—the great strengthener, s.—Ei . M. ASHTON, 63, Sankey-at., Wairington.—Advt. Rinking and Dancing At the •St. Helens Police Court on Monday, Mr. Forshaw, of Warrington, applied on behalf of Mr. Bertram Silcock, secretary of the Palatine Rinks, for a. music and dancing licence for premises in course of erection in Warrington Old-road to be used as a skat- ing rink.—Alderman Massey thought a dancing licence could not be required for a skating rink.—The Mayor (Councillor Foote) : They dance 94 their sicatest.— Mr. Forshaw ; There is no dancing in the Wavily sense of the term. Simply a healthy sport which has come into vogue lately.—The Clerk (Mr. Brewis) When will it ffo out, do you know? (Laughter).— After deliberating some minutes in private the Mayor announced that the Bench had decided to adjourn the application for fourteen days, in order to be able to consult the Chief Constable, who is away at present. In the meantime rules and regulations as to how it was proposed to conduct the premises would have to be deposited with the Clerk. The Bench were not satis- fied with the application as it was; they wanted to know more about the intentions of the management. Extra police might be required, and if that were so the Bench would have to have the advice of the Chief Constable first.</p>
        <p>THE KING'S VISIT.—FLAGS, STREAMERS, BUNT- LN,G, etc., for sale or hire. Large stock. —GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Warrington Guardians' Excursion. Favoured with brilliant weather, the annual excur- sion of the Guardians and officers of the Warrington Union, which took place on Thursday, was a "complete success. The destination was Windermere and the Lake District, and the party left Bank Quay station by the 7.10 a.m. train. Amongst those taking part in the trip were: Messrs. W. Houghton, W. C. Brooks, G. Mather, E. L. Bent, R. Bolton, M. W. Seton, J. Cook and W. Flannagan (guardians), A. Bottomley (clerk), R. W. Peacock (assistant clerk), Drs. Peacocke and Dowling (medical officers), F. H. Aukland (relieving officer), and B. Shearer (inquiry officer and collector). Windermere was reached at 11.10 and the visitors drove to the Royal Hotel where luncheon was served. Afterwards they enjoyed a 25* miles drive through magnificent scenery, visiting such beautiful places as Grasmere, Red Bank, and. Rydal. On returning to Windermere at five "o'clock dinner was partaken of at the Royal Hotel, after which a pleasant.time Was spent in rowing and sailing on the Lake. The return journey was commenced at. nine o'clock, Warrington being reached at 11-24, after a highly enjoyable outing. The secretarial arrangements were admirably carried out by Mr. B. Shearer. It was generally regretted that Mr.A.J. Jolley, chairman of the Excursion Committee, was unable to accompany the party.</p>
        <p>- - Death of Mrs. Payton. Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mrs. Payton, wife of Mr. James Payton, the licensee of the Old Blue Back,Allen-street,who passed away on. Sunday. The deceased lady, who was sixty years of age and had been in a delicate state of health for some months, as held in high esteem by a Large circle of friends. Much sympathy has been expressed with the widower and family of four daughters and one son, in their ber4avement.—The funeral took place at the Cemeteiy on Thursday, the Rev. John Yonge officiat- ing. The chief mourners were: Mr. James Payton. (husband), Mr. W. H. Payton (son), Mr. A.H. Burgess and Mr. J. Pickton (sons-in-law) ; Mr. H. Mather, Mr. E. Lake, Mr. Bonnell, Mr. G. Wallington. The following acted as bearers: Messrs. W. Brown, H. Hawthorn, A. Griffiths, J. Poole, T. F. Wallington and T. W.: Payton. Floral tributes were sent by the following Mr. Payton (husband). and Blanche," Will, Mary and grandsons Edgar and Bertie," "Tilly, Bert and. grand-daughter Ethel," " Mabel and Ike, " Polly and Pora," " Tilda and George," " Eliza and Harry," Nurse Grounds, Mrs. Baird and Mrs. Higginbottom, members of Wycliffe choir, Mrs. Brophy and family, Mrs. Dean and family, Mr. and Mi.s.Robertson, Mr. and lkirs. J. Bailey and family, Mrs. and Miss Riley, Mr. and Mrs. Tom Payton, Mr. Samuel Wakefield,"Hetty," Mr. and Mrs. H. Pickston, Beer and Wine Sellers' Association, Mrs. Ince and Mrs. Edwards, customers of the Old Blue Back.</p>
        <p>T'VE GOT A GOOD THING ON, Good for You, GOod for*Me. TOMATOES PRONL THE GROWER ON COMMISSION. McDERMOTT, Cheapest Man Alive. TWO STALLS IN MARKET, and BRIDGE-STREET</p>
        <p>The first case of its kind under the new Children's Act was heard at the Borough Police Court on Monday, when the Mayor presided. John Stubbs, a pawnbroker, carrying on business in Lovely-lane, was charged with accepting a pair of boots from a girl named Annie Saunders, aged 13. (The Act btates that no goods or chattels must be accepted from children under 14 years of age). Mr. Browne, solicitor, represented defenlant and denied the charge.—Detective Reddington said he was on duty at 11.30 on the 9th of the present month in defendant's shop when the little girl went in the shop and offered for pledge a pair of boots. The girl appeared to be under ape and on witness asking her, she replied that she would be 14 next Walking Day. Witness sent the girl home. He then approached the assistant in the shop, Austin Crow, who had accepted the boots and asked him why he had done so. The man said that the girl was over 14 years of age. Witness said Ile knew differently and would have to report the matter.—Annie Saunders, the daughter of John Saunders, of Robert-street, said she had been to the shop on several occasions. Mr. Stubbs •asked her age and she said "14 next Walking Day." In answer to Mr. Browne she said that she pledged the goods in the name of Bond.—Mr. Stubbs stated that he had carried on the business for over two years. He had notices in his shop to the effect that no goods can be pledged by children under 14 years of age. He knew the girl as Annie Bond had never known her as Saunders. Defendant had asked the girl her age and she replied "14 last Walking Day." On the morning in question there was a very large number in the shop. Defendant remembered rejecting a number of children under age that morning. Continuing defendant said there was 700 people in the shop that day. Sergeant Barlow who was also in the shop during this particular day saw the defendant reject a number of children. Mr. Browne in his defence said be thought it was a very trivial ease to be brought before the magistrates. The Act did not state that all children must bring their certificates of birth and the pawnbroker had only to satisfy himself that they were apparently over the age of 14.—After deliberation the Bench agreed to dismiss the case.</p>
