      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. .1. W. Hayward (in the chair) A. .1. Jolley, It. T. Fairclough. T. Pemberton, Dr. Markel and Dr. Joseph. THEFT OF SEED.—Sarah Maloney of no settled abode, was charged with stegling a basket containing a quantity of seed valued at £3, between March 7th and 2.3 rd, the property of Joseph Lawrenson. She pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the prosecutor was a green grocer and carried on business in Old-road. On Wednesday March Nth, the seed was seen by him 'safe in the kitchen, but on looking for it the following Tuesday be found it had gone. He reported the matter to the police. Detective Clough made inquiries and found that the prisoner had sold the seed to a woman at Latchford. Nothing was heard of her until the previous night when Detectibe Reddington saw her looking in the Seven Stars Bridge Street and brought her to the Police Station.—Prisoner who had 23 previous convictions for drunkenness, disorderly con- duct etc., was sentenced to four months hard labour. A SEttious OFFENCE.—George Parr, of Knutsford- road, was sentenced to three month's hard labour (the maximum penalty) for indecent exposure on April 24th and June 7th.—Ptisoner said he could not account for his conduct. He had gone through a great deal of trouble lately, and this was causing him to lose his mental balance. MONDAY.--Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Smethurst and Messrs. S. Wright, W. Roberts, W. Furness, H. Thornton and G. Piling. BEGGING.—Tbomas Mitchell % of Barhauld-street, was sentenced to three months for begging in Market- place on Saturday. Prisoner had been previously Convicted. 6311 D APPEARANCE.—John Heaton, who made his -Ord appearance before the magistrates, was sentenced to one month's hard 14bour for sleeping in a shad on the premises of Messrs. Fairclough and Co.' Me.sev Mills, on Sunday. Thomas Hickman and Annie Hickman received one month each for sleeping in an unoccupied house in School Brow on Sunday night. AN UNLICENSED DOG.—James Barnes, 86, Bewsey- street was fined lfis. for keeping a dog without a licence. • OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—WiIIiam Cunningham 9, James- street, was summoned for using obscene language in Scotland-road.—The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>Tuesday,—Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (chairman), W. Richardson and C. Jackson Holmes. DISTURBANCES IN SIIIP VARD.—Bridget Costello, Ship-3ard. was summoned for making use of obscene language on the Bth .June.—Defendant pleaded guilty ' because she was very poorly." She was fined ss. and costs.—Margaret Rhoney, Lord Nelson-street, and Christopher Couley, Ship-yard, were charged with assaulting Mary Rogerson, a lodging-house keeper. They pleaded 'not guilty."—A cross-summons against Mary Rogerson was also taken out, to which Mr. A. Browne, w ho represented her, pleaded not guiltv.-51r. Browne stated that Mr. nnd airs. Rogerson kept a common lodging-house in Ship-yard, and it was a very difficult task to carry on the business in a right and proper manner. It appeared that Mrs. Rogerson was eequested to give evidence at the Police Station against Mrs. Costello (the defendant in the above case). Having .given evidence the case was disposed of end she went home and found all the people. including the two defendants, drinking. The complainant went up to Mrs. Costello and told her how sorry she was for her ond said she would pay the wompn'a fine. whereupon the female defendant went up to her and said" Oh, you are square with the police," and hit complainant in the face, and also in the left eye. Mrs. Rogerson then struck the defendant back in self defence. Couley • then went up and also struck complainant. The police would agree with him (Mr. Browne) that Mrs. Rogerson required all protection in order to keep the common lodging house.—Mrs. Rogerson corroborated and Mary and Thomas Connelly spoke to witnessing the assault.— The cross-summons was then proceeded with, in which Margaret Rhoney stated that Mrs. Rogerson hit her first. —Four witnesses were called in support of this state- ment.—Rhoney was fined 10s. and costs, the summons against Cowley was dismissed, and the cross- summons against Mrs. Rogerson was also dismissed. .THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), A. J. Jolley, John Upson, William Roberts, , J. Rymer Young, and W. I'. Williams. ALeee RD MARKET THEPT.—Three Warrington youths named James Harris,lodaing at Gallop's, Buttermarket- ' street, Alfred Bent,lB, George-street,and Frank Stokes, 3, Edward-strret, werecharged with stealing from the stall of Mr. Rosenburgh in the market, five vests, one jacket and one boy's suit value 18s. 3d.—lnspector Williamson asked for a remand until Saturday morning and continuing said that Mr. Rosenburgh made his stall secure late on Saturday evening the sth of this month. On the Sunday evening when the constable on .duty was making his rounds be Sew the stall open. The matter was inquired into by Sergeant "Meacock and Detective Clough. Harris pawned one of the vests on the Monday, Bent sold one for a shilling and Stokes :sold one for twopence to a gipsy. From the informa- tion the police had received they thought prisoners were concerned in other robberies.—The remand was granted. TiCK ETLESS Tuaveemist.—Henry Brown, sign writer Pringle-street, Liveroool, was charged witn travel- ling on the London and Nortb-Western Railway from Liverpool to Warrington on the 16th inst. without a ticket, and with intent to avoid payment of the fare.— Ticket-collector Dawson said that he was on duty when • -the train from Liverpool arrived at 9.48. He challenged prisoner for his ticket and he replied that be was going north. Witness demanded the ticket and he said he would not give it him. He would not give him his name and address either. Witness sent for the Police and he was taken into custody. Sergeant Meacock gave evidence to arresting prisoner and said that be had no ticket in his possession except an old one from -Waterloo to Liverpool.—Prisoner said that he had a lot of drink and had evidently got in a wrong train, for be was going to Tuebrook a few miles out of Liverpool. There was no reason at ell for him to come to Warrington. —The Bench imposed a fine of 10s. including .costa and is. 6d. to pay to the London and North- Western Railway Company, or 7 days. BOYS' DISGRACEFUL BEHAVIOUR.—Two boys named Peter Bate (aged 14) and John Cartwright' (aged 12), of Hamilton-street. were charged with a serious offence on a girl of 12 :Sears on Sunday night. Bate was sent to a . reformatory for three years, and Cartwright bound over to be of good behaviour for three years in the sum of £lO and one sueety of .£lO, and to be under the super- vision of the probation officer. FRlDAY.—Before the Deputy-Mayor (chairman), Alder- man J. Evans, Messrs. W. Furness, H. Thornton, C. S. Burgess and G. Pilling. , SEPARATION 0 lIDER GRANTED.—John Longshaw Whittaker, of Pinner's Brow was summoned by his wife (Emily) for being an habitual drunkard, and in cense- quence of which she applied for a separation order.— Mr. Browne, who appeared for the complainant, said that he had approached the wife, who had a good business as a greengrocer on Piuners Brow, and had suggested an adjournment of the case for four weeks, in* order to get defend int to sign the pledge. There was an unfortunate accident some years ago, when the Bold- street Chapel gates fell upon defendant, injuring his head and in consequence very little drink would affect him. The wife had said that defendant was an excellent husband, except when he took too much drink.— Defendant said that a man could not possibly be an habitual drunkard it he had to work 7 days per -week.lie would not agree to NI r.Browne's suggest ion as to -an adjournment and the case was proceeded with. Mr. Browne stated that there were five children and one son had left home through the defendant's ways. the daughters had also threatened that if proceedings were not taken,</p>
        <p>they would leave home as well. For the past 181 months defendant had not been sober for two days together. On Sunday last he ran them all out of the house, and even in the presen .e of Detective Metcalf the following day he did likewiie. He (Mr. Browne) bad proof that defendant was an habitual drunkard. He went. out, with a horse and cart but rarely brought any money home.—Emily Whittaker (complain int) corrobor- ated Mr. Browne's statements and said that defendant was at one time a Sunday school teacher. His eldest son (the one who had left homes wet, also a Sunday school teacher and was also in the choir.—Margaret Whittaker, a daughter, said that durine the past 18 months her father had been terrible. He was never sober.—A separation order was granted, the wife to retain the business.</p>
        <p>SEPARATION ORDER REPCSED.—WiIIiam Kay, of Cartwright-street was summoned by his wife (Fanny) for neglecting to provide reasonable maintenance' in consequence of which she had to leave him on the lOth October. She asked for a separation order to be granted. Complainant stated they were married on the 24th September last year. On asking defendant for money the lint week after the marriage, he told her thtt if he , had known that, she was going to aek for it, he would have thrown himself in the "cut." The fliid week he gave her lls. the 3rd and 4th 17s. 61. He bad ptid her nothing, since the 4th week alter the marriage. Complainant was a widow when she was married to defendant.—Defendant handed in a letter from hi wife saying that she didn't want anything more to do with him again, as she did not receive any support from him. —The case was dismissed. _</p>
        <p>ARaEARS --ClFTlifk-I-N-71:-E-NANC K.—William Donnell, C, Edward-street, was summoned for arrears or . mainten- ance amounting to LI 18. 61. doe to the Guardians for the maintenance o f his father.—Mr. Shearer stated that an order was made on November 3rd last to pay 3s. per week for his father-it maintenance. He was a single man and had fairly regular work,and could earn 30s. per week. —Defendant said he thought 2s. was too much to p iy. —The Chairman said it really disgusted him to see men like defendint who refused to pay 24. per waek for the support of their parents. He felt that he would like to horsewhip some of them for the way in which they treated their parents. Defendant would have to p.ty the money at once, or go to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>WARRINGTON COLIN CY PE rry SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON COLIN CY PE rry SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>W}:,;;N:s.l;t:lt..--1-rie-tor-e-lkiesars. John White (in the chair) F. A. Frost, H. Roberts and Roger C. Parr. MiseELLANEOUS.—WiIIiam Tnornas Ashton, Poulton, was charged with being drunk at Poulson on the 31st May. P.C. Butler proved the case and defendant wss fined ss. including costs.—William Rowlinson' Croft, was summoned for allowing a horse t) stray in Spring. lane, Croft, on the 25th May.—P.C. Butler gave evidence and a fine of ss. including costs was imposed.—James Smith, Birkenhead, was summoned for allowing his motor engine to rest on the crown of Spits! Bridge, Poulton, whilst, he drew water from the brook below, on the 24th May.—P.C. Metcalf proved the case and defendant was fined ss. and costs.- -James Gill, of Middleton, was charged with allowing a large quantity of ashes to remain on Liverpool-road, Sanksy, on the 13th ult. Defendant after cleaning his boiler fire out allowed the ashes to remain in the road. When spoken to about it he turned back and removed the cinders.—P.C. Corlett gave evidence,—A fine of 10s. including costs was imposed.—For driving a horse and cart without reins in Warrington Road,Cuerdley, Joseph Humphrifq4 was fined sq. includinf, costs.—Superin- tendent Strickland gave evidence.—Joseph Garrity was charged with having removed goosls from a house in Sankey to avoid distraint for the rent.—Defendant's wife appeared on behalf of Garrity.—Mr.Gittens, agent for Mr. Henry Thornton, who owned the house, sqid that during April Garrity gave him a lot of trouble over the rent. He owed £1 ss. on the 18th May. After remov- ing he went to live at 39, Plumpton-street, where he went under the name of Joseph Beatty.—The magistrates made an order for payment of £2 10s., £1 .ss. for the sent and the coits of the court. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Edward Lance, Rose and Crown-street, and Martha Green, Cross-street, off Winwick-road, were charged with sleeping in a barn at Grange Farm, Sankey, on the 16th Inst and also with being without any visible means of subsistence. P.C. Houghton end Corlett gave evidence to finding them in the barn —They were both sent to prison for one month with har.i labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>POINTS FOR FREE TRADERS. TAXING THE CONSUMER..</head>
        <p>POINTS FOR FREE TRADERS. TAXING THE CONSUMER..</p>
        <p>How the Tariff Falls 03 the Poor in Germany</p>
        <p>The extent of the burden which the import duties on articles of food place on the German consumer has recently been the subject of investigation on the part of some of the most prominent German economists of the day.</p>
        <p>According to Professor Julius Wolf the additional enhancement of prices of necessities of life due to the agrarian tariff cannot aggregate less than £50,000,000. which is equal to a burden of about 16s. per head of the population. In this connection it must always be borne in mind, when comparing the burden of indirect taxation in Germany and in England, that while in the latter the articles affected by the taxes consist of foreign produce, in Germany the import duties also affect the home produce, and therefore cause a rise of prices all round. Another economist, Her Gerloff, has calculated, on ,the basis of a large number of household budgets, the percentage which the amount paid away in indirect taxation forms of a family income, with the follow- ing result:</p>
        <p>FAMILY INCOME. .Percentage of Income paid away in Indirect Taxes. £2oo—£3oo £lOO-1200 .t6o—,tloo £40—£60 Under £4O 4.39 6.50</p>
        <p>These are remarkable figures, showing that with the decrease of income the burden of taxation grows in an inverse ratio, pulling with the greatest weight on those classes which are the least able to bear it.</p>
        <p>And this burden is still growing, as can be seen from the figures recently published by the " Vossische Zeitung " with regard to the recent rise of prices. Taking the prices of the prime necessities of life in the decennium 1889-1898 as equal to 100, it would appear that in April, 1900, the prices of all articles of food, excepting sugar, coffee and herrings, stood considerably higher—rye by 19-7, wheat by 40-2, potatoes by 23-8, beef by 111, pork by 17-1 veal by 501, suet by 56-1 per cent and so forth. This at the present time of industrial Crises creates an ahrost unbearable situation, and calls forth from all sections of the working and lower middle class a cry for the suspension of the import duties and the throwing open of the frontiers. • Yet the Imperial Government finds no better reply to this demand than the imposition of a fresh burden of £25,000,000, of which at least four-fifths are to be raised by indirect taxation.</p>
        <p>Small wonder, then, Prince Bulow, in spite of his present difficulties, refuses to have recourse to the only rational method of settling his differences with the" Bloc," viz., the dissolution of the Reichstag and the ordering of new elections. He knows that the result of the elections at the present moment can only be a complete reversal of his victory in l 907, and a corresponding triumph for his bitterest enemies, the socialists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>PROTECTION GONE MAD.</head>
        <p>PROTECTION GONE MAD.</p>
        <p>Of all the crazy forms Protection takes, Austria is about to go one worse. As in Germany the Agrarian party is the .most powerful: They have already a heavy tax on the imports of beef, and are now agitat- ing and apparently will carry their point, to have a bounty on exported beef. The consumer will thus be doubly hit; he has already to pay on the raised price of his beef in consequence of the duty, the price will be further raised by the reduction in supply due to the export of beef the result of the proposed bounty, and in addition he will'have to pay his share of the bounty as a taxpayer, This movides a further illustration, if such were needed, that tariffs are regulated by the dominant trade interest, and the welfare of the consumer has always to go under.</p>
        <p>WHO PAYS THE DUTY. THE FOREIGNER OR THE HOME BUYER?</p>
        <p>"The Foreigner" says the Tariff Reformer. - The Home Buyer replies with the facts. Average price of Wheat per quarter, 1008 : Great Britain. Germany. 'Actual In London. In Berlin.. Import duty. Difference. 335. 455. ad. its toil. I2S. ad. Percentage of Imported Wheat to total supply:— ;84 39, SUEZ CANAL.</p>
        <p>The report of the Suez. Canal for the year 1908 has just been issued. Although the receipts are down on account of the state of the trade between the East and the West the same dividend is paid as last year by borrowing from the Reserve. The great hold which this country has on the world's shipping is evidenced by the following figures : .</p>
        <p>Total net Nationality. No. of ships. tonnage. British German let ench Dutch Austro-Hungarian... _lO7 - 38,546</p>
        <p>The percentage of the British tonnage together with the number of vessels and tonnage going through the Canal are as follows : No. of . British tonnage Year vessels. Net tons. per cent. 1908 ... 3,795 1907 ... 4,267 1900 ... 3,957</p>
        <p>1905 ... 4,116 1900 ... 3,441</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Mission.</head>
        <p>Ellesmere-street Wesleyan Mission.</p>
        <p>The sale or work which was organized to reduce the debt of £2,00 on the new Sunday School buildings, was brought to a close on Saturday. At the re-opening ceremony in the afternoon the Rev. T. 1%1 Bier presided in the absence of Mr. J. B. Isaac, and referred to Mr. Isaac's keen interest in the work of the Mission. Mr. Isaac had, be said, sent a donation of two guineas, arid he had received a donation of £5 from a Frodsham gentleman. Everybody had worked hard to promote the success of the sale. He bad much pleasure in ask- ing Mr. Barlow to open it. Mr. Barlow said that in the future be hoped they would work with even still greater success at the mission. He would be very pleased to rpe what influence he could for its good.— The total proceeds of the three days' sales amounted to £ll7 15s. 2d.</p>
        <p>-.NNW , . . . -</p>
