        <p>L</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>St. Peter's Church.</head>
        <p>St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>The ant.ual choir services were held at this church on Sunday and were well attended. The preacher in the morning was the Vicar (the Rev. B. Percival) who took' for his text Exodus xv. 21, " Sing ye to the Lord, for He bath triumphed gloriously : his horse and his rider bath He thrown intO the sea." After commenting on the text and drawing some valuable lessons from it, the preacher pointed out that St. Peter's Church choir was entirely voluntary and that the members took veil,' great pains to prepare themselves to lead the music at the services. In the afternoon a musical service was condurted by the Rev. J. H. Garnett. Solos were well rendered by Miss Violet Brown and Mr. Dan Gittens as well as a quartette by members of the choir. The anthem "0 clap your-hands " was also sung by the choir. Mr. PadmOre was at the organ. The pulpit at the evening service nag occupied by the Rev. G. B Thurston, rector of Lymm, who took for his text. Joshua v. 13. The collections amounted to £4 Sc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Warrington Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Warrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>A special vestry meeting in connection with the Warrington Parish Church was hold in the Fairfield Schools on Thursday at noon. The Rector presided; and there were present :—Rev. A. J. Bratt, Messes. J. 0. Craik (Rector's warden), S. Taylor (people's warden), Watson, J. Wood (sidesmen) and E. A. White (vestry clerk). The meeting was" for the purpose of considering and approving plans for the erection of an oak screen in the west end of the nave of tbe Parish Church, Warrington, in commernorktion of a departed member of the congregation (MisfWillis) and for the erection of a brass tablet at the west end ofthe south aisle of the said Church in commemoration of alterations and additions made to the Caurch during past, years, and for passing resolutions atitilQiiaing applications for faculties for the same and for dealing with matters and things arising out of or in any way incidental thereto."—On the proposition of Mr. S. Taylor and seconded by Mr. Craik, the resolution that the plans be passed and faculties applied for, cras unanimously agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting of the Brotherhood was held. tin Sunday, there watt, a fair atteutdance of members. Mr: A. Healey presided and Mr. Lloyd Jones gave- a stirring address, his subject being :—" His face was set towards 'Jerusalem." tie dwelt upon the heroism of Christ, who journeyed to Gethsemane and Caltaryi knowing full well the result of his journey. Christ bad a purpose in life, and He knew He was here on earth to do the will of God, He wAs prepared for His trials, He perseveted in His work, He was heroic and full of truth, He was faithful unto 'death. The result was He gained the Crown of Life. The speaker referred to the confluence of Christ, when He said, 1., if Ibe lifted up wilt draw all men unto Me." He impressed upon all, to have a purpose in life, and to pursue it faithfully. through trouble, trial and difficulties, as it was only in this manner, that his hearers could ever hope to obtain the Crown a Life, laid up for all who will take up the et oss and follow Christ Jesus. A iota of sympathy was passed to the parents of %V. B.Wycherley who died rather suddenly on Friday last. Two new members received a heerty welcome, Mr. R. Atkinson and Air. MeEachern, also took part in the meeting. Mr. W:. Payton Music Bac. presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Warrington Church Society. Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>Warrington Church Society. Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Church Society was held in the newly 'acquired Church House, Bridge- street, on Tuesday evening, the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson (president), presiding over a fair attendance which included the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), the Revs. H. B. Firth, G. W. Dickenson. A. J, Brett and H. A. Lester (hon. sec.) end Mr. C. S. Burgess (bon. tress.) The PRESIDENT said he was thankful to say they had bad a very successful season. They bad two most striking lectures from Canon Joyce—lectures whicha possibly might have been a little difficult for seine members of the Society but which those who we're privileged to hear felt were lectures of nimarkable power and learning. The Lenten services for men and women were very well attended. They made a new departure this )ear in having Services for womhn every Friday in Lent, as well as those for men, and he thought they would all agree that they had an excellent list of preachers, and that the attendances were most encouraging. The annual conversazione was very successful. Archdeacon Madden delivering an excellent address. While the membership of the Society kept up, and possibly even increased a little, one could not but feel that it ought to be larger. If all those who were already members would act in a missionary spirit i- the different parishes, they might have a much larger roll of members. It was quite absurd to suppose that 300 or 400 people at all represented the number of those who took a keen interest in the welfare of the Church, and he believed it only needed to be brought home to people to increase the membership at least ten-fold. Canon Stevenson concluded by expressing great regret that Mr. C. S. Burgess, who had been a valued helper of the Society from its inception, was about to relinquish the hon. treasurership. and also voiced the Society's appreciation of the Rev. 11. A. Lester's; zealous work as hon. secretary. Mr. BURGESS said he was one of a few who founded the society seventeen years ago, and in 1898 he Mc- ceeded Mr. Robert Dobson as treasurer. Although he was now resigning that, office, he would continee..to take a deep interest in the society and do everything he could to promote the welfare not only of that society but of the whole Church in his native town. Mg. Burgess then presented the accounts which showed that the receipts amounted to £7O 2s. 3d. and included g2,g9. brought forward from last year; members' sithscdP- tions, £l4 us.; collections at men's services, £22 Os. 7d.; collections at women's services, £24 10s. 4d. After giving donations of £3 3s. to the Warrington TotirmfiTY, £1 is. ta the District Nursing Association, and the Church House, there was a balance in hand of £0 las. Id. He understood, the total membership was about 350. The Rev. H. A. LESTER moved the adoption oft-fre balance sheet and said that if he was able to work at all efficiently as secretary, it was due to the apprenticeship he served under the tuition of Mr; Burgess. Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD (auditor) seconded and the report was adopted. , •. The Rev. H. A. LESTEFt then presented the annual report, which stated that the work of the session had been most encouraging from every point of view. The Society's efforts had been attended with increased success and in some directions the year might be regarded as a record one. At the anniversary service the preacher was the Bishop of Liverpool, who took a keen Interest in the Society and was ever ready to give them big stimulating help. The other events of the ,1 session were reviewed and gratitude was expressed to the preachers at the special services for men and ' women. So encouraging was the attendance at the women's service that the committee felt justified in I including those services as part of their annual pro- gramme. Not only were the Lenten services self- supporting, hut after paying all expenses, a balance of £2O was left at the disposal of the committee, and it was devoted, among other things, to assist the Infirmary, Nursing Association and the new Church House. in conclusion, the report stated that the. committee realised that there was still a useful work for the Church Society, and while thanking God for ' the encouragement ot the past year they humbly prayed that He would so guide them in the future that the work of his Church might be strengthened and Christ's Kingdom extended. The RECTOR in moving the adoption of the report; said that while they had cause for thankfulness at 'the laree attendances at the special services it was not sufficient that people should come in crowds simply to hear eloquent addresses. 'They wanted to make,. them devout church people so that they would delight to come and worship God, independent of the sermons. He hoped exery incumbent would feel the immense import- ance of the Society's work and also do his utmost' to interest the laity in it.</p>
        <p>The Rev..G. W. DICKENSON seconded and the report was adopted. On the motion qf Mr. C. S. BURGESS, seconded by the Rev. H. B. FIRTH, the Rev. Callen Stevenson was re-elected President of the Society. Mr. Llttier was invited to undertake the "'hon. treasurership, and the Rev. H. A. Lester was re-elected hen. secretary. The following committee was elected:— Revs. Canon 'Willis NV. Braceramp, G. C. Briggs, A. .1. Bratt, E. C. E. Carleton, J. Collins, H. 11. Corsbie, .1. Davies, G. W. Dickenson, H. B. Firth, J. 11. G. Hollingworth, B. Percival, 11. V. Pigot, T. A. J. Ridpath, G. Vale Owen Colonel J. D. Buckton. Messrs. C. S. Burgess, J. desketh, J. Hill, 0. Lolthouse, J. H. Smethurst, T. S. Steel, 11. J. Westbrook, Luton, and J. J. Firth. The PRESIDENT referred in feeling terms to the death of Mr. Hamlet N. Houghton, who was alway ready at any iacrifice to himself to help on the work of the society. He proposed a vote of condolence with the widow. Mr. BURGESS, in seconding, said that he knew Mr. Houghton all his life. A truer and better friend he never had,and a truer and better churchman did not live. The resolution was supported by the Rev. H. A. Lester and carried in silence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>INFANTILE MOkTALITY.</head>
        <p>INFANTILE MOkTALITY.</p>
        <p>THE SUGGESTED CORONER FOR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse's Views.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of an inquest at Warrington on</p>
        <p>Wednesday, Mr. S. Brighouse, ()minty Coroner,-said he wished to mention a matter that \ Vtl S of moment to</p>
        <p>himself and to the borough of Warrington. At the last meeting of the Town Council, he saw from a report in the local newspapers, that one of the Councillors (Dr. Joseph) in discussing the medical officer's report, referred to the infantile mortality, and pointed out that the uncertified deaths were bver 4 per cent. " War- rington had an unenviable notoriety in the country for that class of death " said Dr. Joseph. "He would like to see that remedied and he had thought about it many times, but he did not see any cure for it except by having a local coroner. As long as they had a coroner living ata distance with a wide district in which he had to bold many inquests during the day, they could not expect anything better. It was it well-known thing all over the country amongst coroners and others associated with inquiries into deaths, that nothing deterred people who neglected their children—sometimes wilfully and criminally—so much as the holding of an inquest in every case Where was anything like a shadow of suspicion. That could never be done, so long as we had not a coroner of our own, and the time had come when a town of the size of Warrington should have a corOner."—Mr. Brighouse went on to say that he had every respect for the members of the Warrington Town Council, and ho felt certain that if Dr. Joseph had been acTiainted with the facts he would not have said what he had done. The Coroner then read the following corresponlence which had passed between himself and Dr. .1. Cote Hibbert, medical officer of health for Warrington :</p>
        <p>FROM DR. 'HEBERT TO MR. BRIGHOUSE.</p>
        <p>September 11th, 1908. Dear Sir,—My attention has been called to an article in "The Lancet" of September sth on the high per- centage of uncertified causes of .death in Warrington and suggesting the desirability of an explanation. Among the 76 great towns the proportion of uncertified deaths for the second quarter of this year does not exceed 1 per cent,whereas in Warrington the proportion reached 6.3 percent. The only reason I can ascertain for this high figure is the small percentage of inquests held on deaths uncertified by a doctor. As I have been instructed by the Health Committee to answer what they considered to be a reflection on the town,perhaps you will assist we by giving your views on the matter. I enclose an extract from the article above referred to. Yours faithfully.—(Signed) J. Coon: HIBBERT. •In answer to that letter Dr: Hibbert • received en intimation that Mr. Brighouse was on holiday and would reply when he returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FROM MR. BRI GHO USE TO DR. H [EBERT.</head>
        <p>FROM MR. BRI GHO USE TO DR. H [EBERT.</p>
        <p>, Dear Sir,—With ferther reference to your letter of the 11th ult., and to the extract from " The Lancet "of the sth tilt,, which was sent therewith. I draw your attention to the Coroner's Act 1887. By Section 3it is enacted "Where a coroner is informed that the dead body of a person is lying within his jurisdiction and there is a reasonable cause to suspect that such person has died either a violent or an unnatural death, or has died a sudden death of which the cause is unknown, the Coroner shall summon a jury to inquire into the death." In "Jervis on Coroners' in - the comments to this section, it says "upon receipt of information the .coroner must consider whether it is necessary to hold an inquest under the above sub-section. He has no absolute right to hold inquests in every case in which he chooses to do so. -It would be intolerable if he had power to intrude without adequate cause upon the privacy of any family in dis,ress." . The comment further states that "when the coroner receives the information of a sudden -death and when there is no medical certificate of death from any natural cause or other ground on which he can reasonably form an opinion as to the actual cause of death, it is his duty to hold an inquest." I observe that in the extract from "The Lancet" the writer refers to the proportion of uncertified causes or death during the second quarter of this year. From the Ist April to the 30th June of this year I received 18 reports of deaths from the Warrinc,rton Police in which, after carefully considering the circumstances set out in such reports' I did not consider it necessary to hold an inquest. I nl5 of those cases a medical officer conversant with the case had been seen by the police and had expressed an opinion as to the cause of death, which enabled 1111 to dispense with an inquest. It in the face of, those expressions of opinion by medical men I had held inquests, I should be intruding without adequate cause upon the privacy of a family in distress. In one of the remaining three cases, the lady sanitary inspector of the , Warrington Corporation, together with the midwife. , • were present together at the death of the child, and I ' sae; no reason to doubt the statement that the lady sanitary inspector conveyed to me in the police report. In the remaining two cases the police report conveyed sufficient information to me to enable me to come to the conclusion that the deaths were from natural causes and that an inquest would serve no useful purpose. If the writer in "The Lancet" pro- poses that in every case where the medical man is unable to certify to the cause of death there should be an inquest held by me, then it will follow that there must be a post-mortein examination, and he had better place himself in communication with the County Council of Lancashire, and then come and sit with me when I hold unnecessary inquests and hear what the relatives have to say in the matter. The Coroners' Act of 1887 has to be construed in a commonsense way, and I trust that I carry out the duties ettaching to my office so as to comply with the Act of Parliarnpnt and at the same time not to wound the feelings of those who are in sorrow and distreis.—YoUrs faithfully, S. BRIGHOUSE. ,</p>
        <p>October Bth, 1908.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—Referring further to your letter to. me and to my reply of yesterday the Registrar would class the 18, number of cases I referred to in my letter, as uncertified and "The Lancet" would argue accordingly. Now, I should just, like to consider whether the arguments in "'The Lancet," are worth anything or not. Take a typical ease. A member of your Town Council has the misfortune to have a wife who is in delicate bealth,but whose complaint has been. So closely diagnosed by the family physician that he knows there is no necessity to be in constant attendance upon her. Her death takes place and the medical man not having seen her for a period of six months does not feel justified in issuing a certificate under tho Births and Deatha Registration Act, seeing that he had not attended her in her last illness, but he is quite willing and as a matter of fact does give a letter addressed to myself to the effect that in his opinion 'there is not the slightest doubt that the deceased died from natural causes, and under these circumstances I dispense with an inquest and the death is registered as an uncertified death. What does "The Lancet" think I ought to do under these circumstances ? Intrude into the privacy and sorrew of the family; order a post- mortem and hold an inquest. Whatever "The Lancet" may say I am not goine to do it and if I so acted under the circumstances above described, I do not think I should stand well in the eves of your Town Council.— Yours- faithfully (Signed) S. BRIGHOUSE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>DR. If IFIDERT TO MR. S. BRIGHOUSF:.</head>
        <p>DR. If IFIDERT TO MR. S. BRIGHOUSF:.</p>
        <p>In reply to that letter Dr. Hibbert sent the following: 12th October, 1908. Dear Sir,—l beg to acknowledge with thanks your letters of the 7th and Bth inst., and will reply to the same in the course of a day or two.—Yours faithiully (signed) J. COOTE HIBBERT. On the 22nd October, 1908, Dr. Hibbert sent a further reply to Mr. Brighouse which read as follows :</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,--I regret my delay in sending you it further reply to your letters of the 7th and Bth inst. There can be nothing hut syMpathy with the desire of a coroner to avoid intruding, without cause, on the privacy of a family in distress, end certainly there does not seem to me need for a public inquiry into caeca where a doctor, although not in attendance on the termination of a long iliness which may have ended suddenly, can nevertheless express an opinion as to the cause of death. The main point, I think, which has exercised the minds of the Health Committee of Warrington is, why the percentage of uncertified deaths in this Borough should be so much higher than in most other districts. Is it to be inferred that the coroners elsewhere do unduly intrude, with art cause, on the privacy of families in distress and that in other districts inquests are held on cases where a doctor although not able to certify the cause of death can nevertheless express an informal opinion as to its cause? I must say that as a medical officer of health I have felt that the more frequent holding of inquests, especially in connec- tion with the deaths of infants, the causes of which are uncertified by a doctor, would tend to make careless parints more prompt in calling in a doctor to an ailing child, and more careful about the feeding and general welfare. To my mind the parents who through careless feeding, such as giving a child milk out of dirty bottles or allowing it to haven bit of anything that is going, bring about the child's death probably from convulsions are justly deserving of publics censure. it is only a somewhat less glaring offence than neglecting to feed the child at all.—Yours faithfully (signed) J. COOTE HIBBERT.</p>
        <p>Dr. }Ebbed received a reply from Mr. Brighouse stat;ng that he could give nothing further. Continuing the Coroner said a number of deaths had been reported to him where he had held no inquest. Where a medical man conversant with the case had been unable to give a certificate under the Births and Deaths Registration Act, hut had sent him a written communication stating what in his ' opinion wirs.the cause of death, and where there were no suspicious circumstances the dispensed with an inquest, the sam9. as any member of the jury would have done. He woula not stand well in the eyes of the people of South-West Lancashire if without just cause 'he intruded into the sorrows of families, and he for one was not going to do what was unnecessary and hold inquiries, the result of which would be of no practical good to anyone.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" The Empty Cradle."</p>
        <p>Melodrama still holds sway at the Royal Court Theatre and this week "The Empty Cradle" is pre- sented. An abundance of excitement and pathos provides good .food, for., the "gods," and the comic element, which is of a somewhat crude and boisterous</p>
        <p>description, is freely interspersed. Miss Nancy Battersby, as Ida Boswell, the gipsy, has a trying part, embodying much weeping and wailing, and she gives a powerful and touching portrayal. Mr. David Urquhart as Carnaby Bloodworth is a realistic stage villain, and Mr. J. M. Olivert earns great favour as , •</p>
        <p>the virtuous Kenneth Bloodworth. Tom Wells is deserving of praise for his bluff and natural present- ment of James Boswell, the gipsy. Miss Marion Mayne (Sarah Puddicombe) and Mr. Wallace Black (Hon. St. Clair Selby), are responsible for the humorous scenes and create great merriment. The scenic effects are admirable and the orchestra, under Mr. Norman</p>
        <p>Latham's conductorship, gives some well-chosen selections with good taste. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. •</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Herbert Skardon will present his specially selected company in a new and original play in four acts entitled "'The Derelict." This is the first time the play has been seen in Warrington and it should prove a good 'attraction. Mr. • Herbert Skardon, will appear as Reginald Grant, "The Derelict," and Miss Sidney Crowe will impersonate Lucie Legardie, known as Madame Dubois.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY'S NEXT OPERA.</head>
        <p>THE MUSICAL SOCIETY'S NEXT OPERA.</p>
        <p>" The Pirates of Penzance."</p>
        <p>Following up the brilliant successes of the last six years the Warrington Musical Society's Opera Committee has fixed upon Gilbert and Sullivan's "Pirates of Penzance" as their next venture. The opera will be given on the six nights commencing Monday. October 11,th and, on account of the difficulty experienced in finding sufficient seats of the right kind in the Royal Court Theatre, Parr Hall has again been secured for the place of performance. "The Pirates of Penzance" is pre-eminently a picturesque opera, and although the gaiety of out-of-door summer conditions pervades the whole of it, it is anything but of the boisterous kind which characterises ." Merrie England." Asd an interesting feature is that the ladies costumes at least, may, by a little stretching' of probabilities, be made quite modern. These, mingling with the quaint yet gay costumes of the Pirates and the sad and sombre uniforms of real present-day Police- men, duly helmeted and truncheoned, make up an essemble such as is calculated to delight the eye of the painter-artist. Curiously, all the men, with one exception, are either pirates or policemen, and, still more oddly, the ladies are, with one other exception, all the daughters of that Major General Stanley who figures so prominently in the piece. On this latter idea of the ladies all being sisters—so peculiarly Gilbertian in its whimsical originality—a great deal of the fun turns, for are not these numerous "blushing buds of evet blooming beauty" destined to become the wives of the renowned outlaws of the Cornish coast? The rehearsals of the work are now well-advanced and we understand that so well has the thing been memorised that the coach and dramatic instructor will enter upon his duties within the next fortnight. Mr. Crossley is, of course,again the musical director and con- ductor, with Mr. Payton as his assistant and accompanist, the coaching being also again entrusted to Mr. Foden Williams. By the courtesy of Mr. H. Bond, the opera honorary secretary, we are able to append a list of the ladies and gentlemen who have been: cast for the principal characters :</p>
        <p>THE PIRATES OF PENZANCE. • Major-General Stanley Mr. G. H. Smith The Pirate King Mr. Fred Fieldhouse Samuel . Mr. Wallace Ray Fredprip - • ' Mr. J. H. Draper Sergeant of Police . Mr. J. W. Bibby Mabel Miss Emily Taylor Edith . Miss Dorothy Tiltslove Kate Miss Annie Warren Isabel Miss Lily Boyle Ruth • Miss Jennie Armstrong</p>
        <p>Chorus of Major-General Stanley's Daughters, Pirates and Policemen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. PETER H. LAKE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. PETER H. LAKE.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death which took place with painful it.ddenness at 12.39. on Sunday morning,of Mr. Peter Holt Lake, 275, Knutsford-road, who was one of the be,-.4 known off-licence holders in the town. On Saturday about noon Mr. Lake visited his brother, Mr. PhilipiE. Lake, at the Blue Lion Inn, in the Market Place, and about five o'clock he went for a drive in the country. He w,iv seized with illness ' while in the trap, and was at once taken home, and despite the attention Of Dr. Joseph he passed away at the time stated from heart failure. Mr. Lake was born at Friars Green on June 25th, 1857, and was the son of the late Mr. Philip Lake. He served an apprenticeship as a joiner and builder under the late Alderman J. E. Wright, formerly Tayor of Warrington, and afterwards entered the, licensed trade as the licensee of the Talbot -Inn. In his younger days Mr. Lake was well known as a runner and cyclist and was at one time a mem- ber Of the Cycling Club. He remained at the Talbot Inn for ten years but retired, owing to ill-health, about ten years ago. .He took the off-licensed premises inKnutsford-road three years ago and conducted the business until his death. For seven years he acted as treasurer of the Warrington Licensed Victuallers' Association and worked most energetically for that organization. He was an expert at valuation and his services were frequently sought for that purpose by members of the trade. Much sympathy is felt with the widow and two children in their bereavement.</p>
        <p>Association :—Mr. J. Shillcock, Councillor Rogers, -no Messrs. R. Perdue, Thomas Cooper, and Arthur Brow" (solicitor). Wreaths were sent by the following Peterll/1; Lake, Mr. and Mrs. J. Houghton, Miss Lily Lahe, and Mrs. James Lake, Mr. and Mrs. George Lake, and Mrs. William Lake, Mr. and Mrs. Philip Lake, :Tr. and Mrs: John West, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Banks, "Ip, and Mrs. Grounds, Mr. and Mrs. Fox, Mr. and Derbyshire, Mr. and Mrs. William Domville, Mr. 84r. Mrs. T. Domville, Mr. and Mrs. F. D Domville, and Mrs. J. Astley, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Astley, and Mrs. Bradshaw, Mr. and Mrs. H. B. Astley, "Mtiggl,'l; Clare, and Ada," Mr. and Mrs. Holt and family, na William Lake, Mr. and Mrs. John Houghton. Mr' i%jr, J. Horton, Mr. W. Wardle, Mr. and Mrs. T. Cook, • and Mrs. Albert Mynekyme, Mr. and Mrs. Freeman, and Mrs. Tsrer, Messrs. Arthur Browne and Conir Mr. and Mrs. J. W. Bullen, Mr. W. Brierley, AL1.1,1, Shillcock, %Ir. T. Crompton, Mr. A. Linsey (P' - Warrington Off-Licence Holders' Associat ion. The arrangements for the funeral were carried out ble Messrs. R. Garnett and Sons, and the carriages Ile{ supplied by Mr. E. Connor, Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance at the funeral which took place at the Warrincton Cematery on Tuesday afternoon. The Rev. H. B. Firth (Vicar of St. James's Church) officiated.</p>
        <p>The Chief mourners were :—Mrs. P. H. Lake ;widow), Mr. and Mrs. J. Houghton (son-in-law and daughter) ; Miss Lily Lake (daughter) Mr. and Mrs. James Lake. Hunt's Cross (brother and sister-in-law) ; Mrs. William Lake, Liverpool (sister-in-law) ; Mr. and Mrs. Lake, Liverpool (brother and sister-in-law) ; Mr. and Mrs. P. E. Lake (brother and sister-in-law). Messrs. William and Philip Lake (nephews). Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Inks, Mr. and Mrs. West, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Grounds (Chester), Mr. and Mrs. Darbyshire, Mr. and Mrs. Fox, Mr. William Doraville, Mr. and Mrs. 'Fred Doraville, Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Domville, Mr. and Mrs. John Bradshaw, Mr. and Mrs. Astlev (brothers-in-law and sisters), Mr. and Nil's. T. Cook (Sankey), Mr. and Mrs' I'. Domville (Bootle), Mr. J. Jennings, Mr. and Mrs. Tyrer, Mr. J. Johnson (Stockton Heath), Mr. W. Harrison, Councillor Halliwell, Mr. Albert blynekyme, Mr. John Muskett, and Mr. James Waldron. The bearers were all members of the Off-licensed Holders' Association, viz. : Messrs. W. H. Mayers (president), Robert Smith (secretary), James Grounds, S. Fone, W. G. Davies, and E. Allen, and Messrs. W. Throssell and C. Harrison, members of the same associa- tion, were also present. The following represented the Licensed Victuallers'</p>
