      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>NO PEOPLE'S WARDEN. Curious Position at Burtonwood.</head>
        <p>NO PEOPLE'S WARDEN. Curious Position at Burtonwood.</p>
        <p>THE VICAR AND "THE TRADE."</p>
        <p>The current number of the •` Burtonwood D„r.ne Monthly "contains the following :—A curious pos"tir has been created with regard to the people's churc wardensbip. It is still fresh in all our memories tb: no Easter vestry was convened this spring, and -ec?D I%arden elected for the current year. lbe iocunibebe declined to summon the usual annual meeting, i 3 the form of " vestry" and did not not exhibit on principal door of the church,notice of the rlrchdeaeLr Court tit Wurrington. This he did, or did not,</p>
        <p>long and carefurdeliberation, for to reasons: ,icar (a) As a protest, much needed, against the elea 0 of anyone engaged in the demoralizing drink tragi° manufacturer or retailer.' (b) As affording opportunity for testing the suPP4efed 01 oblipatien to hold such meeting in a parish emu under the New Parishes Act (6 and 7 Viet, Ch. 37)' At the vestries of 1907 and 1908 the legal cha;B declined to preside and vacated the •chair whe°l•ol name of a local representative of the liquor trade, tied houses in the parish and district, presented b' self for election as people's -warden, in defiance ?0, obvious canonical disqualification as well Paw, opposition to the wish of the incumbent. This act'iog however, did not prove sidliciently drastic to tor.ad matters to the much desired crisis, hence. further 'fij more aggressive measures have become necessary, the more so as packed vestry meetig as follow„ be trade,' and what has bean et Vestry Meetings Wis'ort again. The dropping of the Vestry is. an all Kale challenge which must be taken up. The final 13,tico may be delayed a while, but it has to take pi!is eventuilly,—the sooner the better. If the battle 110 proves to be the Ecclesiastical or Civil Courts-I°' be so. dug The questions to be determined may be samme under three heads. (1) Is a person holding tied-house property'. Randit moreover, in an unfortunately d.unken district, mon"; qualified to fill the office of warden in that district •io, (2) Is the individual claiming the warden ihip, b°ol ing neither nomination by the incumbent nor vote be the people, really in possession of that office? legally claim to act? Is the annual vestry, if; ,technical sense' obligatory except in Old Parishes to (3) Has theArchneacon. or Chancellor, authorltloBl admit. to office without election or nomination fr year to yesr ? These questions, in our opinion, almit of ansOf the negative only. (1.) According to the old Canons of the Chareybe churchwarden is responsible for the moralities 000, , parish. He is not to be a Sunday Church chair nn.tf, went; he is to be an activeagent for sobriety, ruori'loos, and cleanness of living. These are the sort, of Ws t. the Churchwardens are ordered to tackle arid 'pres, If any, offend their brethren either by adult, w whoredom, inctst or drankenness, or by sear' , ribaldry, usury and any other uncleanness and iri,C.llo; ness of life, she churchwardens, or questmen aaci:o men in their next presentments to their ordinaries • 8, faithfully present rill and every of the said offenderot it is evident, therefore, if a churebwardpn does 'lv is required of him, seeks out the frequenters of o'oo houses, and all the drunken men and women parish, he will, if a drink man, assuredly ruin his tile( and be compelled to put up his shutters. Whether, dis the twain is he, being %hat he is, likely to elect? t`de? his duty as warden ? or to mrike money by his tta Why, of cote se, he shuts his eyes to the effects of dr ie and the demoralizing horrors which follow in its trt'of In sp:te of all representations from the incnmbent,lllo drink ridden parishes, in these parts such inelib,d and, we contend, disqualified candidates for eboyor office are duly admitted." sworein; without leties hindrance, after election by packed vestry' or, OS tie now happened, without any election at serious injury of Christian morals and spiritual The Diocesan officials. ultimately responsible for 5-to doings, probably believe these things ought not sates; be, but regard themselves as the victims of a 0.5' 01 which they cannot control, and from which there Is„Bis escape. If, Ii rwever, these ecclesiastics hid the coufred of their privsee convictions, It is very certain_ e 5</p>
        <p>such candidate would be refused admission to office•oo (II.) The situation is not only peculiar' it is terciv humorous. A man is admitted to' office at the 14er deacon's or Chancellor's Court who has been ne,", nominated nor elected as warden. A warden who 'etioo, a warden is an amusing anomaly. If, because of elscAo. in a preceding year a gentleman still holds the co,crttio, office of warden, and can claim to fulfil its duties 00 farce' of the Easter vestry is plainly demonstratedV the ceremony of admission becomes a delightful 110 comedy which would be more delightful still not for the charge of a dozen silver shillings, paid of church funds—a mere trifle of course. tfer. Enquirers are anxious to be informed how Wive! stand, dud whether. or not., the crucial point 100 ;01! settled. For the life of us we catnot say pre. how the mattor stands, and whether the claimset office is warden, quasi warden, or no warden at sil• must' bide A wee 'and tee. id (III.) To have omitted the Easter vestry is „el be " irregutar." The question, however, is not .913;i0 " irregularity" Nit of compulsion. Isa parish, s.ctith6., is not, an old parish, compelled to hold such 1.23e:f nod to elect officers pro forma? Can the hsnds ;00 unwilling incumbent be forced to sign the nbtl' Easter Vestry—say by writ of Mandamus? lor These are legitimate questionings, and ue" reasonable answers. tliPs</p>
        <p>Under the altered conditions of vestries, neire they discharge no parochial functions, and longer subsidized by the old church rate, the poscee, compel by writ, imprisonment, or any other Ilior would seem to he extremely doubtful, not to saY , thing mere strongly affirmative. Wet It may be said in reply, " there is no altertvi 0, these things have been always and must remain irld Established Church." In God's Name, then,,o none other name. let the Church be disestablisbeoivei soon as aBM can he passed through Parliament. 51 the Church of England her freedom that she her face steadfastly towards the Suti of Righteolls Righteousness it is which exalteth a nation 4" aPd diplomacy. It is worth while for some time to in the effort after Church purity, which is the tiilej thing, when footed up, as consistency of Christifillidic It will be evident to all unbiassed and tinpre.i;.otf, minds, whatever the legal aspect of the Easter, question may be, that the Church of England 'Br in need of men who, in high places, will give 11,5,, (Pi lead to the rank and file of the Clergy. 1:1:`:,10,`, strength of the Church is in her leaders. L'n'-ncl",, dignitat les are prepared to put the foot down, stter,.; show a firm front to all drink trafickers, gamblers, professional money lenders, and otivt,:tlF Church; necessarily, must be weak. If uncles'', and, as often happens, unworthy men are all°'site fill the office of Guardian of the Poor,or to oil! representatsve men On our Councils and other PsviV bodies, the Church, for her part, should be read,i, of' her veto 'when candidates for Church officerso,rlr),' proposed. Here, anyhow, the line' should be " piCI;; clearly, decisively, and once for all. Any Toroi, fee or Harry who may come along is not good 800109 Cnurch representation. Church honour and serosoci not to be bestowed upon the enemies of righteousness.</p>
        <p>Journalist Honoured. • At Runcorn, on Wednesday evening, . testimonial in the form .of a magnificent gold `t`o 314 bearing a suitable inscription, was handed Frank Fetmelly, a local journalist, who has the town to take up the editorship of tile de 014 rington Observer." The presentation was mai smoking concert by Mr. Thomas Cooke, 311(t, oge were many felicitous speeches. The recipi.o .40 is a former chairman if the Liverpool district Institute of Journalists, replied in pleasant te10:0/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE HERO OF THE ANTARCTIC.</p>
        <p>LIEUTENANT SHACKLETON'S ARRIVAL AT DOVER.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Shackleton, the Commandcr of the Nimrod Expedition, wbich reached !githin 111 miles of the South Pole, arrived in London on Monday, where he was received 11 a deputation of the Royal Geographical Society, headed by Major Darwin, Sir Clements -Markham, and Captain Scott of the Discovery. The intrepid explorer created a world's record by reaching the Southern Magnetic Pole, and he is to tell the story of his dash to t4e South on the lecture plattorm, and through the medium cf his great book. Our photo- traPh shows him, with his handbags, on the landing stage at Dover,</p>
        <p>11._ This year's open champion is generally known as a man of few words, but here vs „..7e him going through the trying ordeal of making a speech. Still it was an exceptional cs""heatsicT, even in the life of a golf champion. Taylor's latest success makes him open v2vPion for the fourth time, so that he now stands equal with James Braid and Harry 'don. In our photograph he is seen acknowledging the plaudits of the crowd on taking eceptance of the cup, gold medal, and cheque for £5O.</p>
        <p>EARTHQUAKE IN SOUTHERN FRANCE. tion ie South of France has been visited by an earthquake that has caused much destruce ketrand loss of life. The shock was felt over a distance of 100 miles, all along the townisterrt_anea n coast, from Montpellier to Nice. Little damage was done to the cities and t knit in the district around Aix-en-Provence, its effects were disastrous, whole 4" being destroyed.</p>
