      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>HOME DOCTOR BOOK FREE.</head>
        <p>HOME DOCTOR BOOK FREE.</p>
        <p>INlunyon's " Guide to Health," describes most diseases, giving their symptoms and the instruc- tions for treatment so plainly that one has simply to select the remedy, get a shilling bottle from the chemist and euro one's self. This Home Doctor Book can be obtained nest free by any- one (mentioning the Warrington" • Examiner ' ) addressing Munyon, GI, New Oxford Street, LoAdou, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. A GLIMPSE OF WARRINGTON IN 177 1</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAPTERS. A GLIMPSE OF WARRINGTON IN 177 1</p>
        <p>In this week's chapter we are giving our readers a glimpse of Warrington as it appeared to the eye of a visitor in 1772. In that year Thomas Pennant was setting opt upon a tour which took him through Scot- land—a journey which then imposed more upon the traveller than a journey through Siberia would upon the traveller of to-day— and passed through Warrington on his way northwards. Pennant was an accomplished traveller. He compassed the most difficult and remote parts of the kingdoin on horse- back and wrote some entertaining and useful volumes in description of them. He entered the glens of Wales and Scotland and earned thereby a reputation for uncommon courage. That we can easily take a week-end ticket to the same places is evidence of how far we have advanced in the matter of travel since the middle of the eighteenth century. Pennant describes the journey to Seotland by saying that he "had the hardihood to venture on a journey to the remotest part of North Britain," and by describing it in his book roused in the minds of many a desire to see this unexplored tract. We may introduce our author by saying that he lived at Downing near Holywell having been born there in 1726. He was famous as a naturalist and in 1765 spent some time with Buffon in Burgundy. His most notable books were" A History of Quadrupeds" and an extensive work on "Zoology." These long remained as classics, and Buffon himself drew upon them. Readers of White's " Selborne " will remember that many of the letters in that famous book were addressed to Thomas Pennant. The " Zoology " and some of the volumes of travel were printed by William Eyres at Warrington and the "Zoology " is justly regarded as one of the finest speci- mens of his handiwork: By his contact with William Eyres, Pennant would probably be familiar with and pay many visits to the town of which we quote his description. Most glad would many of us be to see, if only for a moment, the town that rose "as if in the midst" of a wood of poplar, and that counted salmon fishing and potato growing among its foremost industries. Pennant's book is written in the abrupt style of a journal. He approaches War- rington from Cheshire, a county which he describes as "the very seed-plot of gentility."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>"Continuing my way along a flat dull country, reach the banks of the Mersey, ride over a long causeway, having before me a perfect wood of lofty poplar, that speak the soil ; and Warrington as if in the midst of it. Enter Lancashire, after crossing a handsome stone bridge of four arches, which leads into the town, and was built by the first Earl of Derby, to accommodate Henry VII, then on his road on a visit to his lord- ship, probably to soothe the Earl after the ungrateful execution of his brother Sir William Stanley. It was at first a toll bridge but his lordship generously released the county from the tax, at a loss 'of as many marks as was equivalent to the portion of one of his daughters. "The priory of the hermit friars of Augustine founded before 1379, stood near the bridge but not a relique exists. The entrance into the town is unpromising, the streets long, narrow, ill-built, and crowded with carts and passengers; but farther on are airy, and of good width, but afford a. striking mixture of mean buildings and hand- some houses, as is the case with most trading towns that experience a sudden rise: not that this place lacks antiquity, for Leyland speaks of it as having a better market than Manchester upwards of 200 years ago. At that time the principal part of the town was near the Church, remote from the bridge, and was accessible only by a ford, which the con- veniency of a safer transit soon drew the buildings to that end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>"The Church has of late undergone much alteration, but two of the ancient Chapels still remain: one belonging to the Massies contains nothing but a small monu- ment, with a very amiable character of Francis Massey, Esq., Lord of the manours of Rixton and Glasbrook, last of the ancient family, which was extinct with him in 1748, but in an opposite Chapel with a magnificent tomb of Sir Thomas Iloteler and his lady in alabaster; their effigies lie at top, hand in hand, he in armour, she in a remarkable mitre-shaped cap; round the sides are various figures such as St. Christopher, St. George, and other super- stitious sculptures. The Botelers were of great antiquity in this place; the first took his name from being butler to Rannulph de Gernous or Meschines, Earl of Chester; and his posterity gained great possessions in this county, and one of them obtained the charters for markets and fairs at Warrington from his Prince Edward I. Tradition says that this Sir Thomas, then resident at Bewsey House, near this town, was, with his lady, murdered in the night by assassins, who crossed the moat in leathern boats to perpetrate their villainy. "Beneath an arch in the wall near this tomb is another, containing a figure in a long robe, muffled up to the chin; a ghostly figure: the head wrapped up in a sort of cap, and bound with a neat fillett.</p>
        <p>"Besides this church is a neat chapel of ease, lately rebuilt, and many places of worship for Presbyterians, Anabaptists, Quakers, Methodists and Roman Catholics; for in manufacturing places it often falls out that the common people happily have a dis 7 position to seek the Lord, but as unhappily disagree in the means of rendering themselves acceptable to him. •Here is a free school,very considerably endowed, and made very respectable by the merits of the present mastet. An academy has of late years been established in this town, with a view of giving an education to their youth on the plan of a university.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MANUFACTURES.</head>
        <p>MANUFACTURES.</p>
        <p>The manufactures of this place are very considerable; formerly a- great quantity of checks and coarse linens were made here, but of late years these have given way to that of Polldavies or sail cloth, now carried on with such spirit as to supply near one half of the navy of Great Britain. The late war gave a great rise to this branch, and a sudden improvement to the town.</p>
        <p>The making of pins is another considerable article of commerce; locks, hinges, cast-iron, and other branches of hardware, are fabri- cated here to a great amount; very large works for the refining of copper, are carried on near the town ; and the glass and sugar houses employ many hands. By means of all these advantages the town has been doubled within these twenty years; and is supposed to contain ht present about seven thousand inhabitants.</p>
        <p>THE RIVER AND SHIPPING.</p>
        <p>"The manufactures of this place are most</p>
        <p>. , readily conveyed down to Liverpool by means of the Mersey. The spring tides rise at the bridge to the height of nine feet, And vessels of seventy and eighty tons can lie at Bank Quay, the port. of the town; where warehouses, cranes, and other conveniences for shipping of goods are erected. I .must not omit that thirty or forty thousand bushels of potatoes are annually exported out of the rich land of the environs of Warrington, into the Mediterranean, at the medium price of 14d. per bushel. This is the root which honest Gerard, about two hundred and forty years ago, speaks of as a food, as also a meat for pleasure being either roasted in the embers or boiled and eaten with oile vinegar and pepper or dressed some other way by the hand of skilful cooke.'</p>
        <p>"The ialm- on fishery is very considerable, but the opportunity of sending them to London and other places at the beginning of the season keeps up the price to about Bd. per pound, which gradually sinks to 3d. or PA. to • the great aid of the poor manu- facturers. Smelts, or as they are called in all the North, sparlings, migrate in the Spring up this river in amazing shoals, and of a size superior to those of other parts, some having been taken that weighed .half .a pound. and measured thirteen inches. "In this river is found a small fish called the graining, in some respects resembling the dace, yet is a distinct and perhaps, new species, the usual length is seven inches and a half. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>i•'""%,.l'</head>
        <p>i•'""%,.l'</p>
        <p>ORFORD HALL</p>
        <p>"Make a visit to John Blackburne, Esq., at his seat of Orford,a mile from Warrington; dine and lie there. This gentleman from his earliest life, like another Evelyn, has made his garden the employ and amusement of his leisure hours; and been most successful in every part he has attempted, in fact, he has a universal knowledge in the culture of plants. He was the second in this king- dom that cultivated the Pine apple, has the best fruit and the best kitchen garden, his collection of hardy exotics is exceedingly numerous, and his collection of hot house plants is at least equal to that at Kew. He neglects no branch of botany;has the aquatic plants in their proper element, the rock plants on artificial rocks; and you may be betrayed into a bog by attempting to gather those of the morass. _ _</p>
        <p>_ "Miss Blackburn°, his daughter extends her researches still farther, and adds to her empire another kingdom; not content with the botanic, she causes North America to be explored for its animals, and has formed a Museum from the other side of the Atlantic, as pleasing as it is instructive." A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>OUR MISCELUNY.</head>
        <p>OUR MISCELUNY.</p>
        <p>The welcome which has been given to our "Warrington Chapters" is evidence that our readers take pleasure in learning some- thing of their town in its earlier days, and their appreciation encourages us to continue to draw upon the past. This week's " Chapter " coming as it does from the pen of an observant and scientific visitor has special interest and we venture to add in our Miscellany some obiter dicta of his upon the villages of Frodsham and Winwick. His instance of Cheshire longevity would be hard to beat, and it is interesting to know that in 1772 the villagers kept themselves in prac- tice as archers. Pennants lament over the "degenerate days" in which not even a Cheshire man could reach the furthest butt, leave one wondering how he would have regarded the present generation.</p>
        <p>* ii-* *</p>
        <p>"Cross a little brook and reach Frodesham ; a town of one long street, which, with its Castle, was allotted by Edward I. to David, brother of Lewelyn, last Prince of Wales, as a retainer in his double perfidy against his own blood, and his own country. Not a vestige is left of the Castle, which stood at the West end of the town; was latterly used as a house by the Savages, and was burnt down in 1650 when one of that name, an Earl Rivers lay dead in it. The church stands at a vast height above the town. In the register are these two remarkably instances of longevity: March the 13th, 1592, was buried, Thomas Hough, aged 141; and the very next day was com- mitted to earth, Randal Wall, aged 102. I observed also that in the winter of 1574, the pestilence reached this sequestered place, for four men are there recorded to have died of it. In early times that avenging angel spread destruction through all parts of the land; but her power is now ceased by the providential cessation of the natural causes that gave rise to that most dreadful of calamities.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>"Above the Church is Beacon hill, with a beautiful walk cut along its side. At the foot are four butts (arching still being 15racticed here) for an exercise in which the warriors of this county were of old eminent. The butts lie at four,eight,twelve and sixteen roods distance erom each other; but in these degenerate days the last exceeds the strength of the nery es of even a man of Cheshire to reach."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>"Pass through Winwick a small village remarkable for being the richest rectory in England; the living is worth £2,300 per annum; the Rector is Lord of the Manour, and has a glebe of £1,300 annual rent. In the wall of an old porch before the Rector of Winwick's house is safely lodged a bible, placed there by a zealous incumbent, who lived in the days of Oliver Cromwell,in order that at least one authentic work might be found, should the fanatics corrupt the text, and destroy all the orthodox copies."</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Now that the birds are in such evidence with us there is special interest in alittle book published by Messrs. Witherby on "How to attract and prolect wild birds." It is intended to inform bird-lovers, 9f the best ways in which the wild birds may be made welcome, and the best kinds of food and apparatus . for feeding. The making of nesting boxes should prove a good hobby, and where successful will secure the happy and cheery companionship of feathered friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RAILWAYS AND STREET PAVING.</head>
        <p>RAILWAYS AND STREET PAVING.</p>
        <p>A cue arising out of dispute between the Warrington Corporation and the Cheshire Lines Committee with regard to the paving of Elaine-street was down for hearing at the Borough Court on Tuesday morning, but Mr. Hallway, the deputy town clerk, informed the Bench that he was sorry to have to ask for another adjournment. This would be the last time he would do so. It was proposed to bear the case on Tuesday, June Nth. The case was adjourned to the date named.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ORY.</head>
        <p>ORY.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>• s, , &lt;,,, :,,...,,,,,, ~,,,.. 7.,,11,..t.,4,2,_-,4; ;•''' ') :',,',S.:* ‘'. 'f., • &gt; ks.t., &gt; • , 40.,,:f.,': ~.: e:.,yr,„,4‘, ~ Ai-4k ~ , ~:14,,,,;,,i,,,`•,,,,,, ;',... _40,, ,• , ''', 1 ‘;',,,:; ' •*, • 445.$ • ~. -'.-. '.. so- ~,,,..:,, ,s-4.,:•• • •••,`,,:,•::',' ' ,•:',:',:', .• , ::: 'F -404:44V; • '',t .. , . • , . • ' . , " , , • • , , . . , • , ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>COUNCILLOR THOMAS BARCON. •</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR THOMAS BARCON. •</p>
        <p>Like several of those whose life stories you have already published, my life has been spent within the borders of Warrington, and therefore of necessity I have little that is new to relate. I was born on June 20th, 1844, in New-road, or what is now Crossley- street. About two years later my parents removed to 54, School-brow, at which address they lived and died, having occupied the same house for about forty-five years. My father was a master fustian cutter and omployed about ten hands, the workshop being the top storey or garret which was over the whole building, as was the case with many other houses in the locality. This part of the town has undergone a great change since my early days. It was then known. as the North-East Ward. Standing at our front door and looking up the lane, the trees overhung the road, making a beautiful arch. Fruit trees in bloom could be seen all the way to what is now known as Dalton Bank. There was no property beyond the Grammar School. The country lay before you in living green, and from the " Sixpenny " fields you could see the Monu- ment, Orford Hall ,and Winwick Church. Now this once rural spot is a small town, and hundreds of dwellings have been erected. I can remember the time when corn grew on Cockhedge, a Mr. Welsby having a farm near the site now occupied by the weaving shed at Messrs. Armitage and Rigby's mill. When I was very young I was sent to a dame school held in a house next door to the "Manx Arms" and afterwards I attended the National Schools, Church-street. Of course, there were no school attendance officers then to see that we put in ten attendances per week, and what with taking care of other children, running errands, and doing what work I could in the home while father and mother were at work, you will see I had little or no education, and what little I possess I have gained since I grew up. I commenced to work when ten years of age in my father's workshop. There were no specified hours either to begin work or leave off. The Factory and Workshops Acts had not come into operation and sometimes we began work early and finished very late. I well remember how many times I left my occupation tired and weary, not troubling to go downstairs, but walking straight into the bedroom and going to sleep for the night. I remained at this business off and on until 1877 when a fire broke out in my own work- shop and did much damage. This dis- heartened me and I decided to adopt some other means of livelihood, so having disposed of my stock-in-trade, I forsook the business. Fortunately I obtained employ- Iment with Mr. W. D. Houghton, wire manu- facturer, at the old mill on Friars' Green and afterwards at the Sankey Wire Mills. I remained with the firm for about twenty years and shall ever be grateful to them for their kindness. I believe I possessed their confidence and it was certainly reciprocated on my own part. With the consent of my wife and family I.relinquished the position about ten years ago, and have not done any active work since. There are a few local events which stand out vividly in my memory. I can recollect the great fires at Allen's (Buttermarket- street) and Cockhedge Factories, and another which burnt down Messrs. Worrall's flour mills in Fennell-street. I saw troops pass along Church-street on their way to embark for the Crimean war, and I also wit- nessed the procession which took place on the declaration of peace after that terrible campaign. I believe the procession was the largest ever held in Warrington. It assembled in St. Elphin's Park and included representatives of all the local trades and friendly societies. When Warrington was something of a ship-building centre I saw several vessels launched at Bank Quay,and I can recollect going with my father to Dobbies' Locks to meet the packet which plied between Manchester and Warrington. In the days when great floods were of common occurrence I have seen the Howley district practically covered with water, which reached as far as the playground of the , National school. During the flood of 1866 the water came into many dwellings, rising as high as the ovens, and Mersey-street was impassable, all traffic having to be diverted along Buttermarket-street. As a youth I was accustomed to go to the baths in Church- street and on many occasions I have enjoyed bathing in the Mersey—then a beautifully clear stream—as far as Dobbies' Locks. I remember the days when the Post Office was in Market-street, and when the building lately occupied as an office by Messrs. W. D. Jeans and Son, solicitors,Winwick-street,was a gentleman's residence, commanding a fine prospect of open country. Another event which stands out in my recollections -is the opening of the Market Hall during the Mayoralty of Mr. Joseph Chrimes. Amongst the Warrington worthies whom I used to see passing to and fro almost daily when I lived on School Brow,were. Mr. Joseph Stubs, head of the well-known firm of file and tool manufacturers, Mr: Samuel Rigby, of Messrs, Armitage and Rigby, the Rev. Horace Powys, who preceded the Rev. W. Queckett as Rector of Warrington, and Mr. Fitchett Marsh, the first Town Clerk of Warrington. As your readers know, I am a Liberal in politics. I am proud to say I am the son of a Liberal, but I have always supported the progressive cause from conviction. My first vote was given in 1868 for the late Mr. Peter Rylands. Those were indeed stirring times. I have not suffered much for my political views, but even if I had been called upon to to do so, the cause is a good one and worthy of some sacrifice. My mother had strong Liberal opinions. I have heard her say that when I was a little fellow a neighbour knocked me down saying" Go whoam to thi' Whig of a mother." On another occasion, when I had become old enough to take an active interest in politics myself, a foreman of</p>
        <p>strong Conservative leanings was so annoyed at the defeat of his party that he said" If I had my way, I'd sack thee "—but he had not his own way. Still we were always good friends, and I stood firm to my convictions. In 1902 a vacancy occurred in Whitecross Ward through the death of Mr. G. Plumpton. I was nominated as the candidate and returned unopposed. I have been twice re-elected without a contest, and hope I have not betrayed the trust reposed in me by the electors.</p>
        <p>In 1862 I began to attend Brick-street Sunday School and Friars' Green (Indepen- dent Methodist) Chapel—not the present building but the old one which occupied premises over some cottages. There we wrote in copy books for half an hour each Sunday afternoon and many obtained their education in that way. It was about this time I met and married Elizabeth Johnson, who, I may mention, shares my views on religion and politics. Our wedding took place at St. Paul's Church on April 9th, 1866 and we have had few troubles and many joys. Church work has occupied a great deal of my attention and in addition to having held the office of teacher, superintendent of the Sunday school, and president of the Church, I have been a preacher in the circuit for many years. I have travelled many miles in order to fulfil my preaching appoint- ments and I have 'occupied most of the Nonconformist pulpits in and about the "good old town," as well as occasionally conducting services at the Workhouse. One of my treasured possessions is a silver trowel and mallet which were given to me when I laid a memorial stone at one of our churches. Two years ago the teachers and friends pre- sented me with a framed enlarged photo- graph of myself and an address in which they recorded appreciation of my long ser- vices both in the the school and church. Needless to say, my family and myself highly prize this kindly recognition. My object through life has been to leave the world better than I found it, believing that</p>
        <p>Ile liveth long who liveth well All other lives are short and vain</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON PERMANENT BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PERMANENT BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The thirty-ninth annual meeting of the Warrington Workingmen's Permanent Benefit Building Society was held on Mondtty evening in Golborne-strest Baptist schoolroom. There was a good attendance, which included Messrs. James Harding, R. Isherwood, J. Baxter, W. H. Wallington, Harry Hope, W. W. Leigh, E. Massey, S. Lawton, W. Charnock, F. E. Penketh, F. J. Owen. jun., J. H. R. Pontefract and Peter Dalton (sec ret axy</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. WALLINGTONt on being elected unani- mously to the chair, said he was pleased to have the honour of presiding at that meeting. It would be a meeting, he thought, which would be satisfaceory to all concerned.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY then read the annual report for the year enden 30th April, 1909, which reads as follows : During the year the sum of £1,820 ss. 2d. has been received as subscriptions ; £2,545 98. 3d. for repayments; and £8 ss. fid. entrance fees, fines, 6:c.; and banker's interest, £l4 178. 3d., making the total receipts £4,388 17s. 4d.; the working expenses being .£72 2s. 9d. The withdrawals amount to £1,359 138. lid. The sum of £2,450 has been advanced to members. There are 250 subscribers, holding 342 i shares; and 47 borrowers, holding 2311 shares; making 297 members, holding 573 shares. The bonuses this year amount to £428 15s. on the amount lent, of which sum £7B f,s. is divided as profit, ano £350 7s. carried to the prospective Interest account. The profits for the year, including £27 158.6 d. from the last account, after deducting working expenses, amount to £455 ss. ld., out of which the managers recommend a dividend of 4 per cent., which will take £412 10s. 9d., leaving a balance of £42 148.4 d., £25 of which they direct to be placed to credit of the contingent fund, and the balance, .£l7 14s. 4d., carried forward to next year's account. The total amount owing to the society on mortgages is £12,003 14s. 4d. The valuation of the property upon which the advances have been made is £24,705. The whole of the managers retire. ' Messrs. Harding, Isherwood, Hope, Baxter, W. H. Wallington and Wm. Charnock are eligible, and offer thea,selves for re-election. Six additional managers will be taken from the register in rotation. The CHAIRMAN said he thought they might consider the year which bad gone by a very satisfactory one indeed; in fact, he as one of the managers felt honoured to have to ask them to pass a balance sheet like tbat. The conditions during the past year bad not been satisfactory in many businesses, and they had felt it in the same ratio. Ihe subscriptions had decreased, and also the repayments. The entrance fees and fines had increased. the total receipts had decreased, and tbe advances had decreased. The withdrawals had diminished, and he thought that was a very good sign. Although they had had a bad year in which one would have expected a lot of withdrawals, their withdrawals had actually been less this year by £573 Gs. The whole report was one which they could as managers with a good heart ask them to accept and pass. He did not think they needed to be ashamed of their work; in fact, he as one of them was not ashamed of the iVork which bad been done during the past year. They might all con- gratulate themselves on the result of the year, which had been bad in all parts of the country, and in all phases of trade. They were able to declare a dividend 9f 4 per cent. notwithstanding all. They bad also increased the contingent fund by £25. There might be some objection to that, and he should like to forstall any objection that might be made. He thought the managers were wise in keeping the contingent fund PS high as they could. He did not think it would be wise to reduce it to £75 so as to be able to pay a dividend of 44 per cent. He thought the course for the members to adopt was to accept the report as it stood and rely upon the fact that they had £1,416 3s. 3d. as prospective interest and also had in addition to that a contingent fund of £125.</p>
        <p>• Mr. fSHERWOOD moved that they accept the balance sbeet and the report. Mr. PENKETH seconded and then Mr. R. GREELEY moved an amendment to the effect that the £42148.4d., which included the money paid to the contingent fund and the balance carried forward, be carried to next year's account. He admitted that it was a good balance sheet. One fact bad been omitted, and that was that during the year they had had an increase of eighteen members. Where was the use of creating a contingent fund? He could not see any necessity for the creating of that fund or building it up. There was no reconder to the .amendment and the report was carried.</p>
        <p>bn the proposition of Mr. Greeley seconded by Mr. HARRY HOPE a dividend of 4 per cent. was agreed to. Mr. GREELEY moved and Mr. PARKIN seconded that the retiring managers be re-elected. This vtagcarried unanimously. Mr. William Bodle jun., and Mr. Robert Lee were re-appointed surveyors on the proposition of Mr. EDWARD WALLINGTON, seconded by Mr. GREELEY. Mr. A. W. Howard and Mr. George Armstrong were appointed auditors.</p>
        <p>"Mr. E. WALLINGTON proposed and Mr. GREELEY seconded that the managers he paid le. per meeting for their attendance. This was unanimously carried. The Chairman was accorded a hearty vote of thanks on the proposition of Mr. ISHERWOOD seconded by MT. GREELEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>, 5,000 PEOPLE IN BRIDGE-STREET ON SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>, 5,000 PEOPLE IN BRIDGE-STREET ON SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>A ORRAT RUSH FOR DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>It is estimated that close on five ihoesand people called on Saturday at Boots Cash Chemists in Bridge- street, Warrington, to obtain free boxes of Dr. Cassell's Tablets. This remed% is the one which has been creating such a stir in medical and lay circles on account of its remarkable efficacy in curing some of the worst eases of bodily weakness, nerve troubles, anemia, debility, loss of flesh, children's weakness, spinal anti nerve paralysis, and stomach and kidney troubles. Dr. Cassell's Tablets are strongly recommended by such authorities as Dr. Ramsey Colles, J.P., L.L.D., of London, and Dr. Forshaw, D.Sc., of Bradford, and it is questionable whether ever a proprietary remedy before received such remarkable endorsement. Dr. Cassell's Tablets can be obtained for iOid., Is. ild.,and 2s. 9d, of all chemists ; and as a safe and ,pertain cure for all diseases arising from physical and nervous exhaustion they stand unchallenged. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TUFTED PANSY ROYAL BLUE.</head>
        <p>TUFTED PANSY ROYAL BLUE.</p>
        <p>A really good blue Tufted Pansy has been wanted for years, and not until the present season bevel been 4blo to 'obtain a variety that produces blossoms of the colour above mentioned. True Blue is a very old Tufted Pansy that many have planted for its beautiful colour in the border, but its flowers are very poor, when com- pared with those of the Tufted Pansies we have to-day. The variety under notice should supersede True Blue for colour, and for quality also.— D." in" Oardening Illustrated."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>a, r9DNq I. 1 _ ! 0, 1? ftc t, ‘• ; , – _</head>
        <p>a, r9DNq I. 1 _ ! 0, 1? ftc t, ‘• ; , - _</p>
        <p>[Mr. James Fogg, of Serjeant's Inn, receives an unexpected answer to an expectant let- ter.)</p>
        <p>- Château do Bougival, near Paris. December 2nd, 1905. dames Fogg, Esq. Sir,—ln answer to your esteemed letter de- manding to know how far the conditions of my revered father's will have been observed by me. I beg to state: 1. That they have been wholly fulfilled. 2. That an agreement was signed yesterday between the editor of the "Daily Bulletin" and myself, in which I am engaged by him as his Paris correspondent for the term of one year, at a salary of seven hundred pounds. A copy of this agreement. I beg to enclose for your inspection, and remain, Sir, yours faithfully, HENRY GASTONARD.</p>
        <p>LOO it-.4...it j .1•:••rmilt . : ,;/..,.• i • ..t ;',t,. iva 'lt• ..,V 11 Litt-. •-•'• -:-1---- 1*::,:7.' vi:Pll, ~ //-fi!ik, i: it.klo• .. \,,, . i, 4_41,,i. B. .....„. ~ - _..,._, 4,1 1 6.v(_-_, - - • bh, .A ?a-a, , - ' ' - ••' arm /1....• 0... irptt, . _ • -</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XLIV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CHAPTER XLV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLV.</p>
        <p>[Mr. James Fogg, of Serjeant's Inn, passes on the unpleasant news to the Reverend Arthur Warrington, of Beldon, Suffolk.] 3, Serjeant's Inn, London, E.C. December 4th, 1905. The Reverend Arthur Warrington, M.A. Reverend and dear Sir,—l have this day - received the enclosed letter from Mr. Gas- tonard. Its claims, I fear, are incontestable.</p>
        <p>The Will of the late Henry Gastonard, of Bordeaux and London, provided that the bulk of his considerable fortune should pass from the possessien of his only son in tne case that the young man was not earning five hundred pounds a year by his own efforts at. the age of twenty-fire years. Such a condition. I regret to say, has now. been fulfilled. • The Agreement between. Henry Gastonard and the editor of the, "Daily Bulletin" is %ot a document I should advise you to contest. No court would ques- tion its bona fides; nor would any Chancery judge be willing to interpret. the Will in any strict sense.</p>
        <p>Such are the facts. Believe me, dear Sir.. that I. deplore then?, and am, your faithful'.</p>
        <p>servant,</p>
        <p>JAMES FOGG.</p>
        <p>My charges in the matter of thin business,. £l4 6s. Bd., are detailed in the schedule have the honour to enclose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CHAPTER XLVI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLVI.</p>
        <p>[The Reverend Arthur Warrington receiv-tre *te news and makes some complaint of it.l The Red Farm, Beldon; Suffolk, December sth, 1903.- .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>James Fogg, En.</head>
        <p>James Fogg, En.</p>
        <p>Dear 14—r. Fogg,—God's will be done— though this, indeed, is dreadful news. That the cup should be dashed from my lips at the, last moment! I can hardly believe my eyes while I read.</p>
        <p>Of course, this alleged contract is nothin hut a. trick. Would anyone pay seven hundred pounds a year to a youth who . has. lived a worthless life? Why, even I cannot. earn a hundred,pounds a year by my writings,. and you -know how voluminous • they have, been.</p>
        <p>I must tell you plainly that if I cannot de-. rive some benefit under this Will, my affairs: will be in a very bad way. The debts incurred by my wife at the time of that miserable and. insensate ,pageant at Lowestoft still press; heavily upon Me and interfere with my work: among the poor. I owe some two hundred pounds besides, chiefly to my publishers, who brought out my last volumes upon. Athanasius and St. John Chrysostom. They- have the inde:icacy to say that there is no money in the Apostles. I must pay their ac- count immediately, and also your own</p>
        <p>charger. Would it he possible, do you think, to-find some wealthy person who would lend me money upon note of hand alone? I should. not object to a reasonable rate df interest— though perhaps they would lend it to the Lord at a lower 'figure? How can a clergy- man do his duty when harassed by debt? This is a dreadful misfortune, and I cannot contemp;ate it with equanimity. What will Henry do with his vast fortune? Oh, it is deplorable to think that it may be spent chiefly upon this dancing girl, who. I have reason to believe, is the natural daugh- ter of a dissolute French nobleman. My own position is rendered more difficult by the, fact that I have already entered into considerable engagements upon the supposition that; nothing could deprive me of the money. And_ now Henry so far forgets his birth and tradi- tion as to write for a common newspaper. Will you please to let me know what I shall have to pay by way of interest for a. loan, say of five hundred pounds? Would fifteen per cent. satisfy the lender? I have some hopes of being chosen for one of the new canonries at Bury St. Edmunds, which is about to become a cathedral city. This would mean eight hundred pounds a year and a house, I suppose. I could pay off the money in three years. Kindly write to me without delay.—Yours truly, Argil UR WARRINGTON P.s.—Should you see Mrs. Warrington, I beg you will not mention to her the fact that, I am trying to borrow money. My loss, I regret to say, causes her some amusement. She is going to London this week to support, the meeting of the Society for the Suppres- sion of the Human Emotions,--but I have not the heart to accompany her. She would have gone to the Charing Cross, Hotel, but she hears by accident that a very objection- able Irishman' who is her particular .aver- sion, happens to be staying there; so she will be at the Grand. She may call upon you. Be discreet, I pray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CHAPTER XLVII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLVII.</p>
        <p>[Paddy O'Connell informs his sister Clara that he is detained in London upon busi- ness of some •importance.l The Grand Hotel, Charing Cross. December 13th, 1905.</p>
        <p>Dear Clara,—l arrived here on Saturday night from Paris, but I didn't come on to you for reasons which the great Deep can speak of, and especially that part of it which lies between Dover and Calais. Faith, every soul on board but me was sick; and nothing but the natural delicacy of my feelings pre-. vented that, same calamity overtaking me. I propose now to rest a few days in Lon- don. There are my stockbrokers to see, and some of Harry's business to be attended to. You have heard by my letters and the news- papers all that has been going on in France, so I do no more than to tell you that Harry is hard at work already ,as a newspaper cor- respondent, and a mighty pretty one at that. If he doesn't succeed as a newspaper man, it will be because he tells the truth, which is not what some of 'em in Fleet-street want* at all, as .I found out when I called John Ferguson, of the "Daily Herald," a black- hearted liar. But Harry will do well, I hope; and he's saved his fortune for certain. Apropos of this, 'tis the oddest thing in the - world, but I met Mrs. Warrington in this very hotel, and had some talk wi,th her. She is a sensible little body, and doesn't grudge Harry his fortune at all. "After all," says she, ''twas his father's money and not ours." But I fear the parson man, her husband, is in a bad way about it and swearing like the devil. It appears that he's been counting his chickens before ti,ey were hatched, and a pretty brood of blue envelopes have come out of the nest. I shall have to be writing to Ittirry to do something for him. 'Tie not cricket at 411 to be expect-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>lINC. PEAvfI3ERTON</head>
        <p>lINC. PEAvfI3ERTON</p>
        <p>Author of "The iron Pirate " "The</p>
        <p>Hundred Days," "A Puritan's Wife,"</p>
        <p>etc., ctc.</p>
        <p>lug a fool to pay the whole price of his folly, for God knows, we are no wiser than we were born, and that's Rot very wise at all where some of us are concerned.</p>
        <p>I shall stay a few days in London, though I expect to be very busy all the time. To- night I may go to the theatre by way of re- laxation; you wouldn't have me kill myself with hard work, though anxious I am to get home to Ireland and the riding. _</p>
        <p>I will just say that Harry- writes to me almost every day, and is full of his happi- ness. My Triend, the Princess 'Helene, has gone to Corfu on the Marquis's yacht, an the Duehesse de Bourg with her. Hairy and Mimi could have the, great château te them- selves if they chose; but, will you believe it, they have gone up to the little house on the Butte, just to pass a day or two, they say, and to see what the old life was like. "[would be odd to hear that the li.ttle Grenze cared anything about that now, but I sup- pose Harry knows what he is doing, and he'll have plenty of murders to send to the news- papers. Do not be at the pains to write to me, Clara. I shall be coming home as-soon as all these lawyer men have finished with me. _</p>
        <p>Meanwhile, I am, a 3 ever, your affec- tionate brother, PADDY.</p>
        <p>There was a man stopped me by Charing Cross Station yesterday, and asked me if could direct him to the house of the Arch- deacon of Middlesex. .I had a little talk with him, and took him afterwards to my hotel. 'Twas a most wonderful story he had to tell me. His cousin, it appears, is a prisoner in Spain, and knows of a buried treasure near Cadiz. About fifty pounds will buy• the man's liberty, and he's willing to share the trea- sure, which dates back from Columbus' day, and is mostly in moidores, though all sound gold, with any man that will find him the money. • I'm of the mind to join them. 'Twould be something to .come home to you, Clira, with a ship full of guineas. And all for a paltry fifty pounds! I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>CHAPTER XLVIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLVIII.</p>
        <p>[ln which Henry Gastonard hears of Jules Farman again, and of the criminal known as Bedotte the valet.] . 4 (bis), Rue du Quatre Sentembre,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Paris</head>
        <p>Paris</p>
        <p>February 21st, 1906. Monsieur,—l have the honour to report as follows :--</p>
        <p>The criminal Henry Bedotte Sanvalier was executed this morning on the public ground between the prisons of La Roquette and Les ieunes Detenues.</p>
        <p>I was privileged to visit the pr:soner at five o'clock, and spent some minutes alone with him. He was entirely unrepentant, and heard with much satisfaction that his com- rade, Gondre, the showman, had received a sentence of ten years' forced labour. I have, monsieur, seen many men die before the gates of La Roquette, but no man mbre con-. temptuous of death or its sequel. Of this, however, you will read in the newspapers. My questions to Bedotte were few but im- portant. I had to discover (a) if Madame d'Alencon had played any conspicuous part in this affair. and (b) under what circum- stances the abduction of Madame Gastonard from the Convent of Orleans was safely accomplished.- To both of these 1 had satis- factory answers. _</p>
        <p>There was another actor in this drama, monsieur, but he is dead. I refer to the hus- band of the woman Marie, a man who had been in the service of the Marquis de Saint Faur, who planned the abduction of the child, and who hid her successfully from the police during the active weeks of the pursuit. Madame. your wife, is sr4wking but in gene- ral terms when she tells us that she has no clear memory of this criminal—nor might we expect it. But it is clear that no convent would be so conducted that children could pass its gates when they chose; and that if any such event had occurred, the police of Orleans would not have failed in their duty. No, monsieur, Madame Gastonard was cleverly kidnapped from the house during the hour of the children's recreation. She was carried to the poorer quarter of Orleans, and there hidden for many weeks. In the interval an unforeseen ,event occurred : the husband— the man who knew the truth—was arrested. The old woman had but a vague notion of ; she carried the child away with her upon a gipsy's journey, fell in with the showmen Gondre, and finally sold her prize to that hOnest fellow Cassadore. And now, mon- sieur, the sequel becomes clearer. When you sent me to make enquiries at Orleans as to the truth of certain stories you had heard concerning the infancy of Madame Gastonard, it chanced that another was simi- larly occupied for a purpose which will be self-evident. She was Madame Lea d'Alen- con, then posing in Paris as your friend. Her acquaintance with Count d!Antoine was slight, but she used it as a clever woman could to extort from him some confession of the truth. Possibly, although this could not be proved in pa court of law, she herself set counter agencies to work. She may have be- trayed the secret to the man Gondr6 ; if not to him, then to the old. woman Marie. The criminals themselves appear, upon this, to have joined forces and arrived at a deter- Mination to -work together. They did not know where Madame Gastonard was to be found in London; but they followed the Count to her house, and there murdered him —as Bedotte declares—because he threatened them with the police. Their object was to blackmail the Marquis de Saint Faur and the Princess Helene. But most fortunately this 1% as prevented. The public may have guessed the truth; but many truths are guessed by the public, Monsieur, and remain guesses to the end.</p>
        <p>Monsieur, the world forgets these things very quickly, and it will not be less willing to blot out this page. If it should be turned once more, you will have Madame d'Alencon to thank. But I shall not fail to frighten her, and fear is the only weapon which will silence a woman's tongue. In this you may count upon me, not only in your own interests but in those of my esteemed patron., the Mar- quis de Saint Faur... - I will add, monsieur, • that a cast crowd assembled to see Sarivalier die, and that his death was accompanied by that brutal cir- cumstance which unhappily is still favoured by our French law: the man would not re- ceive the priest Abbe Falier nor permit any Consolations but those. of a glass of brandy and of a cigarette. He smoked when they had bound him, and-the cigarette was still between his lips when the head fell. Such was the end of a very evil scoundrel who mur- dered a noble gentleman and deserved a fate less kind. , I will add that at the moment when the knife descended and struck the head.. from the body, I heard a shout from the crowd, and perceiled there the notorious Jean-le- Mont. This _ man's release WAS necessary, but I regret it. He has returned to the old Lapin Agil on the Butte, where I doubt not that I shall Soon have the pleasure of arrest- ing him upon. another account. Be assured, monsieur, of my ' esteem, and permit me to remain, Your faithful servant, JULES HENRY FARMAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>CHAPTER XLIX</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XLIX</p>
        <p>[Madame Lea d'Alencon has really nothing to say; but she says it charmingly as ever.] The Hotel Metropole, Monte Carlos February `4lsth, 1306.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>"What would happen to people if they were to fall in love with</head>
        <p>"What would happen to people if they were to fall in love with</p>
        <p>their husbands?"</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Gastonard,--I compliment you upon your self-denial. To refuse an invita- tion to dinner with .your old friend ! And to forbid me the opportunity of complimenting Madame!</p>
        <p>Surely it is not true; cher Monsieur Henry, that Madame Gastonard is about to return to the circus? I positively refus2 to believe it. But the has cud) a mechanic tongue. Tell inc that it is not so. Indeed. the report—and your unkindnes.:---move, Inc to some envy. • Must I tame the lions—at my age? Helas, the spangles do not suit me 4 It was good of you to come here, for now we may talk. How proud you must be of Madame's success. I have even heard it said that she is learning to speak grammati- cally. How clever of her when one remem- bers the lions and the hoop! lam sure you are very, very proud! My dear husband has returned from Tunis. We were laughing together yesterday at your misfortune but very kindly of Course. How very foolish of you to amuse Paris as you did. And all for a mere oman. Ah well, as the proverb goes—il n'y a que le matin en toutes choses. Your friend, LEA. D'ALENCON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>CHAPTER L</head>
        <p>CHAPTER L</p>
        <p>[Mimi the Simpleton writes a brief note to Madame the Princess Helene of Ilidze and we translate it.( The Riviera *Palace Hotel, Monte Carlo, March 3rd, 1906.</p>
        <p>Dearent Mother,—We return to Paris to- day, and Harry says that I may write to you. I have wished to write so many times, but he has forbidden me. We are to go to Paris and then to London to see that great big Monsieur Paddy, who loves me. Dearest mother, 'there has never been any- one in my life to whom I could speak as I have spoken to you. All the secrets have been locked in MT own heart, but you have shared them. When I was in Paris at the cafés and the circus, I knew that someone would come some day and open the secret place of my love. You only in all the world can do so. Many many years I carried my hope with me and never dared to speak of if,. How much, dearest mother, is a woman alone among men. We bear our burdens and none knows of them.</p>
        <p>Will you write to me, dearest, to the chateau; and I will have the letters sent to London, if we go there. I know that I may not see you often but I will count the days until I see you again. Our jewels do not shine less brightly because we do not look at them, and I know that you think of me as your child. So often have I said she is my mother, and that is the sweetest word. The years were long, dearest, until I learned it; but 1 would live them all again if the end might be like this.</p>
        <p>Harry writes so well that they wish him to go to England. He has promised to go there to see his cousin, Madame Martha, who is in trouble. She has lost all her money and Harry is going to give her some, for her hus- band the clergyman cannot preach without money, and that would make the people un- happy, she says.. Afterwards we wish to buy the, Château of Marcey-le-Rideau end to go there for all our holidays. Harry says that he does not wish to write any more for the newspapers. It makes him so angry when they leave his work out and put someone else's in. And it is never so good as Harry's. Dearest mother, will you come .to Marcey- le-Rideau and let me call you mother there? It will not be my home if you do not.</p>
        <p>Al! the lonely, lonely years forgotten! Dearest, will you say that they must never return? Your loving daughter,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CHAPTER LI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER LI.</p>
        <p>[Mimi and Harry Trite to Paddy and advise him of their approaching visit to Ireland.] On board the Lapin Agil, Lisbon, April 2nd, 1906.</p>
        <p>Dear Paddy,—You have been too long with- out a letter, so now we are both writing to you.</p>
        <p>I am holding the pen and Harry is spelling the English words. That is why there are so many blots. Dear Mr. Paddy, we cannot go back to Paris. We went there once and lived for three days at the old Maison du bon Tabac. It was so beastly; Harry says you (meaning me) must be very young to live where the people have *nothing to do but to say what they will do some day. So we have grown up and do not do it.</p>
        <p>We are now in Lisbon. where, Harry says, they used to have an earthquake. It has not swallowed up all the people, but there are a great many left. Harry says the gins are very pretty, but I do not think _so. It is better to be on a yacht when she is standing still trian when ,she is walking. I do not like the sea, but Harry says it is good tor me.</p>
        <p>Please, Mr. Paddy, may we come to Ireland to see you? It was very naughty of you to quarrel with cousin Martha because :she said you could not play OM And to go back to Ireland in a huff. Of course, we shall say you play golf very wea when we are at your</p>
        <p>*Harry has bought the château of Marcey- le-Rideau, and we are to descend there in the summer. One room is to be called Mr. Paddy's room. Harry says it shall be very big. so,that you can beat the floor with the golZ clubs. He is building a little- house in the grounds which we have nameil the Maison du bop Tabac. You may smoke there, Mr. Paddy, when you are, angry .and cannot get out of the bunker. We met Madame ,Lea, at Monte Carlo, and I did not bow to her. She has grown so ugly and has fallen in love with her husband. Ah, fries enfants! What would happen to people if they were to. fall in love with their husbands!</p>
        <p>At Cintra, which is a very beautiful moun- tain near here, we met an American lady, who, Harry says, is an heiress. You should come out and marry her. Perhaps she would fall in love with you afterwards. But I will not have anybody fal;ing in love with Mr. Paddy. He is my friend. I wish him to .die as a. bachelor. Harry says heywill do it, so I am happy. . • Please say if we may come to your castle. The yacht will take us, hut I shall go upon the railway line. Your affectionate ,•</p>
        <p>P . S.--Say ,if it's convenient, old chap? Don't fott heaven's sake turn your good sister out to grass, or anything of that sort. Just a bed and a crust. with a pipe and a whisky afterwards. It will be enough to see you, old Brian Born. And God bless you, anyway. •</p>
        <p>THE END;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</head>
        <p>OPEN-AIR POLITICS.</p>
        <p>THE FREE TRADE CAMPAIGN CONTINUED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Howes and Councillor Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>The campaign which the Progressive Labour League has organised in support of Flee Trade is meeting with enthusiastic support and there was another large and keenly attentive audience at the open air meeting held at Bridge Foot (near Arpley Cannons) on Monday evening. Mr. S. Wright presided and wam supported by Councillor Middleton, of Oldham (Lancashire and Cheehire Liberal Federation), Councillor J. T. Locker, Messrs. E. Wynne Jones, J. Howes ; J. Morton and .J. hones, jun. (hon. secs:).. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The• CHAIRMAN said Free Trade meant cheap food. Under the present conditions they must have cheap living or they would very soon starve. A man earning 18s. or a week had a difficulty in making ends meet, but if they did as the tariff reformers proposed and put a tax of 10 per emit. upon foodstuffs, it would be equal to a reduction of 2s. in the Eon wages. The tariff reformers said the foreigner would pay the tax, but in the same breath they told us that a tax would keep the foreigner out. How did they reconcile the two statements ? Not long ago the men who were advocating tariff reform were crying out for eight Dreadnoughts, which cost millions of pounds each: What for ?—that we might be a bogey to Germany. Mr. Lloyd-George said : "We will provide you with four, but we will make you pay for them," but, intmedi ately they were asked to pay they cried out " Don't tax us who have the millions. Tax the poor working- man. Tax his food, that is the way to raise money." Mr. Lloyd-George, however, said they had taxed the workers enough and they intended to tax those oho had extracted from the workers that which did not belong to them. Their desire to-day was that it. should he hard to become rich but easy to live and to obtain a living wage. 7119 Warrington Town Council had been discussing the slum problem. Who lived in the slums? The poor people, who went there not from choice but because the condition of things forced them into such dwellings. The workers must endeavour to raiso themselves out of the mire in which they were to-day. They wanted tie see the children brought out of the slums and into God's day- light which was intended for them. That could only be done by the workers looking after their own interests. Mr. Winston Churchill was bringing in a Bill to do away with sweating and thay hoped that, in a very short period alter that measure had been intro- duced, it would not he confined to three or four trades. If there was one thing which ought t) convince the workers that tariff reform was not, to their interests, it was the fact that not a single Labour representative in the House of Commons favoured the policy. Would not the tariff reformers be glad if they cauld only collar one of them, if they could only bribe one? However, he was thankful to say there wus not one Labour representative in Parliament who would be bribed oven by the brewers, the landowners or 111111111- facturers, but they were firm to a man in saying "We will have no tariff reform." (Applause and a voice : " It is tariff de-form.")</p>
        <p>Councillor MIDDLETON then delivered an interesting speech full of homely illustrations and illumined with many flashes of humour. Referring to complaints that food was dear now, he said if they really vanted to find out what was meant by dear food they must go back to the times before we had Free Trade, and they would come to the cenclitsion that to-day we had not dear food but really cheap living. People were saying " You promised us the big loaf and cheap Weld, but since we voted for you at the last election breed has risen." The suggestion that the advance in price was due to the fact that they voted Liberal and Free Trade was most unfair. As a matter of fact, the reason was the failure of the wheat harvest in some of the large wheat-producing parts of the world. The Government could not control the weather. (Laughter.) Councillor Middleton defended the policy (5f exchange in trade between nations, and as an illustration pointed out that we sent £1,600,000 worth of herrings to Germany every yearend received pianos in exchangelt could not be said that the German instruments were imported on account of their cheapness, because he believed they were actually dearer than most pianos that were produced in this. country. If a German piano now cost .£6O, a ten Per cent, duty would increase the price to £66, which meant that a man would be fined for exercising his preference in regard to the kind -of music he would have in his household. That would be an unfair use to which to apply the pressure of taxa- tion. Tariff reformers proposed to surround the eotiatry with a tariff well and to tax everything that came in from foreign countries. Some of them said they would exclude food and others said they would exclude raw materials, but when once they began—and at the very beginning—food would be taxed. Mr. Chamberlain was honest enough to say so. He said he would propose a 5 per cent, duty on dairy proiuce, a 2s. duty on corn, and a 10 per cent, duty on manufactured, articles. For their comfort be told them that the tax on corn and food was only to be a little one. He said "I pledge my word that it shall never be more than 2s. ft quarter." What was that promise worth ? Unfortunately ! Mr. Chamberlain would not always be there,- and when he was gone the men he had set going would take care that, the tax went up. Little taxes, like little children, had a tendency to grow. (Laughter.) They bed grown in every country. Mr. Henry Chaplin, a good, all- round, stalwart Protectionist who believed in making agriculture prosperous by that means, said "It is no good trying to help agriculture with a tax of 2s. a quarter. You will have to begin with one at ss. a quarter." They had got to ss. before they started ; as to where they would get to when they had begun lie could give them another hint. Mr. James Lowther, also a strong Protectionist, said " It is no use troubling about either a 2s. or a ss. tax on corn. You will have to be prepared, if you are going to ao anything for the farmer, to go to 15s. a quarter." That was the way taxes went on. They might start with Mr. Chamberlain and 2s. and move on to Mr. Chaplin and 55., and that was the way to Mr. Lowther and 15s. Ile (the speaker) believed the workingmen of this country would adhere to the untaxed loaf. A tax on bread would hit the poorer classes much more heavily than the well-to-do. Mr. Chamberlain calculated that the effect of a 2s. tax on corn would be a difference of 33ozs. in the weight of a 41b. loaf. They did not want short-weight loaves:. (Applause). When an article was taxed the consumer invariably paid the tax, and they must not be deluded with may notion Unit the foreigner would. pay their share. The Budget introduced .by Mr. Lloyd-George was the great triumph of the Free Trade system, and the speaker appealed to the workingmen to support the Government in their efforts to .prove that Free Trade finance was equal to all the require- ments of this great nation. If they could prove that, tariff reform would have gone by for good. He believed this Budget would be the killing of tariff reform and he asked them to support the Government in their endeavour to carry through a scheme which would do so much for -progress and for, labour in this country. (Applause). Mr.' J. HOWES, referring to the Tariff Reform League's meeting last week, said another Daniel bad come to judgment. (Laughter). He referred to Coun- cillor Broadhurst, who possessed sufficient confidence in his party to be put forward-by them to meet one of the Progressive Labour League's representatives in public debate. One thing that was troubling Councillor Broadhurst was the speech he (Mr. Howes) made on_ the " Lusitania ' and royalties. The tariff reformers had got the royalty nightmare. (Laughter). They would have forgiven him anything else if be had not referred to royalties, and to say that the landowner wits responsible for bad trade to a great extent, was supposed from their standpoint to the greatest offence he could have committed. (Laughter). Councillor Broadhurst said "ho held in his hands a statement from the highest authority on that question in the whole world—Mr. .1. Stephen Jeans." Mr. Jeans, continued Mr. Howes, was a gentleman whe gave evidence before Mr. Chamberlain's Tariff Commission and he (Mr. Howes) held in bin hand the report of that</p>
        <p>Commission. It was a very interesting document and he wished he -could persuade tariff reformers to read it—it was very seldom they read their own literature. (Laughter). Most of the wit- nesses were like a celebrated Biblical character who came to bless and•remained to curse. Councillor Broadhurst said "Mr. J. Stephen Jeans said that in the United States sometimes 2s. per ton was paid in royalties. Ile also said with reference to Belgium, that since 1830 minerals had been owned by private persons. Mr. Howes ought; therefore, to correct his statement, for he was evidently, absolutely ignorant of the conditions which were obtaining. in Belgium or in. America." What he (Mr. lIowes) said was-that this was the only country in the world where the. land owner claimed the: minerals. He knew thatin some countries the land owners- paid royalties and got royalties, but this was the only country in-the world where the land bwner claimed the minerals. lie-madel that claim on the strength 'of an Act of' Parliament passed before the peoplchad any Parliamentary repre- sentation—prior to 1832: Before the reign of Charles lithe whole of the minerals and all the land were owned by the nation. The-land was national-property because it was natural nroperty,but in the reign of Charles 11. a landlord Parliament altered 'these things in their own, interests. They took the land-and then passed,a law-to say that theaaking was legal. (Laughter.) They seized the minerals and had been in possession of them ever. since. The land being 'national property provided for all the requirements of the State, but after the landowners took it to themselves they had to introduce a system of tariffs, making the people who had not the land pay taxes, because those who had-seized the land refused to pay the taxes any-longer. He-blamed-the British aristocracy and chiefly the -Tory aristocracy— although now, thanks to the development of trade, even Liberals bad become land owners and, some- of them were very rich—for introducing this system and being. responsible for all its awful consequences. Councillor Broadhurst, said ; "In bringing up- the question of mining royalties, Mr. Horses was only drawing Isred herring across the path." Well, it was a strong- rod. herring and it was time they-smelled-it. (Laughter.). A very significant passage in Councillor Broadhurst's speech was : "lie would ask, did mining royalties stop foreign countries from buying the raw matt rials of this country? Last year, they sent a-broad in minerals to less than £67,027,350 worth." Where did we send them to ? We sent them to Protected courttries— Councillor Broadhutst said so. "I thought" continued Mr. Howes "he said the foreigners were-shutting our goods out. If they were shutting cur ponds-out the could not go in, and if we sent them £67,000,000-worth it shows they did not shut them out.. So- you see, despite all their Protection, we stilt. send goods to foreign countries." Councillor Broadhurst. went on- to. say that "mining royalties were not tha interference- to trade which it was said they were. In-corroboration- of this statement, Councillor Broadhurst quoted- a statement signed by Mr. W. Abraham and Mr. Barnes. ell known labour leaders." He (Mr. Howes)- would like to see the letter, or what it was that Councillor Broadhurst quoted from. Mr. Abrahon and Mr. Barnes were strong Free Traders and they would. be- the very last, to-endorse anything Councillor Broadhurst said on Protectionist lines. Councillor Broadhurst • also stated " that Steel- rods were imported from,Germany at £6:l2s. per tons while the Brain's' rod cost £91.25. 6d-. per ton. If they took off the 2s. Gd. per ton paid in royalties it would not go very far." Mr. J. Stephen Jeans, in his evidence said that another considerable burden which was to he -borne in the British iron industry was that of royalty rents. The ownership of the minerals in this country was vested in the owner of the soil. Mr. Jeans said it was the same in the United States. In Germany the ownership of minerals was vested in the State ; it was the same in- France and Spain, hut in Belgium since 1830 it bad- been vested in private owners. Why? Because they made an arrangement with tbe State and, paid over an annual large sum to-the State in lieu of the royalties. Mr. Jeans, in giving the average figures as to the extent of this burden per ton of pig iron, said that in Great Britain the royalty was 4s. lOri. per ton—not 2s. 6d. per ton as stated by Coun- cillor Broadhurst. In Germany the royalty was 6d.and in France Bd. How could we compete successfully when our landlords took 4s. 10d ? The amounts taken in royalties in Germany and France Went to the reduc- tion of taxation but when the landlord in Great Britain took 4s. 10d. it dropped into his own pocket and we got no benefit at all. The fact that we , could send £67,000,000 wot th abroad, continued Mr. Howes,showed that the old country was not played out and that tariff reform was is fallacy. If tariff reformers would read the repbrt of their own Commission, they would either have to abandon,their Protectionist policy and stick to Free Trade or get someone to dig a hole and bury the. report. (Laughter), 'rho whole of the Labour repre- sentatives were Free Traders. lhe great iron working authorities, including Mr. Cox' their secretary, were Free Traders. Mr. Cax quoted extracts published. in the Tory blue book before the last election in' which they showed that Protection in Germany made their iron and steel industries so expensive that they were not able to build their own. strips because the makers were dumping their materials over here at. a low price -in order to keep up the erica in Germany. We bought tha steel- cheap, built ships and sold them to Germany. What he -(Mr. Howes) asked the tariff reformers to do was to find a single country in the world which had a ship-building industry equal to ours, a single country in the world where the workers got a wage equal in purchasing power to ours, a single country where the hours of labour were shorter in proportion to the purchasing power of the wages, or where unemployment was smaller than it was in this country at the present moment. A report from the United States only last week gave a melancholy description of the state of affairs there, 400,000 being unemployed in the States through which the trade union represen- tatives who made the report travelled. He (Mr. Howes) know when the Tariff Commission was about to sit what tempting offers where held out to some of the trade union representatives, but they were men, and they were not going to sell themsalves body and soul, even to the tariff reformers. Mr. Tennant, Parliamentary Secretary to the Board of Trade, in a printed reply to a•question In Parliament, stated that the average official price of wheat sold in Paris, during the week was 41s. per imperial 'quarter, and the average price of wheat sold in London at the same time was 42a. 6d. When the price of. bread went up, we were. always at the bottom price in this Free Trade- country. (Applause.) We- beat every protected colintry in the world. (Applause.) Free Trade meant that God's gifts were for all people. Tariffs were a curse brought about by the ingenuity' of covetous men. who had tried to corner and monopolise the good things end keep other people :from them. Free Traders pre- ferred sympathy to inhumanity, open ports to closed ports, Free Trade to restricted trade, and they said the Britisher would always be able to hold his own against all the world if he got a fair opportunity of doing so and the dead weights were taken from him. Mining royalties were a curse invented by the covetousness of the land owners. In conclusion Mr. Howes said pro- gressives must grapple with the land question. This .Budget was a bonmencement. It was the thin end of -the wedge and he hoped the workers would push. that wedge a little further when they had the chance, because if those who had wealth bad not to pay in pro- Tort!on to their wealth, why should those who had-only wages pay in proportion to their wages? (Applause). On the proposition of Mr. E. WYNNE-JONES, seconded 'by Mr. J. MORTON, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the-Chairman and speakers. Mr. HOWES, in responding, referred to the illness of Alderman Pierpoint, and said they were all glad to hear .he was now on the road to recovery. They would welcome him back to the field of action as soon as possible. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>" PERFECTION."</head>
        <p>" PERFECTION."</p>
        <p>Soap Trade Nark Case.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S APPLICATION FAILS</p>
        <p>Mr, Justice Swinfen Eady, in the Chancery Division on Tuesday, gave judgment in a motion on behalf of Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited Warrington. asking the court to direct the Registrar of Trade Marks to proceed with the registration of the word "Perfection" as applied to Messrs. Crosfleld's common soaps, notwith- standing the opposition of Lever Brothers, Limited, Port Sunlight. His Lordship said the question he had to determine was whether the word " Perfection" was adopted to distinguish the common soaps of other people—manu- facturers as well as dealers. The result•of the evidence was that in sonte counties of England and Wales Perfection soap to many people indicated Crostield Perfection washing soap ; to other persons in the same counties it did not convey any such meaning ' • and in some ' counties it scarcely ' indicated the plaintiffs' soap to anyone, as it was almost unknown in the county. The applicants' soap wati only little known in London. In Scotland and Tzeland the word " Perfection " did not indicate or distinguish the plaintiffs' soap from that of tho other manufacturers to any appreciable / extent, if at all. Considering the evidence as a whole, he had come to the conclusion that the world " Perfection " was not a distinctive mark, and was not adopted to distin,,uish,the goods of the applicants from the goods of other persons. He had taken into consideration the evidence of users, and still was of opinion that the ,word was not adopted to dictinguish the goods of the applicants. In his opinion the .`• Perfection " was not a- distinotive mark within the paragraph or Sub-section 5 of Section 9 of the Act of 1905, notwithstanding the use of the word by the applicants in the past. The conclusion to which he had come would not, of course, affect the applicants' Pyramid trademark, nor prevent the applicants from preventing other persons from passing off their soap as that of the applicants. Indeed, Section 45 of the Act of 1905 set at rest any doubt which might have arisen under Section 42 of the Act by providing that nothing in the Act contained should he at liberty to affect the rights of action against any person from passing off their goods as those of another person, or the remedies in respect thereof. His decision merely prevented the applicants from monopo- lising the word" Perfection "as a trademark for other people. lie refused the application, with costs, and the applicants must also pay the costs QI the Registrar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
