      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Lymm.</head>
        <p>Lymm.</p>
        <p>T. PETER'S SCHOOLS, OUGHTRINGTON.—One of the Diocesan Inspectors visited these schools on Thursday for the purpose of examining the children in religious knowledge. LYAIM WESLEY AN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.— The 106th anniversary was held on Sunday -last, when the special preacher was the Rev. F. Welwyn Kernick, M.A. The subject of his morning's discourse was "Ile Lad's Miracle." In the afternoon the Sunday school gathering was presided over by Mr. David Daniels, an appropriate and bepfal address being given to the children by Mr. Kernick. There was a good congregation in the evening, the subject of the address being "The Shepherd's psalm." The singing at all the services was of a particularly attractive character. The anthems, "I-low amiable are Thy habitations' (Salanian); "Jean, Priceless Treasure" (Varley Roberts), and " There is a green hill " (Gounod), were well rendered. A notice- able feature was the singing of the scholars. Over £lO, which will be devoted to the funds of the Sunday school, was realised at the Services. DEATH OF MRS.GRIFFITHS.—The death took place on Sunday last after a long illness, patiently borne, of Mrs. Giiffiths, wife of Mr. Thomas Griffiths, of Lymm who was the first manager of Lymm Gas Works. The deceased lady bad attained the ripe age of 78 years. For more than 45 years she bad been a worshipper at and a liberal supporter of Higher-lane Baptist Church, and although living a quiet retired life, she was always ready to help by her counsel and advise any worthy cause brought to her notice. She will he remembered by all who knew her, but particularly by the members of the community in which she took so loving an interest, as an earnest Christian woman. The funeral took place en Thursday afternoon at the Baptist Church in the presence of a number of sorrowing friends and relatives, the officiating minister being the Rev. J. S. Hughes, formerly of Hill Cliff° Baptist Church, who has been a great friend of the deceased lady and her family all his life. The chief mourners present at the gravesiee were Mr. Griffiths, Mr. Jones, Mr. Roberts, Mr. and Mrs. Evans, and uoices of the late Mrs. Griffiths. A largo number of beautiful floral tributes were sent by relatives and friends..</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS AT LYALNI GRAMMAR SCHOOL. At a meeting of Cheshire City Education Committee at Crewe OD Monday a report from the County Architect was received in regard to the alterations necessary at the Lyrnrn Grammar School. In preparing plans for the alterations an effort had been made to retain the existing buildings as much RS possible. The new wing will be built on the west side of the present Assembly Hall and will be two floors in height. The ground floor will contain girls' entrance, cloakroom, lavatory and w.c. s., assistant mistresses' room and lavatory, kindergarten classroom for 20, -dad kindergarten boss' lavatory, conveniently placed; music room 18ft. by 13ft. There will also be three classrooms on the ground floor, one for 20, and two for 15 each, while a stone staircase will lead up to the upper floor, where there will be two classrooms for 20 each, rind one for 15, and an art classroom, with north light, 28ft. by 19ft. If a cookery room was required it could be placed on the first floor where indicated on the plan. The estimated cost would be as follows :—New wing. 90,544 cubic feet at 6d., £2,263 12a.; gymnasium, manual instruction, ecc., 27,540 cubic feet at 5d.. £570; beating apparatus and minor alterations, £250 ; con- tingencies, £100; total, £3,153 12s. • If a cookery room is required, the extra cost would be £250. The committee resolved that a copy of the report he sent to the governing body of the. Lymm Grammar School, and that they be informed that before the county can undertake to expend so large a 'sum as is therein indi- cated, it would be necessary for the school to be transferred to the county as a secondary school, and a scheme obtained from the Board of Education sanction- ing such transfer. The governors wero asked for their observations of the matter. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ The Higher Education Committee resolved that the following should represent the County Council on the governing body of Lymm Grammar School :—Colonel Legh, Mrs. Legh, Mrs.Callender, Messrs. John Arnold, (Timperley), S. W. Gould, T. J. Ridgway, S.Davies (Heatley), and G. P. Dewhurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>LYAIM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LYAIM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before the Hon. J. E. Cross (chairman), and Messrs. J. E. G. Groves, S. W. Gould and Admiral Jones-Parry. _ _ _</p>
        <p>NOISY TRIPPERS.—On June 4th, the quiet village was disturbed by,the revelry of R brake of loud trippers from Sale. As a sequel, Jesaie Royle,of Egerton-street Sale, was summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Lymm on that day.—Sergeant Washington stated that about 8 p.m. on the 4th of June, he was called out of ,his station to a disturbance tbat was taking place. On an iving there be saw the defendant, who was very drunk and was making use of very vile language. There would be about one hundred people gathered round h er. Another man named Massey caught hold of her and said that her friends would look after her and took her over the canal bridge towards the Police Station. Witness then got hold of her and took her into custody. He afterwards allowed her to go home.—Defendant was fined 10s. or 1 days. William Massey, of Egerton-atreet, Sale, Was charged with a similar offence at the same place and time.— P.C. Washington proved the case, and said that defend- ant got hold of the last defendant and dragged her over the bridge. Witness told defendant to go away but be would not, and when witness took another man into custody defendant said "Let him get hold of me and I will double him up." Witness afterwards took defend- ant's name and address and told him he would be reported —Defendant was fined 10s. or 7 days in default, the Chairman adding that they were not going to have their peaceful village disturbed. He hoped it would be a warning to all people in the future.—Defendant " It will he a warning for me, because I am not coming again." FARM LABOURERS' QUARREL.—John Baskervil le, of High Leigh, was summoned for making use of threats •to :John Connor, an Irish farm labourer, who in consequence was in fear of defend- ant doing !rime some bodily harm.—Defenriant pleaded "Not guilty." Complainant stated that he worked on a farm at Hick Leigh owned by James White and on the 23rd of May about 9.30 be was in the public bonse at Hon Green. He was quite sober, having had only three pints. Complainant did not know whether Baskerville was sober or not. Baskrrville went out of the public-heuse and started a bother and complaint ant saw him strike one Man. The landlord of the house then ordered them off. Complainant along with his companions went on their way to the farm, where they worked —Defendant and a number of other men rushed after complaintant and his gang who just reached their shanty in time and closed the door which had to to barricaded.—Defendant then began to throw stones at, door and threatened to shoot complainant. He also said be would give him a good hiding. Complainant had never said anything to defenda.t•t. Ihe bother arose 'through another Mahn-vim—Complainant in answer to defendant said he did not throw any stones at defendant's bat (produced, which had a large hole in it). Thomas Briar', another labourer, said defendant stood about seven y tvds away from the shanty door and threw stones at it. He also threatened to shoot com- plainant. Detendant said the compleintant began to throw stones first. They went into a sand-hole close by "and loaded up" with stones. Defendant had to retreat as the stones 'came like hail" and one hit his hat and made a bole in it, and also cut his held. Defendant did not go anye here near the shanty.—P.C. White stated that on investigating the matter he found a large quantity of stones round the shanty door.— Defendant: Somebody must have in them there.— Witness also saw a number of dents n the shanty door caused by stones being thrown at it.—The Chairman stated that they had every reason to believe that defendant committed the offence, he would be eound over to keep the peace for 12 month and to find a surity of .C 5 and also to pay costs of the case (155.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RUNCORN MID WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN MID WIDNES.</p>
        <p>FARM FIRE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About half-past tenon Monday morning a fire broke out in the shippon of Picow Farm, and spread to an adjoining barn filled with straw. A horse and a bull were taken from the shippon before they were injured. The Runcorn Fire Brigade, were on the scene a quarter of an hour after the alarm, and their efforts mere directed to preventing the spread of the fire. The farm is occupied by Messrs. Richardson Brothers. The fire was extinguished about two o'clock, the damage being estimated at about £2,000. SHIP CAPTAIN'S LIABILITY.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday, Dr. Delaney, of Runcorn, sued Robert Pritchard, captain of the s.s. Pansy, for £6 for professional services rendered. On January 6th Captain Pritchard summoned the doctor to his ship at Weston Point to attend a fireman named George, who had sustained a fractured thigh. The defence was that the man bad been discharged for drunkenness, and insubordination, and after the crew had retired to rest, got on board, and as found next morning lying in the stokehold with a fractured leg. The captain denied liability. His Honour held that the captain was liable for calling the doctor to the ship, and for what he did on board. Perhaps the captain could recover it from the owners. The captain could have gone to the public authorities to have the man removed. He gave the doctor a verdict for one guinea with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <p>EIFFEL TOWER LEMONADE. 11, is article is undoubtedly the most delicious, cool, refreshing drink obtainable, so healthful, so convenient, so cheap. —41 d. per bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS TERRITORIALISTS FOR BIST,EY. The following have been chosen to represent the sth South Lancashire Regiment of St. Helens at.Bisley : Sergeant R. Norbury, who was second in the King's last year, Colour-Sergeant Gamble, and Corporal Whittaker, of Prescot. Private Frank Smith has also qualified by winning the N.R.A. Jubilee badge. Private R. Milligan, who has won the N.R.A. recruit's medal, also goes along with Private Harry West. The last two named, West and Milligan, are sons of old members of the corps, who have represented the battalion in the national meeting for a great number of 3,-eals.</p>
        <p>KILLED WHEN LEAVING WORK.</p>
        <p>John Leigh, aged fifty-three, a 'miner, living at 58, Providence-terrace, Collins-green, was killed on Saturday while leaving work at the Havannah Parr, St.,Helens. Leigh bad been working from flee o'clock in the morning, and at 12.15 be left work and was going to the bottom of the pit along a ith Joseph Molyneux and a man named Prees. When they got to ODO of the brows Leigh proceeded alone, and the other two on following shortly afterwards`found him lying unconscious and severely injured across the waf2gon rails. It appeared that he had in some way been caught by moving waggons and gushed. The unfortunate man was conveyed to the pit-eye with the object of getting assistance for him, but he died before anything could be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ST. HELENS VITRIOL OUTRAGE.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS VITRIOL OUTRAGE.</p>
        <p>• Joseph Westwell, a young St. Helens man, was charged on remand on Monday with throwing vitriol into the face of his stepfather, William Helsby, South John-street.—Helsby gave evidence, and said that about half-past six on Sunday evening, May 16th, when wit- ness and his wife and family were sitting at tea, Westwell entered the house by the back door and asked his mother if he could have °, wash.' She told him to go Into the washhouse and get one. In about ten minutes he returned to the kitchen and stood by the table. He atited for some tea. Witness said, " -Vou will have nothing here and you had better be going." Westwell replied, "I am not ready yet." Witness got up from the table to make him go. upon which Westwell produced a glass vessel from behind him, and threw some liquid into witness's eyes and tace. The stuff caused a burn- ing sensation and grfat pain; and wit ne,s at once ran to Dr. O'Neeffe's surgery, in Park-road, where the doctor found the 'right eye in, n very bad state, and apparently destroyed; while the other eye and the man's face were badly burnt. Wiines afterwards went to the Liverpool Eve infirmary, where be remained until Friday last. Drs. -Stephenson and Johnson could not see the right eye for a fortnight, and told witness they thought it was gone. On Thursday, May 27th, he underwent, an operation, ,hen skin from his arm was grafted on to his eyelid: Since that time be had been able to see a little, but it was Still painful.—Vtihen arrested on the night of the outrage at Prescot by Detective Anders, Westwell said, " I have been expect- ing you. Is it for the vitriol throwing?" He also asked, Have I blinded him?" and when told that his stepfather was blind in one eye, he said he would blind him altogether when he came out.—The prisoner was committed for trial at the 'assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Business of the Next Meeting</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee will be held at the Town hall on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>EXPENSES SURCHA RGED.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee on-June 14th the report ot the District Auditor of the. Local Govern- ment Board upon his audit of the Committee's accounts for the year ended on the 31st March last %as sub- mitted, and it was noted that he bad taken exception to certain payments, and had surcharged the expenses incurred by the Bead Master of the School or Art in attending, on behalf of the Committee; the inter- national Art Congress in London. it was noted that an appeal was being made by the Law Clerk to the Committee to the Local Government Board against this surcharge. rhe Director was instructed to write to the Board of Education calling their attention to the items objected to by the Auditor, and to seek their interven- tion with the Local Government Board With a view such expenditure being allowed in the future. ASSISTANT DIRECTOR'S SALARY.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee has resolved that the salary of Mr. Robert B. Roberts, Assistant Director of Educa- tion, be increased from £2OO to £220 per annum as from the Ibt July, 1909. . INFANTS' DEPARTAIF.NTS. _ _ .</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee has resolved (a) that, from the Ist July, 1912, no teacher shall he appointed to the position of headmistress of an Infants' department or school unless she holds the certificate of the National Froebel Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MB ACCOMMODATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>MB ACCOMMODATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The Committee proceeded to the deterred considera- tion of the communication from the Board of Educa- tion, in reference to the intended revision by the Board in the Code for 1909 of the regulations affecting the stalling of the public elementary schools and the size of the classes therein, and a report upon tbe subject. which had been prepared by the Director of Education having been received, it was resolved (a) That this Committee, whilst recognising the educational advantage wbich will accrue from these changed regulations, desire to approach the Board of Education with a view to the regulation requiring that the number of scholars on the register of any class shall not exceed 60 being so modi- fied as to provide that at no time during the school year shall the average attendance in any class for any ono week exceed 60 • and (b) that the Association of Education Committees be asked to support the Com- mittee's action in this matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At a meetin g of the Sites and Buildings Cornmittee,it was noted that the Board of Education had expressed their approval. of he suggested site for a public elemen- tary k 01001 in the neighbourhood of Liverpool-road, conditionally upon there being no appeal against it under section 8 of the Education Act, 1902.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICULTURAL</head>
        <p>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICULTURAL</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>DETAILS OF THE SCHEDULE.</p>
        <p>The prize schedules for the Wirral and Bill:kW:, e: Agricultural Society's 67th annual show, to be bei‘i oo the 13th, 14th, and 15th July, at Bebington. under the presidrncy of Arthur E. Evans, Esq.' J.P4Bronwylfa, Wrexham, has now been issued. 'I he handsome amount ot 3,000 is being offered for competition. Several improvements have this year been made in the schedule, also new classes introduced. • A challenge cup, value 10 guineas, has been presented by Messrs. Fowler and de la Perrelle for competition among Cheshire residents, for best Jersey heifer. The classes for weight carrying hunters will be judged in the afternoon of the first day of the show, and the prizes in each class are £l5, £lO, and £5 respectively, In the harness horse section prizes of 25 guineas (given by Mr. John W. Harvie), and ..£lO are being offered for four-in-hand " Park" teams, and for the four-in-hand " Road "teams the prizes are £lO, £6, and There are also classes for tandem teams and pairs, in addition to a number of classes for single harness horses. Competition in the riding classes is not restricted to hackneys, the prixestiaing offered for the `best saddle horse of tiny description. Five classes are provided for " district " and local " tradesmen for horses which are regularly flied in their business. Leaping competitions will be held each day, the - prises being £l5, £lO, £5, and £3 in each class; hut the winner on the first and second days cinnot compete in any other competition for leapers. A grand military arid naval display is being arranged for the evenings of Wednesday and Thursday, and on the latter evening a horse jumping competition will also take place, com- petition being confined to horses which have never previously vi on a prize of • £lO or upwards. Particulars will be sent on application to the secretary. The usual classes for cheese, butter and eggs are again included. In addition to the Society's Challenge Cups, some of which are valued at 100 guineas, a 25 guinea cup is this year being offered by Mr. Richard P. Evans, J.P., for the best harness horse or pony. 'For machinery, imple- ment and miscellaneous exhibits the society's specially designed gold, silver, and bronze medals are offered, in accordance with the conditions of classification. Exhibits of bread will be shown this year, snd in addi- tion to specially designed gold and silver medals, which constitute the prizes, several leading millers are offering extra prizes. There will also be a champion prize for the best exhibit in the bread section, and with one exception. competition in these classes is open to the United Kingdom. A most liberal classification for dogs (which will be exhibited on the 14th and 15th only) has been provided, and the Kennel Club has again selected this show to rank as a championship show, and offers a large number of challenge prize certificates for competition. The charge for admission on each of the above dates C'ill be one shilling. In addition to the usual class prizes there are a number of valuable cups and other special prizes being offered for competition. including several confined to dogs belonging to and shown by children under 15 years of age. To poultry and pigeons an extensive list of classes have been allotted, and several special prizes are offered for almost every variety. The Genuine Flying Homer's Club's Show is being held in connection, with this Society's show' and a large number of classes have been included for this interesting variety of pigeon, viz , the real carrier of messages. In the fur section of the show classes have been arranged for all the popular .varieties of • rabbits and eavies. _</p>
        <p>Entries close in each section at 10 a.m. on the Ist July, and not under any circumstances whatever can entries be accepted after this hour. When applying fora prize schedule, intending exhibi- tors should state for which section they repire same.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SLAVONIA WRECK.</head>
        <p>THE SLAVONIA WRECK.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. King's Question In Parliament. In the House of Commons on Thursday, Mr. Alfred J: King asked the Prime Minister if his attention had teen called to the fact that 410 passengers of the British steamer Slavonia, wrecked off Flores Island, had been saved by the prompt and affective action of two 3erman merchant ships—the North German Lloyd Princess Irene and theHamburg-Amarican liner Batavia —and whether, in view of these facts, his Majesty's Government will reconsider their decision to maintain the right of this country to capture, and possibly destroy, these ships in the unfortunate event of war with the nation under whose flag they sail. • Mr. Asquith lam glad to have an opportunity of expressing his Majesty's Government's appreciation of the action of the German Merchant ships. Such actions of kindness and courtesy no doubt have a reciprocal effect in promoting good feeling between nations. They add to the pleasures of peace, and make nations reluctant to disturb it; but I cannot see what bearing they have on the conduct of the rules of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>MARRIED, FEMALE TEACHERS. An Appeal to the Law.</head>
        <p>MARRIED, FEMALE TEACHERS. An Appeal to the Law.</p>
        <p>~„ At a meeting of the School Management Committee on May 20th, it was resolved that permission be given for the appointment of the following teachers; viz. : Fairfield (girls), head mistress (in place of Mrs. Stansfleld, whose engagement will terminate on the 31st July, 1909) ; St. Barnabas' (mixed), certificated assistant mistress (in place of Mrs. Proud, whose engagement will terminate on the 31st July, 1909) ; St..Barnabas' (infants'), certificated assistant mistress (in place of Mrs. Grounds, whose engagement will terminate on the 31st July, 1909) ; Sankey Bridges' Council (infants'), certificated assistant mistress (in place of Mrs. Hayes, whose engagement will terminate on the 31st July, 1909). In connection with the permission given for the appointment of a head mistress for the girls' depart- ment of the Fairfield school, the Director was instructed to inform the managers of the Committee's intention to advertise the position and to submit the applications received to the managers for their consideration. It was noted that applications would also be sub- mitted to the managers of the other schools mentioned with a 'view to the filling of the positions named. At a meeting on June 10th, a letter was read from the Law Clerk to the Cdmmittee, forwarding copies of communications received by him from a firm of solicitors intimating their intention to commence pro- ceedings in the Chancery Division of the High Court of Justice, on behalf of Mrs. Lydia Mary Stansfield and Mrs. Catherine Grounds, against the Local Education Authority in respect of . the termination of the engagements of these teachers and it was resolved that the Law Clerk to the Committee be instructed to, take the necessary steps to defend the Local Educa- tion!,Authority in the proceedings referred to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There are one or two turns of exceptional merit at the :Palace and Hippodrome this week, and the" bill" generally is of an attractive and amusing kind. The Ahmed Ibrahim Troupe of Arab tumblers and equilibrists give a remarkable show. They are nine in number, and every one seems to be better than the last as they come on to the stage and perform their Val;i9VS evolutions. Some of the tumbling was almost incredibly dating and dexterous; and made one wonder whether such men possessed bones or springs as an anatomical, basis. The massed figures given by the troupe as a whole were also high-class and evoked very heal ty applause. One of the most uncommon turns seen for a long time is that of Viviana and a dancing scena, entitled "The Toy Shop." The scene is admirably arranged, and the " dolls " look like dolls . until they are brought to the front and begin to dance. The two girls are splendid dancers and deserve the fine reception they get, but.the palm must be given to Mr. Golliwog, whose make-up and movements are wonder- fully clever. The 'shop-boy extiacts .plenty of fun out of his single customer, and altogether the turn can be classed "A 1." Bert Bowden and Tom Moore both do well in the funny line, and the first-named was obliged on Monday to respond to an insistent recall for a. song which took the popular fancy. Shirley and Ransome present a coster episode which is both pathetic and humorous. It has some good points, but the pathos is rather weak and does not go\ well to Mollie Watson sings in a bright and taking style and Josephine. Crooke gives illustrated songs of the type now so popular in the Halls. The biograph pictures are full of merit and variety.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME</p>
        <p>There is another attractive bill for next week. Gus Garrick will present his "Music Hall Tit-Bits," and Cassie Walmer, coon delineator,will appear in her latest successes,including"Call again" and "A friend in need." Other artistes will be :—Dan Feeley, comedian ; The Woodwards in their sensational cycle act; Barrett and Knowles, versatile comedians; Godfrey James, the singing collier; Brothers Rees, cannon ball manipulators.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>FORESTERS' PICNIC.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The second annual picnic and bowling handicap of the members of the' Warrington Distriat of the A.O.F.' was held at the Stamford Arms; Bollington on Satur- day last when charming weather Prevailed. 'The party left Warrington at about 2 pm. by Mr. H. F. Greenough's 'buses, and and greatly enjoyed the delightful drive through the lovely Cheshire lanes to Bollington. Immediately on arriving the handicapping was' arranged and the competitors took posses- sion of the green which was in fine condition. The first and second rounds were as much as could be got through 'before 6 o'clock when tea was announced. This function was catered for in Host Jefferson's usual surantious manner. quality and quantity abound- ; 1 the appreciation of the Foresters was shewn 1 it‘arty attacks upon the good things provided. After tea the members again adjourned to the green and some of those who had been unfortunate enough to be defeated in the earlier rounds took advantage of the 'time at their disposal before the 'close of the bowl- ing, to explore those delightful iural lanes and scenes so di ten encountered in that part of Cheshire. The now approaching final on the green was causing great excitement and the last competitors Bro. J. H. Sanson of Court, 430 and Bro. W. Goulding of Court 240. had the pleasure of satisfying the sporting instincts of a large and enthusiastic audience by a splendidly fought game which. resulted in a win for Bro. J. H. Stinson. Prize distribution followed and D.C.R. Bro. James Walsh took the chair supported by D.S.C.R. Bro. James Grayson, Bro. P. Peacock, L. Ditties, J. Chrimes, S. B. Healey, R. Thornley, E. A. A. Clayton and tither district officers. The first prize of a silver and enamelled medal presented by the District was handed by the D.C.R. to Bro. Sansoa who suitably replied: Bro Walsh then proceeded with the presentation of the 25 other prizes which hadbeen offered for, competition delighting all present by the jovial manner in which he carried out his duties. The nsdal votes of thlnks to the donors of prizes and to the Host were passed with much acclamation.and replied to, after which the return journey was commenced and Warrington safely reached • all voting that they had had a jolly good time,' and that complete success had attended the second annual picnic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page 3.)</p>
        <p>WARRI'SGTON THURSDAY V. LYMM.—An interesting match took place at Lymm on Thursday between the Warrington Thursday and Lymm C.C. For the Warring- ton eleven W. H. Podmore made a good show, and Bennett, for Lymm, did effective work with the ball. Scores : WARRINGTON THURSDAY. LYMM. W.H.Podmore b A.Webb 96 S. Marsh c Bennett b</p>
        <p>J. W. Wells b A. Webb 17 W. H. Podmore 25 J. F. Forster b Kelm.ll 10 A.E.Saville b Podmore 2 J. M. Ingram b Marsh ... 21 "8.. Hartley c White b B Lowe odt, out - 38 Podmore S. G.'llay bH. S. Saville 2 G.A.Saville b Podmore 4 J. Simpson b H.S. Saville 1 G.H. Parker b Podmore 0 Bennett bH. Saville 5 A. Urquhart st J. W. A. FQW 1) H. Saville E. W. White c Hartley b B. Collins b Podmore 20 H. S. Saville G. H. Gregson c Kelsall A. Webb b Lowe lO bH. S. Saville - W. Moss b Lowe 2 , Extras</p>
      </div>
