      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <p>Mr. William Bennett's Life Story. Sir,—ln your issue of Saturday June sth I read the life story of Mr. William Bennett. In the course of his history he mentions going to a school kept by Mr. Charles Nelson, an old soldier, who had a cork • leg and no fingers on his right hand. I beg to state Mr. Bennett is mistaken in two items. Firstly, Mr. Nelson was not an old soldier, never having been in the army. He came to Warrington when a young man and was writing master and accountant to what was called in those days the Free Grammar School. After- wards he gave private lessons to several gentlemen's sons. Then he opened his school in Bank-street. Mr. Nelson was a pupil of Dr. Saul, Abbey Holme, ;lad, and also at ,Dumfries Academy, N.B. &amp;cons ly, Mr. Nelson had not a cork leg, both legs being quite sound. He had lost three fingers on his Tight hand owing to an accident when a child. Perhaps you will kindly inform Mr. Bennett of these facts in your valuable paper, and oblige his daughter —Yours faithfully, _</p>
        <p>E. NELSON</p>
        <p>28, Victoria-road, Kilburn, London, N.W. June 13th, 1909.</p>
        <p>-( vsIiIII - .‘ lgtte to the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Jubilee of the Volunteers.</head>
        <p>The Jubilee of the Volunteers.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Next Sunday (the 20th of June) will be the fiftieth anniversary of the memorable meeting held in the Old Music Hall at which it was decided to establish a rifle corps for Warrington and the neighbourhood.- I have not heard of any official celebration of the event; but' any patriotic Warrinc,- tonian who • really desires to serve his town and country might appropriately keep it up by joining the Territorial Army. I have heard with not a little shame that the Warrington battalion is still about 150 men short. I understand that there is some complaint in reference to the scale of pay, but the fact remains that nearly every other district in West Lancashire has provided its proper quota ; and I gather from Mr. Walter Crompton and Mr. George Venn's interesting centenary volume (" Warrington Volunteers, 1798- 1898"), which every patriotic Warringtonian ought to read, that, in 1798, when the" Bluebacks " rallied to defend their country, nobody received any pay at all; that every man provided himself with arms, accoutre- ments, clothing and provisions; that all the distinctions of private life were laid aside ; that, although the population of the town was only about a sixth of what it is to-day, there were 160 present at the first muster; and that they were the the second corps embodied ,in the county. And, five years later, when the " Bluebacks " were succeeded by the " Robin Redbreasts," 500 men were asked for and over 1,300 applied to be enrolled. I do not know what has come over Warrington. Our recently disbanded Volunteer battalion was one of the earliest in the country to offer to serve in the South African War, and sent not less than three contingents to the front, and its actual strength, at the time of the formation of the Territorial Force, -was I believe, over 1,100. Now, the need is greater, and we can only muster about 850 men all told. Surely some of the excited thousands who throng our football matches might do better service to them- selves and to their country by enlisting in our local army of defence! Surely one citizen in seventy is not too much to ask for from a town with such a record!</p>
        <p>We are about to welcome to our ancient streets our gracious King and Queen. The quaint old houses will be bright with bunting and the welkin will resound with patriotic cheers. The King would probably be better pleased if we could send a thousand men to march in front of him at Knowsley Park upon the previous day. In 1898, I wrote some centenary verses with the following refrain: ad Bluebacks, Redbreasts,' valiant Volunteers • Names may alter, men may change, still through all the years Hearts beat high at Duty's call; life would be laid down</p>
        <p>For the dear old country and 'the good old town.'" The verse was very inaifferent, and I have often thought that I should like to revise, it but Ido not want to have to revise the sentiment also. Are there not, in Warrington, 150 men who love their native land enough to join the" Territorials " in the fort- night which remains before the Royal Visit, and so to save the old town, on the day of its crowning honour, from the shadow of. a deep and permanent disgrace? —Yours, &amp;C.,</p>
        <p>ARTHUR BENNETT Paddington House, Warrington, 17th June, 1.00,0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHILD S DEATH. Shocking Home Conditions.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHILD S DEATH. Shocking Home Conditions.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse. County Coroner, held the adjourned inquiry at the Warrington Police Station on Wednes- day, into tbe circumstances attending the death of Beatrice Bandlow, the six-weeks-old daughter of Thomas and Emma Jane Handlon, 6, Back Atherton- street. Mr. A. Browne watched the proceedings on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C. _</p>
        <p>_ Dr. Naden Liverpool-road, stated that on May 31st the mother brought the child to his house.. It then appeared to be suffering from .mal-nutrition and indigestion. He gave the mother a bottle of medicine. On the evening of June sth Mrs. Handlow called and informed him that the child was dead and asked for a certificate. He told her that be could not give one until he bad made some inquiries as Inspector Cox, N.S.P.C.C., had spoken to him about the case. Inspector Cox then submitted a lengthy report. He stated that Mrs. Handlon received 295. a week, and her husband paid the rent apart from that sum.On Wednesday, June 2nd, witness visited the house and found the baby lying on a maderup bed on a chair in the kitchen. The child was emaciated, the skin was hanging about it loosely, and one part of its body was raw, red and dirty. The clothing was also dirty and wet, and the child appeared to be starved and hungry. There was only a little food in a dirty jam jar, and it looked like coloured water. There was no milk in the house, and the place was practically destitute of furniture, except for a table and three old chairs in the kitchen, and three old beds. The woman was indolent and fond of drink, and was always pledging things. She informed witness that she had given the child some home-made bread. In reply to the Coroner' Inspector Cox said the woman had not been brought up for any offence. The Coroner: She has escaped. Dr. Peacocke said he was called in on June 4th and found the baby was practically dying. It was in a very emaciated condition—practically skin and bone. The child died next day and he made a post-mortem examination. The body was badly nourished and devoid of fat. The liver was congested and enlarged, and in his opinion the child died from congestion of the liver anl mal-nutrition. _ _</p>
        <p>dn answer to the Coroner witners said there was pro- found ignorance on tbe part of some mothers as to how to bring up babies. He considered there was neglect in this case, but probably a lot of it was due to ignorance. He thought the mother bad caused the child unneces- sary suffering but he would not swear that there was criminal neglect. In reply to Mr. Browne, witness said the child was 4ilbs. instead of 71bs. Miss Pokes, a lady inspector, said sbe saw the child soon after it was born, and it was a splendid baby. Mrs. Handlon, the mother of the child, was tbqn called and said she was under the impression that se had done her best to bring the child up. She gave it some bread, well-boiled, with a little butter and sugar. The Coroner: Did you hear what Inspector Cox said about your house? Witness: Inspector Cox is telling an untruth. Ile is telling a lie, and a big lie. The Coroner said he did not believe the woman had done her duty to the child, but the evidence was not sufficient to send her for trial. No jury would convict. Although she might not be guilty of neglect which amounted to manslaughter, she might be quite guilty of neglecting the child in such a manner as to cause it unnecessary kuffering, within the meaning of the Act. The jury returned a verdict that death was due to real-nutrition and congestion of the liver, and added that in their opinion the mother had been guilty of an offence which should be brought before the notice of the magistrates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Drink and Suicide.</head>
        <p>Drink and Suicide.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an inquest at Warrington on Saturday into the death, of Peter R. Lawless, an ex-footballer, forty years of age, joiner, of 110, Foster-street, who committed suicide by cutting his throat with a razor. It was stated by the widow that her husband came home drunk on May 27th, and his conduct was so bad that she and the children had to leave him. On going to the -house last Thursday morning she found the door lOcked, and when Police- constable Woolcat forced the door he found Lawless lying on the rug in front of the fire with a deep gash in his throat. She could attach no reason .to her husband's action except drink.—A verdict of "Suicide 'whilst of unsound mind" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S STANDS ABANDONED.</p>
        <p>Arrangements for the Royal visit to Warrington on July 6th, are being rapidly pushed forward. The grand stand is in course of erection in front of the Town Hall, the work' being expeditiously done by experienced workmen under the direction of the Borough Surveyor (Mr. T. Longdin.) This stand wjll be used for the accommodation of members of the Town Council and Education Committee, magistrates and other prominent townspeople. APPEAL TO THE TOWNSPEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The Reception Committee intimate that they will be glad if the owners and occupiers of property along the Royal route will, by the decoration of their premises, assist in tendering a hehrty welcome to theis Majesties, and they also request all employers of labour to endeavour to arrange for the day to be observed as a half-holiday after 1 p.m. CHILDREN'S STANDS ABANDONED.</p>
        <p>The Reception Committee met at the Town Hall on Monday afternoon under the presidency of the Mayor, when it was reported that Lord Derby had intimated that the King would pass throughWarrington in a closed motor car. The committee therefore Considered it would be unnecessary to go to th@ expense of erecting stands for the children who would munber about 13.000 as they would probably be unable to obtain a view of their Majesties from such an elevation. Tenders had been obtained for the erection of thesix proposed stands on the line of route through the borough, and in the aggregate they amounted to £1,600. The committee decided to make the best accommodation they could for the children in another way, namely, by closing to the public the wide part of the road ' from Bruche Bridge to the Cemetery, 'which will be barricaded. An earth embankment will be made and the children will stand three or four deep—an arrangement which, under the circumstances, will doubtless prove much more convenient than the pro- vision of stands. Children will also be accom- modated in Howard-place, and the road alongside the wall belonging to the London and North-Western Railway Company on the Liverpool-road, and near St. Liike's Church will also be barricaded. It is thought that by the adoption of this scheme there will be less danger to the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>DECORATIONS,</head>
        <p>THE DECORATIONS,</p>
        <p>The Decorations Sub-committee met on Thursday under the presidency of the Mayor and three tenders were accepted, subject to the approval of tile Reception Committee, for the decoration of the whole of the route from Bruche Bridge to Sankey Bridges. There will be Venetian masts, and a profusion of streamers, baskets of flowers will be suspended from the electric standards, and gay festoons will heighten the effect. A triumphal arch will be erected at the entrance of the borough and an arch at the bottom of Butter- market-street (in Scotland-road) will take the form of a feudal gateway. A tbird arch will span the road at the borough boundary at Sankey Bridges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FIREWORKS</head>
        <p>THE FIREWORKS</p>
        <p>Arranninents are being made for a display of fire- works in Victoria Park on the evening of the date of the Royal visit.</p>
        <p>THE ADDRESS</p>
        <p>A loyal address will be presented, as arranged, but to save time, will not be read. HALF-HOLIDAY FOR SCHOOL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The School Management Conunittee of the Educa- tion Committee has resolved that in 'view of the visit of H.M. King Edward VII. to the Borough on Tuesday, the 6th July, 1909, the schools to be closed on the afternoon of that day. THE REV. T. MILLER AND THE EXPENSE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Miller, preaching at Padgate on Sun- day morning and Bold-street Wesleyan church on Sunday evening, on" The Joys of Freedom" dealt mainly with the experience of the Jews on leaving exile. • During the sermon the rev. gentleinan said that whilst he was perfectly loyal to the King, he had felt some misgivings at the expenr his Majesty's visit would entail, when he read the splendid report of the Medical Officer of Health. On several pages neces- sary reforms were put aside as impossible because of financial considerations. Personally the preacher said he would rather the money—which some calcu- lated at about .i:2,000--were spent upon improving the slums.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>KNOWSLEY REVIEW.</head>
        <p>THE KNOWSLEY REVIEW.</p>
        <p>Those who have had the pleasure of witnessing a big military review can testify to the spirit-stirring spectacle that such a function presents. Knowsley Park will undoubtedly witness a vast concourse of people on July 5 next, when His Majesty the King will review and present colours to the Terriers of West Lancashire. The review will commence at four prompt, and will occupy about an hour and a half, His Majesty passing twice in his carriage along the whol e line. This will be the first visit of His Majesty to Liverpool since the occasion of the laying of the foundation-stone of the Liverpool Cathedral, and he is certain to receive-a most enthusiastic welcome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TERRITORIAL ARRANGEMENTS,</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL ARRANGEMENTS,</p>
        <p>a Major-General Bethune, commanding the West Lancashire Division Territorial Forces, yesterday issued the final arrangements for the review of the division in Knowsley Park by his Majesty the King on July sth. Rill details regarding the order of parade, salute, presentation, of colours, march past, erc., are given, and it is further stated that guards of honour will be furnished by the following units during the visit of his Majesty to Knowsley : July 5,- Huyton station, on his Majesty's arrival.- sth Battalion South Lancashire Regiment. July 5, Knowsley Park, on his Majesty's arrival at the house.-7th Battalim the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. July 6, Knowsley Park, on his Majesty's departure for Manchester.-10th (Scottish) Battalion the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. July 6, Warrington, on his Majesty's arrival there.- 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment. \ July 6, Knowsley Park. on his 'Majesty's return from Manchester.-Bth (Irish) Battalion the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. July 7, Knowsley Park, on his. Majesty's departure for Liverpool.-6th (Rifle) Battalion the King's (Liver- pool) Regiment. July 7, Lime-street, on 'his Majesty's departure.— Outside station, 9th Battalion the King's (Liverpool) Regiment; inside station, sth Battalion the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. On July 5, the King's Colonials will keep the ground from Knowsley House to the review ground. The Naval Vounteers will form a guard of honour at the house when his Majesty leaves for the parade ground. •The Warrington Territorial Orders contain the following :—Royal Review and Presentation of Colours. The battalion will parade on Monday, July sth as strong as possible for Visit to ,Knowsley for the above purpose. It is 'the bounden duty of all officers, N.C.O's and men to be present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>RECEPTIONS EN ROUTE,</head>
        <p>RECEPTIONS EN ROUTE,</p>
        <p>Extensive arrangements are being made by the outside 'districts through which the King will pass before or after the review at Worsley. At Barton Bridge the Mayor and Town Council of Eccles, the members of the Education Committee, and the County and Borough Justices, with their womenfolk, will assemble, and the Mayor will be presented to the King. It is expected that the aqueduct will be turned on for inspection by the Royal party. The bridge will be suitably decorated. It is interesting to recall that when the King was eight years old he sailed from Patricroft Station to Worsley Hall in Lord Ellesmere's barge. The last' visit of the King to Worsley was twenty years ago, when, as Prince of Wales, he opened the Royal Agricultural Society's Show at Old Trafford. The school children of Eccles and Worsley are to be given a holiday. Scholars of the Barton Church Sct 00l and the the Godfrey Ermen Memorial Schools, with the congregation of St. Catherine's Chnrch, will meet in the Vicarage grounds at Barton and sing the National Anthem as the Royal procession appoaches. His Majesty is expected to arrive at Irlam boundary at 5.30 after the review.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Rostherne Ban to Continue.</head>
        <p>Rostherne Ban to Continue.</p>
        <p>"As I have no intention of changing my decision re refreshments in Rostherne village, it is no use my receiving any deputation. There is ample accommo- dation already quite close to the village." This is the answer of Lord Egerton to a request from the Manchester District Association of the C.T.C. for an interview with reference to the granting to C.T.C. members of facilities for obtaining refreshments at Rostherne. The club has in its published handbook the name of one place at Rostherne where members may obtain tea, and the object of the deputation was to secure a continuance of that or other suitable accommodation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>ST. ALBAN'S ROMAN CATHOLIC CHURCH IN COURSE OF ALTERATION.</head>
        <p>ST. ALBAN'S ROMAN CATHOLIC CHURCH IN COURSE OF ALTERATION.</p>
        <p>• St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, Bewsey-street, is hardly recognisable at present owing to extensive alterations to the front of the building. The building was never a handsome one, and the Church authoritie have embarked upon n useful work in trying to improve its external appearance. As shown in our photograph the facade has been taken out, and will be replaced by one of the later and lighter style, the old bricks being used in order to prevent any appearance of incongruity. A new and roomy porch and a baptistry will be added. • The large gallery inside thd church will be replaced by a smaller one at the west end, gufficient for the organ and choir. The architect for the work is Mr. C. Walker, of Newcastlo-on-Tyne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Clay—lnglis.</p>
        <p>A very pretty wedding took place on the 10th inst. at the Broomknoll U.F. Church, Ardrie, N. 8., when Mr George Edward Clay, A.R.1.8.A.' of Gravesend, Kent, second son of Mr. George Clay, Crosfield-street, Warrington, was married to Miss Jessie Craigie (Cissie) Inglis,eldest daughter of the into Bailie Inglis and Mrs. Inglis,of "Rosedale,"Airdrie, Lanarkshire,and " Craigholm," Burntisland, Fifeshire. The church; was beautifully decorated with palms, plants and flowers, the ceremony being performed in the presence of a large assemblage of guests and the general public by the Rev. William Reid of Broomknoll, Airdrie, assisted by the Rev. Alexander Weir of Dunbello, Coatbridge. The service was fully choral, the choir singing the hymns "The voice that breathed o'er.. Eden" and "Lead us, heavenly Father, lead us," whilst Mendelssohn's wedding march and the bridal march from " Lohengrin " were played by Mr. W. F. Harvie at the entrance and departure of the wedding party. The bride, who was attired in a handsome cream satin' gown with Court train,, trimmed with passemen- terie lace and lovers' knots, wore a wonderful hand-- embroidered veil and wreath of orange-blossoms, and carried a magnificent bouquet, the gift of the bride- groom. She was attended as bridesmaids by her. sisters, Nellie, Adelaide, and Margaret. who were gowned in white lace over pale heliotrope silk, made in the Directoire style, with champagne picture hats, trimmed with lilac and handsome white feathers, the gift of their late father. from South Africa. They wore pearl brooches and curried lovely bouquets of lilies of the valley, helio orchids, and lilac, the gifts of the bridegroom. The bride's youngest brother and sister, James and Caihiet acted as train-bearers. Mr. Walter Clay, brother of the bridegroom, discharged the dut4es of best man. The bride was conducted to ,the alter by her eldest brother, Mr. William Inglis. The presents to the bride were numerous and costly the family being held in high esteem. They included a handsome gold pearl pearl and amethyst bracelet suitably inscribed from friends in the Lochrin Iron- works, Coatbridge, in which her late father had been a partner; a handsome cheque from Sir William Arrol, and a massive silver kettle from the office staff of Messrs. Bridgland and Clay, Gravesend. Many congratulatory telegrams were received, and after the reception and garden party held at " Rosedale," the residence of the bride's mother, the happy couple left on their honeymoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Taylor— Lee.</head>
        <p>Taylor— Lee.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, was the scene of a pretty wedding which aroused great local interest on Thursday, when Mr. Hedley Hargreaves Taylor, L.D.S., only son of Mr. John Taylor, L.D.S., of Newport, Stockton Heath, was married to Miss Florence Lee, second daughter of Mr. T. Jackson Lee, Lyndale, Stockton Heath. The weather was ideal, the sun shining brilliantly, and the pretty church was well filled, while hundreds of 'onlookers assembled ill the vicinity. The chancel of the church was beauti- fully decorated with palms and choice flowers, and there were vases of lilies on the altar. The sisters and friends of the bride were responsible for the decorations. The officiating clergy were the Rev. J. Collins, M.A., Rev. H. A. Lester. M.A., Rev. H. V. Pigot, M.A., and Rev. W. Bracecamp. The service was choral and the hymn " How welcome was the call" was sung. Mr. F. Rose presided at the organ and played selections, including Mendels- sohn's "Wedding March." The bride, who was given away by her father, was charmingly attired in an Empire dress of ivory satin with ninon, chiffon,' and silver trimming, and tulle veil, surmounting wreath of orange blossom and white heather. She carried ylovely shower bouquet of lilies of the valley. The bridesmaids were Miss Bessie Lee ' (sister of the bride), Miss Nellie B. Taylor, and Miss Doris M. Taylor (sisters of the , bridegroom) who wore handsome Empire dresses of white eolienne trimmed with pale blue and pink embroidery, and white hats trimmed with pink rose buds and blue ribbon. Their bouqnets were composed of pink rose buds, tied with blue ribbon. Mr. H. Williams, L.D.S., Hendon, London, discharged the duties of best man, and the grownsmen were Mr. T. C. Stephen, L.D.S., Withington, Manchester, and Mr..T. Gibbons, L.D.S., Stockport. After the ceremony a reception was held at Lyndale, Whitefield-road, a large number of guests assembling. Later the happy couple departed for London and North Devon where the honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travelling dress as a vieux rose cloth costume and white crinoline hat, trimmed with vieux. rose ribbon and green leaves. Many handsome presents were received.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SCHOOLBOYS' COMPETITION. PRESENTATION OF WATCHES.</p>
        <p>The Silver-street schoolboys who were victorious in the final of the 'schoolboys' football competition received their watches on Wednesday afternoon, as an outcome of the splendid football ability they displayed last season. Mr. John Upson had gathered all his scholars in the large room at Silver-street schools, and as each boy received his prize from Alderman Tinnion (the president of the Warrington Football Club),he was enthusiastically applauded. Witnessing the distribution were :—Messrs.J. W. Hayward. E. J.Guest, Dr. Ferguson (managers), and the Rev. E. B. Keeling. Alderman Tinni9n spoke upon the object for whieh the com- petition bad been founded—to introduce the game of Rugby football in the schools. He also complimented the team upon the fine games they had played. The silver medals are to be presented to St. Benedict's scholars (the runners-up) next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-06-19_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Oldham F.C.'s Good Year.</head>
        <p>Oldham F.C.'s Good Year.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Oldham Rugby F.C. was held on Wednesday there being a record attendance of members. The annual report showed a profit of £1.064 Hs. 4d., and the Chairman (Dr. Gourlay), in moving its adoption, said last season had been the most successful since the formation of the club. The players had shown football of the highest standard throughout the season. During a period of twelve years the club's turnover had been increased to over £4,000, whilst last season the gate receipts had been £5OO in excess of any amount taken in the history of the club. Mr. J. Platt was elected president and treasurer, and in thanking the members, said the club was now one of the greatest in the Rugby world. He was glad that the Northern Union Committee had approved of the play- ing fields scheme, as it would be a benefit to 44e" Northern Union. - - • _ -</p>
        <p>Thepast season had been one of the most successful in the Union amongst all the clubs, and that had been brought about by the improved rules of the game,which were now as nearly perfect as it was possible for them</p>
        <p>to be made, and he did not anticipate any radical changes in the future. Twenty-nine persons had been moved for the ten positions on the committee, these including nine retir- ing committee men. Three old players—Arthur Lees, Sam Williams, and Tom Sellars—were amongst the aspirants for the committee, and the voting resulted in Arthur Lees heading the poll with 1,210 votes. The others elected were ;—Sam Williams (871), Tom Manor (831), Charles Hutchens (790) G. H. Rhodes (775), Hy. Langton (760), John Blunn (733), Harry Hilton (669), Frank Nield (620, and Tom Sellars (167). Two old committee men, R. H. Moore aud W. H. Greaves were not elected.</p>
      </div>
